\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Asian Reserves Up In Aug As Central Banks  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Roman   \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \par \uc2 763 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2010\par 05:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hit a new all-time high in August, according to Dow Jones Newswires calculations, driven by strong inflows attracted by the region's growth rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to contain local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.\par \par \uc2 Reserves held by 11 key Asian central banks, excluding China's, amounted to US$2.875 trillion at the end of August, up from US$2.861 trillion at the end of July, a 0.5% rise.\par \par \uc2 The increase came even as euro-denominated reserves lost value, with the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropping 2.8% against the U.S. dollar during August. That shows that countries across the region keep buying large amounts of dollars in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 While not included in the most recent figures, the central banks of South Korea, Taiwan, Singapore, Thailand and Indonesia bought hundreds of millions worth of dollars in the first week of September, local traders said, as their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  approached multi-month or multi-year highs.\par \par \uc2 This heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been sustained for over a year in most Asian economies, with the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also active in previous months.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the trend is likely to continue, especially in the Asian economies most exposed to flagging export demand from the developed world. That would include Taiwan and Singapore, which have seen some of the fastest economic growth in the world in recent quarters.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Asia's most active in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, saw reserves set fresh all-time highs in August for the 21st consecutive month.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  works," Faros Trading analysts wrote in a recent report. "We see a strong correlation to higher GDP growth and levels of physical \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is meant to increase export competitiveness amongst countries."\par \par \uc2 Citing International Monetary Fund numbers, Faros noted that Latin American economies such as Brazil's and Mexico's are also growing quickly, even as their reserves soar Asian-style. As of July, Brazil's reserves were up 21.4% on-year as the economy grew at a 9% clip, while Mexico's reserves were up a whopping 44.5% while the economy grew 7.6%.\par \par \uc2 "Unfortunately for G-10 countries, with no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and little change in their reserves, they are witnessing dismal present and future expectations for growth," Faros added.\par \par \uc2 Both Latin American and Asian markets have been fueled by inflows from investors seeking higher yields and better growth prospects, given ultra-low rates and mediocre economic growth in the U.S., Japan and European Union.\par \par \uc2 The latest data provided by fund tracker EPFR Global shows that in the week ended Sept. 1 emerging market bond and stock funds recorded net inflows for the fourteenth consecutive week, despite growing concern about a global slowdown.\par \par \uc2 Following are the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for Asia's 12 leading economies. All numbers are in billions of U.S. dollars.\par \par \uc2 The total number excludes China. The world's largest reserve holder, China releases reserves data only on a quarterly basis, and reported US$2.454 trillion in reserves at the end of June.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par             Aug 10       Aug 09       July 10     As Of \par TOTAL        2,875       2,625         2,861   end Aug Ex China \par China        2,454       2,130         2,454   end June \par Japan        1,070       1,042         1,063   end Aug \par India        282.8       276.4         284.2    27-Aug \par Indonesia     81.3        60.3          78.8   end Aug \par Korea        285.4       245.5           286   end Aug \par Malaysia      95.2        93.3            95    30-Aug \par Pakistan      16.1        14.3          16.5    27-Aug \par Philippines   49.6        41.3          48.6   end Aug \par Singapore    206.4       176.3         206.9   end Aug \par Taiwan       372.1       325.4         370.1   end Aug \par Thailand     154.7         127         151.5   27-Aug \par Hong Kong    261.4       223.3         260.7   end Aug \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (David Roman, a regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reporter, has been a Dow Jones correspondent since 2002, covering economic and corporate news. He can be reached at 65 6415 4045 or by e-mail: david.roman@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 08-09-10 0349GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4258 5095   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100908e6980009o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pic