\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/KRW Off; Suspected  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Buoys-Traders\b0\par\par\uc2 1,615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2010\par 03:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0114 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW starts Monday session lower, but fall limited by suspected BOK \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in favor of greenback to slow any rapid KRW gain, say traders. Pair last at 1,173 vs 1,175.1 late Friday in Seoul, but off day's low of 1,169.0. "Pressures to push the pair lower are higher now with global players increasing their exposure to risky assets again, but everyone's cautious of selling actively," with the BOK suspected of standing by to step in market if necessary, says local bank trader. Three traders say suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came through local bank, around 1,170 level with small amount just enough to smooth sharp move; pair likely to remain in 1,168-1,175 band rest of session. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] Model funds selling good amounts of EUR/USD from 1.2885 in thin market this morning, driving pair as low as 1.2871, says Singapore-based dealer. EUR/USD last 1.2880. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate with risks skewed to upside after hitting 4-session high $75.44/bbl on Friday, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart still positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode. Rise above $75.44 resistance would target $75.58 (August 30 reaction high), then $76.10 (Aug. 19 high), $76.50 (confluence of 55-day & 100-day moving averages), $76.63 (Aug. 17 high) and $77.50 (200-day moving average). Support at $73.20-$73.11 band (marked by Friday's low & Thursday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, exposing downside to $71.53 (Tuesday's reaction low), then $70.76 (11-week low when hit Aug. 25), psychological $70.00 and $69.51 (June 7 reaction low). October crude last down 48 cents at $74.12/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0104 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to fall vs 32.0180 Friday as better-than-expected U.S. jobs data soothes worries over pace of global economic recovery, which will fuel buying interest in Taiwan shares, increase demand for TWD, says local brokerage trader. Pair tipped in 31.9000-32.0500 band for session. "Exporters' orders to sell USD will pressure the pair. But Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again before the closing." \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been actively buying USD in market in recent months; traders believe \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aims to keep USD/TWD at around 32.000 so as not to hurt competitiveness of exports sector.(lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0103 GMT [Dow Jones] Winter crops including wheat in Eastern Australia helped by weekend rains, says \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  in daily note. Flooding rain hit north east Victoria over weekend but heaviest falls outside of major grain growing regions so for most part, rainfall beneficial to east coast grain crops, bank says. Local wheat futures untraded today, on Friday, most active ASX milling wheat deliverable January in NSW rose A$5 at A$292/metric ton; ASX milling wheat deliverable January in WA eased A$1 at A$312.50. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0055 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY may fall, after USD's decline against most rivals Friday; central parity tipped near 6.7920-6.7930 vs Friday's 6.7973. "Following the better-than-expected U.S. jobs report, investors have shifted into higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like euro, which also indicates to me that fixing will be lower," says Shanghai-based trader at foreign bank. Adds, on OTC, USD/CNY likely to trade in 6.7900-6.8000 band vs 6.8038 last close, as a lower fixing may fuel further dollar-selling. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.2% at 4550.2 in quiet trading due to long weekend in the U.S., Canada. Index hit 4-week high of 4566.3 in early trading, after S&P 500 rose 1.3% on stronger-than-expected U.S. jobs data. But gains have been hampered by ex-divs, worth about 10 points, as well as negative earnings guidance from \uc2 Macquarie Group\uc2  (MQG.AU), down 5.8%. Major banks propping up the market with gains of 0.3%-0.7%, while resources are relatively flat, with BHP (BHP.AU) down 0.2%, ex-div. Overall market is taking a slightly defensives bias, with consumer staples, healthcare and telcos outperforming. But traders are increasingly expecting S&P/ASX 200 rise as global markets price out double-dip U.S. recession and takeover activity picks up. "Equity markets won't have any direction from the U.S. market early this week but I think it would be broadly positive for sentiment if our market can hold steady over the next two days," says Jamie Spiteri, head of trading at Shaw Stockbroking. "Takeover activity in the materials sector in particular is certainly giving some attention to second-tier resources. I also believe that markets are starting to price out a double-dip U.S. recession." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely to drift lower following improvement in global risk appetite, weaker U.S. Treasurys, trader at local bank says; tips yields up 2-3 bps. "Investors are chasing riskier assets as the global environment has improved. That should see Singapore government bonds losing flavor, but the decline will be a bit modest as the bonds had fallen quite sharply on Friday." Singapore 10-year bond yield +9 bps to 2.13% Friday, 2-year up 1 bp to 0.45%. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi +0.5% at 1789.54 on rise in U.S. stocks, which helps boost investor sentiment across Asia, draws foreign interest in local shares. "Kospi's doing well in line with its Asian peers, but it's also true that 1800 still seems a strong resistance level for now with the U.S. market shut on Monday," says Meritz Securities analyst Shim Jae-youb. But adds Kospi may find some good reason to rise beyond 1800 level as U.S. President Obama expected to lay out new economic plan this week. Still, analysts say investors will also shy away from making big bets ahead of BOK's rate decision meeting, quadruple-witching day on Thursday (when stock index options and futures, stock options and futures all expire together). With stronger U.S. data easing global economic concerns, economy-sensitive techs, LED-related issues particularly strong. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) +1.3% at KRW771,000 while Hynix (000660.SE) +2.8% at KRW22,400, LG Display (034220.SE) +1.2% at KRW37,150; \uc2 Samsung Electro-Mechanics\uc2  (009150.SE) +1.2% at KRW124,500 while \uc2 LG Innotek\uc2  (011070.SE) also up 1.0% at KRW147,000. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0042 GMT [Dow Jones] IMM commitment of traders report shows net JPY long non-commercial positions vs USD fell to 49,904 contracts in week to Aug. 31 vs 51,069 contracts in week earlier, showing some investors reduced their JPY-long positions as BOJ decided last Monday that it would begin to offer Y10 trillion of six-month loans in addition to the three-month loans it had been offering since December. Teppei Ino, senior analyst at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ says, though, JPY-long positions staying around 50,000 for a few weeks suggests investors unwilling to pile up more JPY-long positions, but at the same time, they aren't sure when would be best time to square their JPY-long positions. Adds, trend-followers may keep their positions at current levels in coming weeks as he doesn't see major factor upcoming that would drastically changes economic fundamentals in terms of JPY.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0041 GMT [Dow Jones] Australians bet on a broad, and often quirky, number of things, with a well-known saying here that two flies crawling up a wall would draw a large crowd. Given that culture, should be no surprise that while public getting increasingly frustrated with still ongoing race for prime minister, it's been a gold-mine for punters. "About A$5 million has been wagered on this election (at Centrebet) with the biggest difference between this year and 2007, being that we took a million more in seat betting. Punters were much more educated in key seat betting and that was huge," said Neil Evans, spokesman for Centrebet. Betting even has something of a reputation for being a news predictor, with current lines showing Labor with lead. See =FOCUS: Australian Election Uncertainty Produces Betting Bonanza (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may rise, tracking gains in U.S. stocks Friday after August U.S. job data better than expected, renewing hopes for economic recovery. Shanghai Composite Index tipped to test resistance at 2700, after ending flat at 2655.39. "No industry index rose more than 2% last session, which indicates that there's not yet a catalyst to help the index make a leap forward," says Wei Daoke at Shenyin Wanguo Securities. Insurers likely up after China's insurance regulator yesterday says it will allow insurers to invest in shares issued by unlisted companies, real estate. Shenzhen index +0.5% at 1178.26. (jean.yung@dowjones.com) [ 09-05-10 2114ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100906e69600011\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\