\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Tad Higher Late After Rising To Record Overnight\b0\par\par\uc2 367 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2010\par 09:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                          Latest   Change \par    USD/SGD               1.3470   -0.0012 \par    Overnight rate        0.13%    -6 bps \par    Two-year bond yield:  0.44%    Unchanged \par    10-year bond yield:   2.13%    +9 bps \par    Two-year swap offer:  0.91%    +1 bp \par    10-year swap offer:   2.39%    +6 bps \par    2-10-yr swap curve:   148 bps  +5 bps \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was a tad higher against the U.S. dollar late in Asian trade Friday after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the island nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lifted the greenback from a record low.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar had fallen as low as S$1.3425 in overnight trade Thursday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore bought U.S. dollars around S$1.3435 Friday, a person familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar has gained this week after a string of positive data from the U.S. and China helped ease fears of a double-dip global recession and brought investors back into riskier assets such as \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  linked to growth and stocks.\par \par \uc2 The MAS expects "continued weakness in the U.S. dollar. The basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the Singapore dollar trades with will likely go higher in the short term," the person said. "If that happens the local dollar will further rise and the MAS' role will not be to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  but to make sure that the rise is orderly."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  uses the exchange rate rather than interest rates as its main policy tool because trade flows dwarf the island's small domestic economy.\par \par \uc2 "Risk appetite has improved globally and the bias is for the Singapore dollar to rise. The next cues will come from the U.S. non-farm payrolls data due tonight," an analyst at an international bank said, predicting the U.S. dollar to find support near S$1.3440 and resistance near S$1.3500 in the overnight session.\par \par \uc2 Longer-dated Singapore government bonds continued to slide with investors chasing risk.\par \par \uc2 -By Gaurav Raghuvanshi; Dow Jones Newswires; +65 64154 154; gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com [ 03-09-10 0728GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1163 1859   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100903e693000g6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
