s improving; also believes "the worst of the policy tightening environment may be behind us." Ping An best blue-chip gainer, +4.2% at HK$68.90 on removal of overhang; Esprit (0330.HK) slumps 5.1% to HK$40.70 on further post-results weakness. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0647 GMT [Dow Jones] Investor apathy in Singapore persists, with STI flat at 2987.14, still locked in morning band of 2977-2998. This week's lowest close of 2950 set Tuesday likely to provide support if index heads any lower late. "This region, around 3000, is an inflection point," says Phillip Securities. Notes any significant pullback from this level could eventually take index down to last month's 2910 low, while continuation of gains from earlier this week could enable new year-to-date high (currently at 3043); "it remains a wait-and-see at this point." Overall market volume remains thin, well under half of yesterday's 1.72 billion shares worth S$1.63 billion, with number of decliners on par with gainers. Genting Hong Kong (S21.SG) still most sought-after stock, +25.0% at US$0.375, accounting for over a-quarter of total volume, as players switch from recent market favorite Genting Singapore (G13.SG) to sister company; Genting Singapore flat at S$1.76. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0646 GMT [Dow Jones] India's food articles remain stubbornly high despite hopes of easing, bolstering view that prices may remain high for a while, says Indranil Pan, chief economist at \uc2 Kotak Mahindra Bank\uc2 . Notes, non-food articles such as minerals increasing on secular basis, proving any moderation in inflation owes more to base effect than easing of underlying inflationary pressures; expects absolute price index levels to remain at elevated levels. Expects August inflation print to be in line with July's provisional 9.97%, but "we may need to factor in incremental information from the weekly price data now." (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end +1.4% at 7830.21 on gains in tech stocks. Near-term upside probably limited as most good news might have been priced in during recent rally, says Taiwan International Securities trader Rita Li; tips 7750-7860 range for next week; "We expect things to consolidate next week." Apple product launches, local 'back to school' marketing scheme spurs tech names on increase in anticipated demand. Hon Hai (2317.TW) +0.9% at NT$111.50 recouping losses after 10.2% drop following poor 1H results this week. Acer (2353.TW) +3.3% at NT$78.90, \uc2 Chimei Innolux\uc2  (3481.TW) +6.9% at NT$35.75, \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) +4.9% at NT$29.20. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0640 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are little changed Friday, with the market quiet ahead of U.S. non-farm payrolls due at 1230 GMT. Bunds fell Thursday, extending the slide from Wednesday after more encouraging data helped ease worries over a marked slowdown in global growth. Investors will thus look for more signs from the payrolls number. "If the job loss is greater than expected, the figure could help cap the rebound in yields ahead of the long U.S. weekend," says Credit Agricole. Euro zone services PMI are due 0800 GMT and retail sales at 0900 GMT. September bunds are up 0.03 at 132.75 and the 10-year bund is up at 99.80 to yield 2.273%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0633 GMT [Dow Jones] Oil lower as U.S. dollar strengthens against yen, worries persist about U.S. macroeconomic situation, says \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  energy economist Ben Westmore; market players awaiting U.S. jobs data due later today to provide more direction, he adds. Dow Jones poll of economists forecasts data to show loss of 110,000 jobs in August from July, when payrolls fell by 131,000. Also, impact from Hurricane Earl seems more on bearish side as it heads for East Coast instead of oil-producing Gulf, could disrupt travel plans and hurt gasoline demand during Labor Day long weekend, says Westmore. October Nymex crude down 38 cents at $74.64/bbl. (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0630 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB tipped to slide to 30.80 at end-2010 as capital inflows persist, tipped to fall to 29.70 at end-2011, says Daiwa Securities; THB has appreciated 6.6% vs USD so far this year (including 3% alone in August), faster than other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  except MYR. USD/THB recently trading at 31.13. Daiwa says with Thai economy still strong, inflation still more than 3%, should prompt \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  to lift benchmark policy rate further 25 bps this year to 2.0%. Headline inflation at 3.3% in August on year, vs 3.4% in July from year earlier; core inflation at 1.2% on year remains within BOT's target range of 0.5% to 3.0%. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0630 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds off highs as food, fuel prices rise, raising fresh concerns on surging prices, potentially sharper RBI trajectory needed to rein in inflation, says trader with foreign bank; 7.80% 2020 bond at 98.83 vs 98.92 before data, 98.80 last close. Data may spoil moderately positive sentiment ahead of INR120 billion government bond auction later in session, risk affecting cutoff yields at auction. WPI for primary articles in India +1.4% to 312.5 in week to Aug. 21 from previous week's print. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0629 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly higher; Nikkei +0.6%, S&P/ASX 200 +0.2%, Kospi +0.2%, HSI +0.1%, Sensex +0.1%, Shanghai Composite down 0.4%, STI down 0.2%, NZX-50 +0.8%, Taiex +1.4%, Indonesian shares 0.8% higher. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, USD slightly higher vs JPY, supported by gains in Japanese stocks, though major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  largely rangebound as traders wait for U.S. non-farm payrolls data due at 1230 GMT. USD/JPY at 84.27 vs 84.21 late Thursday in New York, EUR/USD at 1.2829 vs 1.2821, EUR/JPY at 108.07 vs 107.96. Japan would find it hard to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if JPY's rise gradual, but \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities more determined than ever to act against any unusually rapid exchange-rate moves, people familiar with matter tell Dow Jones. \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  as USD/SGD hits record low, buying USD at 1.3435, person familiar with matter tells Dow Jones; USD/SGD last at 1.3460 after touching 1.3425 overnight (previous record low 1.3438 on July 15, 2008). Korea's revised, seasonally-adjusted 2Q GDP +1.4% on-quarter, slightly lower than initial estimate in July of 1.5% growth, adding weight to view any further monetary tightening likely to be limited and gradual. Korea's robust growth momentum will likely lose steam for rest of year amid slowdown in recovery of global economy, says BOK official. Seasonally adjusted Australian Industry Group/Commonwealth Bank Australian Performance of Services Index rose slightly to 47.5 in August, remaining below critical 50 point level for fourth consecutive month. Hong Kong's property transactions in August totaled 16,946, +27.9% vs year earlier, +13.8% vs July. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Hong Kong Purchasing Managers Index rose to 52.3 in August from 51.3 in July. Spot gold at $1,250.20/oz, down $1.10 from late New York trade Monday. October Nymex crude oil futures down 27 cents at $74.75/bbl. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0627 GMT [Dow Jones] September bunds are unchanged at 132.72 Friday, having fallen over 2 points in the last 2 sessions. A break here would open a move to 133.20 ahead of further resistance at 133.43 and 133.66, says Paul Day at Market Securities. Support under Thursday's 132.44 low remains at the retracement points at 131.94 and 130.20. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0624 GMT [Dow Jones] September bunds are unchanged at 132.72 Friday, having fallen over 2 points in the last 2 sessions. A break here would open a move to 133.20 ahead of further resistance at 133.43 and 133.66, says Paul Day at Market Securities. Support under Thursday's 132.44 low remains at the retracement points at 131.94 and 130.20. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com) [ 09-03-10 0250ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100903e6930004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
