0.20. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0624 GMT [Dow Jones] September bunds are unchanged at 132.72 Friday, having fallen over 2 points in the last 2 sessions. A break here would open a move to 133.20 ahead of further resistance at 133.43 and 133.66, says Paul Day at Market Securities. Support under Thursday's 132.44 low remains at the retracement points at 131.94 and 130.20. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0623 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bond auction later in session likely to meet strong demand from participants, according to Dow Jones Newswires poll of 5 traders. Cutoff yield for 7.46% 2017 bond forecast at 8.02%, according to median estimate in poll, while 8.08% 2022 bond cutoff yield tipped at 8.05% in poll. Cutoff yield for 8.30% 2040 bond forecast as likely at 8.41% at 8.41% in poll. "Yields may have topped out for now, and we're expecting the market to come in and buy aggressively at the auction," says trader with state-run bank. But some caution on 2017 note as paper relatively illiquid, any surprise would likely come on this bond. RBI to sell INR120 billion of government bonds later in session. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0619 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia's policy tightening pause unlikely to last long, as rates may have to rise again to contain rising inflation, says Capital Economics. Bank Negara kept benchmark OPR steady at 2.75% Thursday. House expects 25bp rise to 3.0% at next policy meet in November; "our view is that the local economic upswing will comfortably survive the tough global conditions." Says inflation may climb to just over 2% in 2010, accelerate to about 2.5% in 2011; reckons Bank Negara may lift rates further "towards the middle of the 3%-4% neutral range by mid-2011." Adds, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities unlikely concerned about rising MYR as long as CNY strengthens vs USD. Tips USD/MYR at 3.1000 by year-end vs last at 3.1230. Expects Malaysia GDP growth at 7.5% for 