\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: KLCI Down 0.3%; Lower Liners Up; 1430-1450 Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2010\par 03:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0142 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] KLCI down 0.3% at 1437.32 but market breadth positive with advancers beating decliners nearly 2-to-1. Blue chips under profit-taking pressure after recent rally; investors switch into laggards, small caps. "With blue chips looking increasing overbought, investors are now doing some bargain-hunting on laggard second liners," dealer says. Still, longer-term outlook for KLCI remains positive, reckons pullback in blue chips likely temporary; support pegged at 1430, with resistance at 1450. Among gainers, Hai-O (7668.KU) +7.6% at MYR3.26, MRCB (1651.KU) +4.0% at MYR1.83, MyEG (0138.KU) +6.2% at MYR0.775; among decliners, Genting (3182.KU), among the best performing blue chips in August (+18%), down 1.9% at MYR9.31. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/IDR steady at 9,008 as market participants await outcome of \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 's monthly-rate setting meeting later today, also U.S. payrolls data, dealers say. "It's been very quiet so far," dealer says; \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  widely expected to keep its policy rate unchanged at 6.5% after August inflation came in milder than expected. Pair tipped in 9,000-9,015 range. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/HKD down on corporate demand (for local unit), says chief trader at local bank. Tips pair in 7.7700- 7.7780 band in near term. Still, "the pair is likely to rise (later) today on bargain-hunting after having touched (near) HK$7.7700 overnight in New York trade," trader says. Pair last at 7.7738 vs 7.7748 yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market after SGD rises to record high vs USD, source familiar with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking says. MAS "went in to smooth the U.S. dollar's fall around S$1.3435," source says. Adds, MAS "will continue to smooth the U.S. dollar's fall for now, but this is a regional issue with widespread weakness for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  follows USD/SGD touching 1.3425 overnight (previous record low 1.3438 on July 15, 2008). Pair last 1.3465. (djn.bureau.singapore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Philippine shares likely to open lower on profit-taking following benchmark PSEi's 2% rise yesterday to 3666.54; market participants also expected to play safe ahead of long weekend in U.S. "But the decline will be limited, due mostly to improved liquidity...our recommendation is to trade within range," says Grace Cerdenia, 2TradeAsia.com research head. Adds profit taking may focus on financial, mining, property names that advanced substantially in recent sessions. Support tipped at 3640, resistance 3680. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0125 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Revision to Japan April-June GDP, due next Friday, may show economy grew at annualized 1.5% clip in quarter, much better than 0.4% rise in government's initial estimate, as MOF survey shows capital expenditure in better shape than expected, says Masamichi Adachi, economist at \uc2 J.P.Morgan\uc2  . Says market consensus had been for 6.5% on-year fall in capex in quarter, but survey showed it was down only 1.7%. Says this much more in line with recovery in Japanese corporate sector, which points towards capex recovery in recent months. "Other data such as industrial production figures as well as anecdotal reports suggest capex is rising, and the previous estimates were absolutely too low compared to what is happening with corporate profits and business sentiment."(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] HSI starts September on sound note (2 days of gains), likely to rise more, with 22,000 as target this month, says \uc2 KGI Asia\uc2  's Ben Kwong; cites favorable factors including expectations of more U.S. stimulus measures, likely renewed pressure on CNY to appreciate, IPO activities again picking up in HK, quarter-end window dressing. Overall, says "the correction since HSI last peaked on August 9 (at 21,801) should be over." Ping An (2318.HK) set to open lower on Newbridge Asia's stake disposal; Esprit (0330.HK) may face more post-results selling pressure, but \uc2 Sino Land\uc2  (0083.HK) likely to rise a tad on above-view FY results. HSI +1.2% at 20,868.92 yesterday, likely to extend gains today to test 21,000. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0119 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Singapore stocks firmer, mirroring modest gains in other Asian bourses after Wall Street's overnight gains, with STI +0.3% at 2996.02. Resistance for benchmark expected at yesterday's 4-week intraday high of 3013. "We are betting on the STI to make a bolder attempt to scale the 3000 mark. Technical indicators look healthy with momentum still climbing," says CIMB. Market breadth at 2 gainers for every decliner. Small caps most active, including \uc2 Genting Hong Kong\uc2  (S21.SG), +3.3% at US$0.315, \uc2 United Fiber System\uc2  (P30.SG), flat at S$0.05, Federal International (F20.SG), flat at S$0.15. Among STI components, notable gainers include \uc2 CapitaMalls Asia (JS8.SG)\uc2 , +1.4% at S$2.20, Jardine Strategic (J37.SG), +1.2% at US$24.88, NOL (N03.SG), +1.0% at S$1.97. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0119 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/THB lower at 31.13 vs 31.15 late yesterday as capital inflows continue. Court late yesterday ruled 74 of 76 halted projects at Map Ta Phut can go ahead; dealers say this may boost local stocks, THB. "The dollar/baht will likely test THB31.10 support, which is the lowest level hit since April 2008, as fund inflows continue" says Bangkok-based dealer. Foreigners net buyers of THB9.38 billion worth of Thai shares in past 5 sessions. Investors keeping eyes on U.S. employment report for August due later today. Support tipped at 31.05, resistance at 31.20. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0113 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Strong USD/JPY bids from Japanese corporate customers stand at 84.00 which also low touched on EBS in late Asian trading yesterday, says Singapore-based dealer; adds strong selling interest at 84.60/70; but if 84.70 breaks, stops-driven buying may lift pair to 85.00. USD/JPY last 84.33. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] (An item published at 0056 GMT misstated the name of the analyst. The correct version follows:) Korea treasury bonds, bond futures lower, tracking overnight losses in U.S. Treasurys. Daewoo Securities analyst Yoon Il-kwang expects yield curve to flatten until next week's BOK monetary policy meeting as rate hike expectations likely to continue weighing on shorter-end bonds, especially 1-year paper. Says, yield spreads between 1-year monetary stabilization bond and 5-year treasury note as well as spread between 1-year MSB, 10-year note may narrow, in particular. Adds, "even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps the key policy rate on hold, shorter-end bonds aren't likely to perform better as chance of a rate hike some time later in the year would still be there." 3-year yield quoted +3 bps at 3.63%, 5-year yield +2 bps at 4.04%, September futures down 8 ticks at 112.01 (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.1% at 4537.2, fades to 4535 on profit taking before U.S. jobs data, Labor Day, after early rise to 4558.7 following 0.9% rise in S&P 500 on better-than-expected U.S. economic data. Materials, industrials and consumer staples underpinning the market, with BHP (BHP.AU) up 0.5%, Rio (RIO.AU) up 0.6%, MAp (MAP.AU) up 1.6%, \uc2 Wesfarmers\uc2  (WES.AU) up 0.9%. But financials weighing on the market, with major banks down 0.5%-1.3%. Traders aren't ruling out a negative close before the weekend. But there's a growing view that the market is posed to break major resistance near 4622. "It's been a strong week, ahead of U.S. payrolls data and the U.S. long weekend," says IG Markets Strategist Ben Potter. "Combine that with the fact that September is historically a weak month and you could see people take some money off the table. But global markets have priced in a double-dip recession and August was a particularly bad month, so September might not be as bad as expected. I think people are too pessimistic and evidence is pointing toward a soft patch in the U.S. recovery, rather than a double dip." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com) [ 09-02-10 2142ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100903e69300017\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
