how economy grew at annualized 1.5% clip in quarter, much better than 0.4% rise in government's initial estimate, as MOF survey shows capital expenditure in better shape than expected, says Masamichi Adachi, economist at \uc2 J.P.Morgan\uc2 . Says market consensus had been for 6.5% on-year fall in capex in quarter, but survey showed it was down only 1.7%. Says this much more in line with recovery in Japanese corporate sector, which points towards capex recovery in recent months. "Other data such as industrial production figures as well as anecdotal reports suggest capex is rising, and the previous estimates were absolutely too low compared to what is happening with corporate profits and business sentiment."(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI starts September on sound note (2 days of gains), likely to rise more, with 22,000 as target this month, says \uc2 KGI Asia\uc2 's Ben Kwong; cites favorable factors including expectations of more U.S. stimulus measures, likely renewed pressure on CNY to appreciate, IPO activities again picking up in HK, quarter-end window dressing. Overall, says "the correction since HSI last peaked on August 9 (at 21,801) should be over." Ping An (2318.HK) set to open lower on Newbridge Asia's stake disposal; Esprit (0330.HK) may face more post-results selling pressure, but \uc2 Sino Land\uc2  (0083.HK) likely to rise a tad on above-view FY results. HSI +1.2% at 20,868.92 yesterday, likely to extend gains today to test 21,000. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0119 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks firmer, mirroring modest gains in other Asian bourses after Wall Street's overnight gains, with STI +0.3% at 2996.02. Resistance for benchmark expected at yesterday's 4-week intraday high of 3013. "We are betting on the STI to make a bolder attempt to scale the 3000 mark. Technical indicators look healthy with momentum still climbing," says CIMB. Market breadth at 2 gainers for every decliner. Small caps most act