2\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps the key policy rate on hold, shorter-end bonds aren't likely to perform better as chance of a rate hike some time later in the year would still be there." 3-year yield quoted +3 bps at 3.63%, 5-year yield +2 bps at 4.04%, September futures down 8 ticks at 112.01 (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.1% at 4537.2, fades to 4535 on profit taking before U.S. jobs data, Labor Day, after early rise to 4558.7 following 0.9% rise in S&P 500 on better-than-expected U.S. economic data. Materials, industrials and consumer staples underpinning the market, with BHP (BHP.AU) up 0.5%, Rio (RIO.AU) up 0.6%, MAp (MAP.AU) up 1.6%, \uc2 Wesfarmers\uc2  (WES.AU) up 0.9%. But financials weighing on the market, with major banks down 0.5%-1.3%. Traders aren't ruling out a negative close before the weekend. But there's a growing view that the market is posed to break major resistance near 4622. "It's been a strong week, ahead of U.S. payrolls data and the U.S. long weekend," says IG Markets Strategist Ben Potter. "Combine that with the fact that September is historically a weak month and you could see people take some money off the table. But global markets have priced in a double-dip recession and August was a particularly bad month, so September might not be as bad as expected. I think people are too pessimistic and evidence is pointing toward a soft patch in the U.S. recovery, rather than a double dip." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures lower, tracking overnight losses in U.S. Treasurys. \uc2 Daewoo Securities\uc2  analyst Yoon Yeo-sam expects yield curve to flatten until next week's BOK monetary policy meeting as rate hike expectations likely to continue weighing on shorter-end bonds, especially 1-year paper. Says, yield spreads between 1-year monetary stabilization bond and 5-year treasury note as well as spread between 1-year MSB, 10-year note may narrow, in particular. Adds, "even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps the key policy rate on hold, shorter-end bonds aren't likely to perform better as chance of a rate hike some time later in the year would still be there." 3-year yield quoted +3 bps at 3.63%, 5-year yield +2 bps at 4.04%, September futures down 8 ticks at 112.01. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to gain modestly, extending yesterday's 0.7% rise to 7720.82. Taiex to get boost from stronger U.S. stocks but investors to remain sidelined ahead of U.S. nonfarm payrolls report due later in global day. "Yesterday's rebound for Hon Hai and new Apple product launches bode well for tech stocks today," says KGI Securities trader Randy Chang. Tips 7700-7800 range, expects airline shares to be strong on anticipated healthy August ticket sales. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0042 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may rise, with gains in U.S. stocks overnight adding to already positive investor sentiment following recent upbeat corporate earnings reporting season. KLCI ends +0.6% at 1441.07 yesterday, highest close in 31 months; key index has closed higher in 13 of last 14 session as improved risk appetite, slew of above-view earnings from key companies spurred renewed foreign investor interest; \uc2 Bursa Malaysia\uc2  data show foreign investor participation at about 40% in recent session vs average 20%-25%. "The KLCI looks set to test the 1450 resistance today," dealer says; tips blue chips such as Tenaga (5347.KU), Genting (3182.KU) to continue to lead gains. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com) [ 09-02-10 2130ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100903e69300014\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
