\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Rises To Highest Level Ever Vs US Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 144 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2010\par 01:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose to its highest level ever overnight, after global economic fears eased and investors returned to assets linked to growth such as equities and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.3434 in early Asian trade after falling as low as S$1.3425 overnight. The previous record intraday low for the greenback was S$1.3438, touched on July 15, 2008.\par \par \uc2 A source close to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Thursday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is poised to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  climbs back to around its record high.\par \par \uc2 Officials of the MAS weren't immediately available for comment.\par \par \uc2 -By Gaurav Raghuvanshi, Dow Jones Newswires; +65 64154 154; gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com [ 02-09-10 2325GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100902e692001bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
