\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: JGBs Likely To Fall If Tokyo  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  - Analyst\b0\par\par\uc2 1,581 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2010\par 02:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs likely to face downward pressure if Tokyo decides to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to push down JPY, because action would reverse recent downtrend in USD/JPY, Tokyo shares, and because that may increase oversupply concerns as government will need to issue more discount bills to fund any foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities strategist Katsutoshi Inadome; brokerage expects government to directly step into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, only if a USD/JPY breach below 80 becomes possible (pair last around 84.52). Lead September JGB futures currently +0.24 at 142.69. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.7% at 4421.7 after hitting 4413.7 amid broad-based retreat following 1.5% decline in the S&P 500, as U.S. economic data continued to disappoint. Australian equities market is showing signs of nervousness, with cyclicals generally weak and defensives outperforming. Banks are down 0.4%-1.6% and Macquarie (MQG.AU) is down 1.2%, while in resources, BHP (BHP.AU) is down 1.6% and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) is down 1.4%. Among defensives \uc2 Wesfarmers\uc2  (WES.AU) up 0.5%, CSL (CSL.AU) up 0.5% and Foster's (FGL.AU) up 0.5%. But S&P/ASX 200 looks to be finding support below the 50-day moving average at 4427 and may regain this level if domestic funds support the market before month end. "Our market looks pretty good," says \uc2 UBS\uc2  Head of Sales George Kanaan. "The reporting season was reasonably good. It's just this volatile U.S. market that is pushing us around but it was very thin overnight. I don't expect Asian markets to go too badly today." Expects optimism to return if Wednesday's China PMI and U.S. ISM data show signs of improvement. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi off 0.6% at 1749.09, weighed by continued worries about global economic growth that pushed U.S. markets lower. \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2  analyst Bae Sung-young says market likely supported around 1742 levels for day, to trade in 1710-1770 band for week; "The Bernanke factor wore off in the U.S. after a day, and generally the economic data coming out this week, including U.S. August non-farm payrolls report and ISM manufacturing report, won't look very good. Unless there are definitive new measures to support growth the market's going to remain weak." Notes banks, which posted sharp gains Monday, and tech plays considered vulnerable to weaker 2H10 global growth. KB Financial (105560.SE) off 1.9% at KRW48,550, \uc2 Woori Finance\uc2  (053000.SE) 1.8% lower at KRW13,500. Hynix (000660.SE) off 2.9% at KRW21,800. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0042 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW rising towards psychological 1,200 in early trade as concerns over pace of global economic recovery continue to weigh on risk-sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like KRW, EUR, traders say. If pair rises to as high as that level, "it would be the fourth time (this month) that exporters deal with KRW1,200 level," says local bank trader. Adds, it's going to be interesting to see whether pair could test 1,200 resistance successfully today but this unlikely scenario if exporters sell as actively as they had been doing in previous occasions. Tips pair in 1,193-1,206 range for rest of session. Players also keeping close eye on USD/JPY as stronger JPY tends to push JPY/KRW higher. USD/KRW last at 1,197.7 vs 1,192.0 late yesterday in Seoul amid Kospi's 0.6% loss. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0042 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to rise in 32.050-32.100 band vs 32.060 yesterday, boosted by expected losses in local equities, gains in USD/KRW, but gains will likely be limited due to exporters' month-end TWD settlement demand, says local bank trader. Adds, "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may lift the pair higher in late session" to help island's exporters; would be continuation of suspected pattern of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over most of month. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares set to consolidate but biased down following Wall Street's fall overnight amid ongoing worries about sustainability of global economic recovery. Shanghai Composite Index support tipped at 2600 after ending +1.6% at 2652.66 yesterday. "The banks' recent string of fundraising efforts put pressure on the (banking) sector, and since the sector has a big weighting, its weakness will hurt the broader market," says Qian Qimin at Shenyin Wanguo Securities. Xinjiang-based companies may be in spotlight today after China's top securities regulator Shang Fulin reiterated Monday that Beijing will continue to support companies in region to raise capital. Shenzhen index +2.6% at 1158.22. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] Home sale transactions in Singapore likely to fall 30% over next 3-6 months, with some moderation in prices for mass-market projects, following latest government measures to rein in housing market, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . Cites doubling of minimum cash payment to 10%, which applies to home owners with outstanding housing loans seeking another property, as probably strongest deterrant. Says restrictions on public housing flats - such as increasing minimum occupation period before owners can sell, requiring private home owners buying resale public housing flats to sell private home - will indirectly affect demand from upgraders for mass-market private housing; "we see the measures casting a shadow on near-term property sales." Rates \uc2 Keppel Land\uc2  (K17.SG), UOL (U14.SG), Allgreen (A16.SG), \uc2 Wing Tai\uc2  (W05.SG) at Neutral with respective targets of S$3.92, S$4.18, S$1.33, S$1.93. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0035 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 2.0% at 8963.36 after BOJ's new easing measures fail to curb yen's rise; sentiment also hit by drops in U.S. stocks overnight on worries about U.S. economy; USD/JPY trading below 85, hurting exporters. "Japan is giving investors the impression that it's running out of options to counter market weakness," says Kenichi Hirano, strategist at Tachibana Securities. Adds Nikkei likely to be supported at 8850 today; if USD/JPY (now 84.55) trades below 84.50, losses may widen. \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  (8035.TO) off 4.2% at Y4,005, Advantest (6857.TO) off 3.0% at Y1,633, Canon (7751.TO) off 2.9% at Y3,480. ANA (9202.TO) bucking weakness, up 0.6% at Y310 on Deutsche Securities' rating hike to Buy. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0035 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bond yields likely down 2-3 bps tracking overnight moves in U.S. Treasurys after global risk appetite wanes. "The positive sentiment lasted just a day and a second read of the U.S. Fed and Bank of Japan statements has only reinforced fears of deepening trouble for the world economy. The flight to safety theme is back," trader at international bank says. Adds bonds at longer end likely to rise more today. Yesterday, 2-year Singapore government bond yield down 1 bp at 0.46%, 10-year also down 1 bp at 2.06%. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0034 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand residential building consents excluding apartments, down 5.3% on month in July, weaker than forecast, says ANZ economist Mark Smith; "we expect that residential investment activity will recover, but for this to be pushed back into 2011." Adds, had thought October 1 increase in GST to 15% from 12.5% would encourage some residential work to be brought forward, but "there is very little evidence of this in the consent data so far." Says higher costs of purchasing new dwelling, large supply of existing dwellings on market, ongoing household develeraging, low income growth, moderating trend for net immigration are playing more influential role in constraining residential building activity than previously thought. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0032 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD higher in Asia after overnight data show U.S. personal incomes rising at slower rate than expected, yesterday's BOJ move to stem JPY rise falls flat. "The markets are once again worried about the faltering global economic recovery, which is fueling risk aversion," says trader at local bank. Expects resistance at 1.3630, support 1.3510. Pair last 1.3561. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0028 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to fall in 7650-7700 band, after edging up 0.2% to 7741.20 yesterday, weighed by losses in Asian, U.S. shares, says KGI Securities trader Randy Chang. Adds losses likely led by tech stocks after Hon Hai's (2317.TW) disappointing 1H results, Intel's warning of weak sales in current quarter. Still, "The downside for Taiex is limited as insurers and government funds may buy around 7650," he says. Hon Hai says Monday its 1H net profit +22% on-year, but based on its 1Q net profit of NT$17.99 billion, its 2Q net profit down 6.9% on-quarter at NT$16.75 billion. Company didn't disclose its 2Q net profit.(mason.wu@dowjones.com) [ 08-30-10 2057ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100831e68v0000p\par }\page {\par\fs2