\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:MAS Won't  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  On SGD At Current Level-Source\b0\par\par\uc2 1,492 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2010\par 08:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0614 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore MAS comfortable with recent rise in local dollar vs U.S. dollar, won't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  near current USD/SGD levels, source familiar with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  thinking says. Also, MAS likely to stand pat at next policy review in October. "The rise hasn't been dramatic and at current levels the Singapore dollar is competitive for our exports," source says, adds, MAS likely won't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unless USD/SGD falls "clearly and sustainably closer to S$1.34." Pair last 1.3629 vs 1.3625 Friday close. (costas.paris@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0609 GMT [Dow Jones] Rallying Aussie bonds defying fundamental analysis, RBA still in hiking bias, says Annette Beacher, senior strategist with \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 . "Everyone is just standing back at the moment even as the levels for 3s and 10s is just too low. And there really isn't much until GDP early next month, which might be the chance for the market to get back in," says Beacher. Aussie 3-year up 5 ticks at 95.44, 10-year up 7 ticks at 95.025. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0605 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end higher, led by gains in tourism, airlines, textile firms; Taiex +0.6% at 7941.22; may test 8000 tomorrow; "Taiwan shares are relatively strong compared with Japan and Korea partly because of strength in China shares," says Andrew Teng, assistant vice president at Taiwan International Securities; adds, relatively lackluster performance of index-heavyweights, such as semiconductor makers, financial firms, as well as lower trading volume (NT$119.43 billion vs NT$131.45 billion Friday) indicates sentiment not very strong. Tourism, airlines rise on hopes of more Chinese tourists visiting island. Formosa International Hotels (2707.TW) +7% by daily trading limit at NT$506. \uc2 EVA Airways\uc2  also +7% at NT$24.55. Textile strong on hopes for strong demand in 3Q. Carnival Industrial (1417.TW) +7% at NT$12.30. Taiwan shares tipped to consolidate in 7900-8050 range near term. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0559 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi off 0.1% at 1743.91, erasing most of its earlier losses, tracking strong Chinese stocks on top of eased program selling pressure due to foreign, domestic institutional buying in futures, analysts say. "Amid a lack of fresh momentum recently, investor sentiment and supply-demand conditions are having a greater impact on the market," says NH Investment & Securities' Kim Hyoung-ryoul; tips Kospi in 1720-1760 range at least until U.S. economic data later in week, such as leading indicators, Philadelphia Fed business survey Thursday. Some tech shares recovering from earlier drops, with Hynix (000660.SE) +1.9% at KRW22,020 while \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE) +1.0 at KRW103,500, but financial stocks mostly down on strong program selling, analysts say. KB Financial (105560.SE) down 1.7% at KRW47,900 while \uc2 Woori Finance\uc2  (053000.SE) down 2.5% at KRW13,550. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0552 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield falls to fresh 7-year low as speculation lingers that BOJ may take additional easing steps in near term as concerns grow that Japan's economy may be losing momentum, says trader at Tokyo securities house. After release of weaker-than-expected GDP data earlier in day, "many investors have started to expect that the BOJ could be forced to do something to curb the stronger yen and prop up the economy. Demand for JGBs will likely remain solid for a while." 10-year JGB yield now down 3.5 bps at 0.945%. Lead September JGB futures up 0.29 at 142.68 points. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0549 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares down 0.1% at 861.13 at midday break in moderate trade, off intra-session trough at 857.51, with tad down after losses on regional markets, Wall Street while local market closed for holidays Thursday, Friday. Optimism over upcoming Thai 2Q GDP data, companies' dividend payments helps limit downside, says analyst with Far East Securities Weerachai Krongsamsri. "The market's correction will be short-lived," he adds. Thailand's 2Q GDP due Aug. 23. Support tipped at 855, resistance at 865. Among most active, \uc2 Charoen Pokphand Foods\uc2  (CPF.TH) +2.2% at THB25.25, \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) off 2.9% at THB99.50, \uc2 True Corp\uc2 . (TRUE.TH) +1.6% at THB6.25, PTT +1.2% at THB253, Bangkok Bank (BBL.TH) off 1.8% at THB134.50. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0548 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR/CHF rate will play a key role in the timing of the next \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  interest-rate increase, according to \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . "Amid the still very volatile environment, the SNB will likely strive to avoid any additional pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair resulting from narrowing of the interest-rate differential to the euro zone," says economist Fabian Heller. "However, if the economic outlook and market environment proves to be robust in coming months and confidence in the euro returns, an interest-rate hike by the SNB ahead of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains plausible," he adds. Libor futures contracts show markets don't expect a 25-basis-point increase until the end of 2011, he says. EUR/CHF at 1.3430. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0547 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +69.5% at HK$2.526 billion, 7.7% of market total, vs 4.8% Friday's midday, suggests HSI's 0.2% rise midday likely a technical correction after index's 4-session 3.3% decline. \uc2 Sun Hung Kai Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) tops list with HK$297.41 million after government's fresh measures to curb property prices, while \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) in 2nd place with HK$210.43 million. Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK), HKEx (0388.HK), \uc2 Sino Land\uc2  (0083.HK) round off top 5 with HK$133.90 million, HK$122.75 million, HK$119.08 million, respectively. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0542 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 September futures down 1.1% at 9170, but well off intraday low of 9080, on slower-than-expected growth in April-June GDP. "Futures have been oversold so dragging them down further entails risk," says Hideki Horikawa, chief analyst at Himawari Securities. Notes technical signals suggest futures index due for rebound to 9300-9400 by end of this week after shedding 4.1% last week. Adds investors also closely watching whether Prime Minister Naoto Kan will hold meeting with BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa to exchange views on yen's recent rise. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0538 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.3% at 4448.1, continues to recover in late trading. "I think results in Australia and fundamentals in Asia are pretty good," says \uc2 UBS\uc2  Head of Sales, George Kanaan. "Outlook statements have been a bit subdued, but results generally aren't missing expectations and you've got underlying dividend support." Notes that companies tied to Asia, such as Leighton (LEI.AU), are performing well. "I think the market has got a good floor at the moment and unless we get a blip down overseas, the market looks pretty relaxed." Continues to expect S&P/ASX 200 to rise to 5000 by year end. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0535 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW continues to trim earlier gains, falling back below 1,190 level as Kospi pares much of earlier losses in afternoon (now off 0.2% vs down 0.8% earlier), triggering bout of stop-loss sell orders on USD. Pair now up at 1,187.4 vs 1,195.2 around noon, 1,183.8 late Friday in Seoul. USD/KRW likely peaked at 1,200.3 for today, trader says. Local bank trader puts support at 1,185. Another local bank trader says, "I'm not sure of which direction to bet on from here," given such volatile trading patterns so far today. Adds, offshore players now quiet after some aggressive buying in morning trade. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0532 GMT [Dow Jones] Sliding AUD/USD is going to keep running lower, says Callum Henderson, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy for Standard Chartered in Singapore. AUD/USD recently at 0.8922, down from 0.9021 late Friday as economic jitters across globe have traders flooding to safety. "From our perspective, there is more to go in this pullback. The previous pullback went from 0.93 to 0.80," says Henderson, adding that on fundamental level, backdrop of slowing global economy, slowing China, will weigh heavily. Still, Henderson tips support at 0.8840 as particularly robust along way down. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com) [ 08-16-10 0214ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100816e68g00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
