\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Japan's MOF Unlikely To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Mkts-Citi\b0\par\par\uc2 1,600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2010\par 07:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0520 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 ministry of finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unlikely to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 , even though government appears to have heightened vigilance on JPY strength. Notes 2 psychological hurdles - "First, they probably think that other industrialized counties, especially the U.S., are seeking weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in order to support their economies and prevent deflation and that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might be problematic in this context." Also, MoF likely believes \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is difficult when international community is pushing China to increase CNY flexibility. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0518 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered expects benchmark 3-month Hibor to stay low near 0.30% from now until 2012 as it expects first U.S. Fed hike to come only in 2Q 2012 amid uncertain U.S. economic recovery. "We believe that the Hong Kong (and China) recovery story will remain fundamentally compelling to global investors," which will help to lure inflows, keeping interbank liquidity ample, report says. Expects Hibors to start climbing only in early 2012. 3-month Hibor steady at 0.28%.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0513 GMT [Dow Jones] India interbank call rate higher as banks cover cash requirements for weekend, but rate may turn lower as most lenders have already covered positions on account of reporting Friday, says trader with state-run bank. Banks have to report cash reserve levels to RBI at end of every fortnight. Overnight call rate at 5.76% vs 5.25% last close. Collateralized lending and borrowing obligation, or CBLO, rate slips to 4.61% vs 5.00% at open, banks avoid borrowing from CBLO window as they have to pledge bonds as collateral. "The RBI has made available two liquidity adjustment facility windows on reporting Fridays, when cash positions of most banks are covered well in advance, so borrowing rates may be subdued," trader says. Tips call rate may slip to 5.00% in session while CBLO support tipped at 3.75%. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0509 GMT [Dow Jones] China's banking regulator may be losing faith in the country's trust companies, says Heard on the Street, another amber light for foreign institutions considering investing in this sector; increased scrutiny by the CBRC comes amid worries over practice of banks selling loans to trust companies so that they can repackage them and sell them on to investors. That threatens steady source of income for trust companies, which at forefront of financial services innovation in China; part of trusts' appeal till now has been their relative freedom from regulatory scrutiny, but that seems to be changing. See "HEARD ON THE STREET: Losing Faith In China's Trust Companies" (andrew.peaple@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0458 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW turns lower in afternoon trade on back of improved sentiment in regional stock markets, pushing Kospi higher (last up 1.1%), traders say. Local bank trader tips pair to target 1,182; "if Chinese shares extend gains later, it will be enough to push the pair down to that low level." But another local bank trader expects downside to be cushioned by foreign selling of local shares, while "exporter selling of dollars isn't as strong as it was yesterday." Pair last tad down at 1,185.2 vs 1,186.6 midday, 1,186.2 late yesterday in Seoul. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0446 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.2% at 9230.12 vs 9186.32 midday close (down 0.3%); volume low with Japanese "Obon" holiday season in full swing and as investors await string of U.S. economic data and action from Japanese government to stem yen's rise. "If it turns out that the government is just giving lip service (regarding strong yen), markets will test critical 9000 support," says Masayoshi Yano, senior market analyst at Meiwa Securities. Puts next week's Nikkei range at 9000-9300, noting index may rise to 9500 provided BOJ takes additional easing step(s). 18/33 Topix subindexes higher; some flight to domestic demand/defensive issues seen: \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO) up 0.6% at Y12,890, Eisai (4523.TO) up 1.7% at Y2,994. Panasonic (6752.TO) up 1.6% at Y1,082 after GS ups to Buy. Many exporter shares continued to underperform; \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) down 1.2% at Y2,765, Fanuc (6954.TO) off 0.9% at Y9,600, \uc2 Nikon\uc2  (7731.TO) flat at Y1,495. CSK (9737.TO) down 8.8% at Y299 after brokerage target cut. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0447 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.1% at 21,120.46 midday, struggles to recover after index's 3-session 3.2% fall, amid uncertainty over global economic recovery after U.S. disappointing jobs data, says Daniel So at Sun Hung Kai Financial. "The index is now struggling at around its 250-day moving average (21,020) and it's unlikely to move much further up or down from that level, as there's still caution over the global economic outlook." Tips near-term support at 20,600. Still, expects upcoming corporate results of China banks, oil plays could help support trading sentiment in local market. Among these, ICBC (1398.HK) +0.2% at HK$5.70, Bank of China (3988.HK) +0.5% at HK$4.04, Cnooc (0883.HK) down 0.2% at HK$12.74, Sinopec (0386.HK) +0.7% at HK$6.15. Volume modest at HK$30.84 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0446 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares by midday up 0.4% at 3037.205 in light volume thanks to foreign bargain-buying in select consumer-related, telecommunication stocks, traders say; tip resistance at 3050. "The market is seen trying to recover from recent falls despite sporadic selling by local funds," says trader at Lautandhana Securities. Car maker Astra (ASII.JK) +1.2% at IDR48,100, heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) +1.2% at IDR8,500. Profit-taking hits cigarette maker \uc2 Gudang Garam\uc2  (GGRM.JK) down 0.7% at IDR35,300. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares still mostly higher as bargain hunters return after steep falls this week. Gains, however, generally modest as underlying sentiment remains cautious. "It is unlikely fears of a slowing U.S. economy will go away anytime soon, unless the next batch of economic indicators, especially job numbers, shows a pick-up in growth," says AmFraser Securities strategist Najeeb Jarhom. STI +0.4% at 2939.24 midday, with immediate resistance at 2950, followed by 2969 (Aug. 11 high). Market breadth at almost 2 gainers for every decliner. Genting Singapore (G13.SG) most sought-after stock in market, +10.9% at S$1.42 on 497.4 million shares, accounting for almost a third of overall market volume, on strong 2Q10 results. Wilmar (F34.SG) bucks gains by most other blue chips, off 2.2% at S$6.13, on disappointment with 2Q10 performance. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares heading for positive close, last up 0.3% at 3015.102. "After a bit of downside over the past few days. It has bounced back mainly in reaction to a firmer Australian market today," says Hamilton Hindin Greene broker Grant Williamson. "It is finding a little bit of buying support." Retailers like Pumpkin Patch (PPL.NZ) +1.2% at NZ$1.75, Kathmandu (KMD.NZ) +1.9% at NZ$1.64, benefiting from stronger-than-expected retail sales data. In other direction, \uc2 PGG Wrightson\uc2  (PGW.NZ) down 7.1% at NZ$0.52 after FY result, outlook; "Investors are just a touch disappointed that the outlook is not a little bit brighter." Says focus now firmly on corporate earnings due to come "fast and furious" next week. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.6% midday at 1357.40 boosted by bargain buying of selected heavyweights, led by Genting (3182.KU) after its Singapore unit swings to the black. Adds, techs (+2.8%) leading gains despite sector dragging Wall Street overnight. "After six-consecutive days of losses, players are finding some bargains among the chip makers... total market volume (at 554 million shares) is also a little better than of late," says dealer. Says 1360 immediate resistance likely to stay intact as players may turn tad cautious ahead of weekend; support at 1350. Market breadth positive with 391 advancers vs 222 decliners; among actives, Genting +6.0% at MYR8.16, Axiata (6888.KU) +1.6% at MYR4.32, tech plays JCY (5161.KU) +6.1% at MYR1.21, Unisem (5005.KU) +5.6% at MYR2.06. Decliner \uc2 Nestle\uc2  (4707.KU) down 0.5% at MYR39.00. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0437 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY stays up midday in relatively quiet trade after higher fixing; as concerns over potential \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -guided flows later in session also kept players from selling USD, say traders. On OTC, USD/CNY at 6.7880 vs 6.7851 last close; tipped in 6.7820-6.7950 band in afternoon. "People are careful not to push the dollar-yuan much lower because the dollar faces strong support around CNY6.7800, given there was suspected dollar demand from state-owned banks close to level yesterday," says Shanghai-based trader at foreign bank. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com) [ 08-13-10 0120ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022 