\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:USD/KRW Up; Geopolitical,  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Risks Weigh\b0\par\par\uc2 1,611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2010\par 02:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW breaks 7-session declining streak, last up at 1,163.65 vs 1,160.1 yesterday, on news North Korea fired artillery in contested maritime border, higher chance of BOK \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  being supportive of USD, say market participants. Still, overall conditions continue to point to a lower USD/KRW, so pair unlikely to advance much today. "Given that the direction is still tilted towards the downside, it's just not that easy to buy actively again simply because it's rising now," says local bank trader. Pegs pair in 1,158-1,166 band rest of session. Foreign bank trader tips USD/KRW to stay within 1,160s today, tips robust export settlements to flow in if pair approaches higher 1,160s. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0049 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 turns down 0.2% to 4584.1 in quiet trading before the FOMC meeting later Tuesday. Index has slipped as low as 4577.7 amid weakness in BHP Billiton (BHP.AU), Westfield (WDC.AU), NAB (NAB.AU). BHP down 0.8% at A$41.21, slightly below its ADR's equivalent close, while Westfield (WDC.AU) down 2.8% ex-distribution, NAB down 0.5% on disappointing 3Q cash profit. But NAB seems well supported on dips and CBA (CBA.AU) is up 0.3% ahead of tomorrow's FY results. "The equity markets are in a holding pattern as they wait to see what the FOMC does in terms of whether they will let the economy continue to chug along or whether they signal quantitative easing," says IG Markets strategist Cameron Peacock. "The Australian share market outperformed on Monday, so it's not surprising to see us underperforming today." Other traders are expecting a break above the June 21 high at 4622 later today, after Monday's break above resistance from last week's high and the 100-day moving average in the 4584-4488 area. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] FOMC meeting focal point of market and there's significant expectation that Fed will comment on a reinvestment policy for maturing assets, says Greg Gibbs, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBS. But adds RBS U.S. economic research team anticipates that Fed will just allude to a weaker economic outlook. Adds perhaps the one dissenter, Hoenig, will end his objection over extended period language. House says there's room for disappointment in Fed statement that undermines risk appetite if it fails to discuss concrete steps under consideration to enhance its easing policy in some way. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi +0.2% at 1793.15 as investors remain cautious ahead of Fed meeting outcome while North Korean issues have limited impact on market, analysts say. "Uncertainties remain, but the market hopes the Fed will signal steps to boost U.S. economy, so the index isn't likely to be pressured downward," says \uc2 Hanyang Securities\uc2 ' Lim Dong-rak; adds, "Many events, such as Fed's results and the BOK's rate review, are planned this week, but they aren't likely to offer a concrete direction for the market, it may just make the market volatile." Analysts say liquidity still abundant with foreigners, local institutions snatching up stocks today. Recent KRW strength also offering "good" excuse for foreigners to buy Korea. Investors calm to news North Korea yesterday fired artillery near contested maritime border; "Unless they (South and North Korea) clash, the impact will likely be limited," Lim says. Kospi tipped in 1790-1800 range today. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0036 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to extend gains after strength in U.S. stocks overnight, Asian stocks so far; Taiex may test 8100 vs over 3-month high of 8034.49 (+0.9%) yesterday; "Taiwan shares' recent gains have been led by non-technology companies (financial firms, traditional industry companies etc.) and we think that trend can continue because of rising global raw material prices," says Maggie Chien, manager at \uc2 President Securities\uc2 . Index tipped 8000-8100 for session. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0033 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.9% at 9654.56 with select exporter shares snapped up on firmer USD/JPY (now 85.98), but light market trading volume expected to continue ahead of BOJ policy meeting result today, U.S. FOMC meeting later in global trading day. "The BOJ will likely not change its policy, but it may indicate a sense of crisis over recent yen strength," which could be enough to boost market sentiment, says Toshikazu Horiuchi, equity strategist at Cosmo Securities. Puts today's Nikkei range at 9550-9700. 28/33 Topix subindexes higher; Disco (6146.TO) up 6.5% at Y5,110, \uc2 Mitsubishi Materials\uc2  (5711.TO) up 5.1% at Y249 on solid 1Q earnings/outlook hikes. \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  (8035.TO) up 2.6% at Y4,560, Kyocera (6971.TO) up 1.9% at Y7,930. \uc2 Shiseido\uc2  (4911.TO) off 0.7% at Y1,951, \uc2 JX Holdings\uc2  (5020.TO) down 1.2% at Y477. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0033 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan 20-year government bond likely to fall sharply on worse than expected 20-year bond auction yesterday, says local bank trader. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells NT$40 billion worth of 20-year bonds at 1.7890% yield vs 1.7400%-1.7600% band expected, on tepid demand from insurers; "insurers did not seem to be pressing to buy the 20-year bond." But expects insurers to step into market after 20-year yield rises to 1.8000% vs 1.7340% yesterday in when-issued trade. 20-year yield eyed in 1.7500%-1.8000% band for day, 5-year yield tipped in 0.9200%-0.9300% band vs 0.9173% yesterday. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0033 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall if BOJ decides not to ease monetary policy at its policy-setting meeting which ends later in day, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  chief strategist Japan Masafumi Yamamoto. House expects no change in BOJ's policy this month as Japan's authorities have been signaling they won't tackle recent JPY strength immediately, he says. Adds, investors to keep close eye on FOMC meeting tonight; if Fed eases its policy or at least signals plans to ease monetary policy in near term, USD/JPY may fall sharply again, he says. Citibank Japan's chief strategist Osamu Takashima says he doesn't rule out USD/JPY's break below 85.00 just yet. Pair last at 85.98. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0030 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds may fall a tad as Asian stocks should remain well supported, with local stocks possibly outperforming on catch-up rally after market closed yesterday for National Day holiday. However, trader says fall should be subdued after recent rally in yields, which may also limit any impact from Fed announcements later. Focus now on upcoming 20-year bond auction in early September, trader adds. Friday, Singapore's 2-year bond yields were flat at 0.48%, 10-year bond yields up 1 bp to 2.03%, ending week with 8bp gain. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0023 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may rise, taking cues from gains on Wall Street overnight; market's rise however, may be limited near-term as investors await outcome of FOMC meeting later today. "The series of earnings reports coming out over the next two weeks also gives investors a reason to stay cautious for now," dealer says; tips KLCI to remain in 1355-1365 range near-term. Glovemakers such as Top Glove (7113.KU) may be in focus on concerns recent appreciation of MYR will squeeze profit margins. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0022 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD lower at 1.3485 vs 1.3498 late Asia Friday, with market shrugging off final Singapore 2Q GDP reading (on-year growth marginally slower at 18.8% vs 19.3% estimated in July) and likely to focus on BOJ, Fed meetings later today, which should clip downside ahead of cues on upcoming monetary policy trends. Action yesterday subdued, with local market shut for public holiday, but pair still dropped in line with other key Asian pairs, to fresh 2-year low at 1.3453 - that may be key support level today. First resistance tipped at 1.3500. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0020 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares may inch up following Wall Street's modest gains, but investors likely to tone down expectations ahead of Fed's interest rate decision, accompanying statement due later Tuesday. STI not expected to challenge year-to-date high of 3043, while 2963 (higher end of minor breakup gap formed July 23) may offer support on any pullback; ended down 0.4% at 2995.06 Friday (market closed yesterday for public holiday). Analyst at Singapore bank expects limited upside for shares in near term; "while corporate results so far have been OK, they are not fantastic. Economic data so far from the U.S. and even from China don't seem to be pointing to a strong 2011. July's U.S. nonfarm payrolls data, for example, came in worse than expected." Singapore's final 2Q 2010 GDP data +18.8% on-year vs +19.3% tipped by government last month. UOL Group (U14.SG), \uc2 Bukit Sembawang Estates\uc2  (B61.SG) may be in focus as companies latest to report June-quarter results. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com) [ 08-09-10 2053ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100810e68a0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
