\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/SGD Likely Up On Risk Aversion, Cap At 1.3590\b0\par\par\uc2 1,281 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2010\par 03:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0119 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD flat in Asian morning trade amid conflicting signals for pair, may rise on weaker Nikkei. "The risk environment has turned negative following the U.S. home sales and factory output numbers that have brought back doubts about the strength of the global economy," trader at local bank says. Adds USD itself weaker on speculation over more Fed easing moves to revive flagging economy. Tips 1.3590 resistance, support near 1.3490. Pair last 1.3521 vs 1.3523 late Asia. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0118 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares may see some pullback today on Wall Street weakness, also after bourse clocked up 9 straight sessions of gains, with foreigners net buyers for 7 consecutive days. "It looked like the market would go down yesterday, but then buying came in at the end. It just does not seem like there is much selling around," says Andrew Yates, head of sales for international equities at Asia Plus Securities. Market may also find some support among energy plays after crude oil hit $82/bbl for first time since May. Support at 860, resistance at 870. Index closed +0.2% at 864.48 yesterday. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW recovers 1,170 support, quickly coming off an earlier low of 1,166 amid Kospi's 0.4% fall as participants increasingly wary over possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities, after BOK suspected of having \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently in recent sessions to support USD, traders say. "A bout of short dollar covering kicked in, in the upper-1,160s region," lifting USD, while some offshore players also seen bidding up USD, says local bank trader. Puts cap at 1,173 amid anticipated USD selling by exporters above 1,170. Pair now tad down at 1,170.6 vs 1,171.6 late yesterday in Seoul. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0104 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may fall on profit-taking after recent gains, also tracking weak U.S. cues, says Francis Lun at Fulbright. Tips 21,200 support today, as any falls unlikely major given underlying sentiment still strong, supported by latest corporate results. "Some profit-taking is expected after the recent strong ride, but I still believe the Hang Seng Index could test 22,000 in the near-term." Says both HK, China property stocks may take breather after latest outperformance (Hang Seng Property Subindex +3.4% over past 2 sessions, vs HSI's 2.0%). HSI ended +0.2% at 21,457.66 Tuesday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0103 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ government says mobile calls to get cheaper now Commerce Commission to regulate wholesale prices charged by mobile network operators for providing services to customers from other network operators. Regulating "will improve competitition in the mobile market and result in lower prices for mobile phone users," says Communications Minister Steven Joyce. But \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  NZ, local unit of \uc2 Vodafone Group\uc2  (VOD, VOD.LN), Telecom (TEL.NZ) unhappy with news; "the New Zealand mobile telecommunications market is dynamic and active, and to further regulate at this point would only interfere in what is proving to be the most competitive part of the overall telco sector," says Tom Chignell, General Manager of Corporate Affairs for \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  NZ. Comments echo Telecom (TEL.NZ) spokesman Mark Watts who says regulation unwarranted, unnecessary. If move forces lower prices will bite into both companies' revenues but could help smaller player 2degrees as it fights to win market share. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0101 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB steady around 32.16-32.165 vs 32.16 late yesterday in Asia, but pair biased downward amid general bearish tone towards greenback after it stumbled against major rivals overnight following weak U.S. economic data, also speculation Fed may push additional measures to boost economy. "It's been fairly quiet this morning, but sentiment is definitely leaning towards a stronger baht," says Bangkok-based dealer. Support remains at 32.10, with \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  tipped to step-in to defend that level strongly; resistance at 32.20. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0101 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped in narrow band, with overnight gains of EUR likely tempered by slight losses in U.S. stocks on concerns over weak earnings and economic reports; pair may still slide to 45.00 psychological support after last settling to 45.13, with support at 44.95 and resistance at 45.40. "We saw mixed signals overnight. So it'll be wait-and-see initially, probably taking cues from the euro," trader says. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to fall after USD weakened further vs EUR overnight; USD/CNY fixing expected to be set around 6.7700-6.7718 vs 6.7722 yesterday; in OTC trade, USD/CNY tipped at 6.7690-6.7750 vs 6.7730 last close. "The likelihood of the fixing being set below 6.7700 is very slim," says Beijing-based trader at foreign bank. "We think it's unlikely the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will send a strong signal for yuan appreciation at this time. Besides, the euro's gains overnight weren't so large that will propel the yuan much higher." (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to fall in 31.650-31.850 band vs 31.850 yesterday, to mark 9th straight session of declines after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of easing on \b 