\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/SGD Buy Interest At 1.3480; Offers 1.3530-40\b0\par\par\uc2 1,371 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2010\par 02:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] Strong USD/SGD buying interest stands at 1.3480, while big sell orders eyed at 1.3530-40, says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -based dealer; adds market participants wary of MAS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . USD/SGD last quoted in interbank market at 1.3511-1.3516. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0052 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 HSBC\uc2  lifts Thailand's 2010 GDP growth forecast to 7.6% vs 6.1% projected in early June after underestimating economy's resilience following deadly antigovernment protests in April, May; tourist arrivals recovering, foreign investors poised to continue streaming in, domestic consumption robustly withstood political unrest, while exports continue to surprise on upside. Also raises 2011 growth forecast to 5.3% vs 4.7%. Such strong growth allows BOT to normalise rates further after 25bp hike to 1.50% last month; house tips 2 more increases by year end to 2.0%. "Overall, given that politics has had a tendency to rear its head in various forms in Thailand's recent history, it is heartening to see that so far there is enough separation between economic growth and political development," says \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Wellian Wiranto. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0049 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last down 0.3% at 1785.85 on weak U.S. cues with local institutions selling, but foreigners buying. But analysts eye only a mild correction and expect another attempt to head toward 1800 level this week. "Given the bunch of economic data expected, such as U.S. jobs data this week and next week's CPI data in China, the market may want to check these before making a move," says \uc2 Samsung Securities\uc2 ' Chung Myoung-ji; adds "investors psychologically view the 1800 as a strong resistance level so it needs good momentum in order to break above that." Foreigners still buying shares, such as autos, steel stocks, banks, but domestic institutional investors selling, pressuring index, analysts say. U.S. tech losses also weighing on local techs, with \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 0.4% at KRW812,000, LG Display (034220.SE) down 1.3% at KRW36,900. \uc2 Hyundai Heavy\uc2  (009540.SE) +2.5% at KRW282,500 on brighter earnings outlook. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0049 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares may fall, tracking weakness on Wall Street as disappointing earnings from U.S. heavyweights \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2  and \uc2 Dow Chemical\uc2  and weaker-than-expected U.S. economic data prompt worries about the strength of the recovery, says Fubon trader Annie Chang. "We see looming profit-taking pressure as Taiex has risen 2.7% in the last five sessions," she says. Tips 7800 as key support as local economy remains resilient, tips Taiwan shares likely to consolidate near term before they break through 8000 resistance. Financial, tourism stocks likely to fall on profit taking after recent gains. Yesterday, Hotel operator Formosa International Hotel (2707.TW) ended +6.5% at NT$494.00, Ambassador Hotel (2704.TW) +6.0% at NT$42.60. Benchmark Taiex closed +0.6% at 7957.53. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] Strong AUD/JPY sell interest stands at 79.00, says Singapore-based dealer; adds current 1.8% loss in Nikkei weighing heavily on pair, which last at 78.15. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] Large USD/JPY option-related bids stand at 85.00, while strong profit-taking bids from interbank players placed at 85.20-85.40 area, says \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 -based dealer; adds market participants wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as 85.00 nears, strong USD/JPY sell interest much higher up at 86.80 vs 85.65 last. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0046 GMT [Dow Jones] JGB yield curve likely to bull-flatten, as growing speculation over Fed additional easing expected to keep pushing USD/JPY down, says \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  strategist Kenro Kawano; USD/JPY earlier falls to fresh 8-month low (last at 85.62). Adds, however, BOJ unlikely to move toward easing at policy board meeting next week because there are no "decisive" indicators which pose threat to BOJ's assessment of Japan economy showing signs of moderate recovery. JGB yields down sharply across curve today due to U.S. Treasurys gains overnight, JPY rise; benchmark 10-year cash JGB last down 4.0 bps at 0.995% -- lowest level since August 2003. 20-year yield also down 4.5 bps at 1.650%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0041 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may trade lower, tracking losses in U.S. stocks; Shanghai Composite Index may test 2600 support after ending down 1.7% at 2627.00 Tuesday. "Profit taking will be heavy at current levels as many investors built up their positions around 2650," says Jacky Zhang at Capital Securities. Gold producers may rise after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said yesterday it will encourage development of gold market. Shenzhen index down 1.7% at 1078.98. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may fall, snapping its 8-day winning streak, following weakness in U.S. stocks overnight; dealers say failure of key index to test 1375 resistance yesterday may also spur profit-taking today. "Investors will probably take a breather to re-evaluate their positions. But longer-term trend remains upwards as long as the 1350 psychological support is intact," dealer says; tips KLCI to stay in 1350-1370 range near term after shares end up 0.2 points at 1363.83 yesterday. Genting Malaysia (4715.KU) may be in focus after winning support of New York Lottery for its bid to operate a slots facility in New York. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0037 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW falls below 1,170 support at market open, tracking broadly weaker USD overnight. Traders put initial support in 1,165-1,166 region after \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities suspected of having \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  yesterday to support USD near that area. Pair likely to stay in tight 1,165-1,172 range, making small moves amid lack of momentum, taking breather after recent steep falls, says local bank trader. But another trader notes, pair could extend falls later - possibly marking a steeper fall than that seen yesterday - given euro's strength, which tends to lift sentiment for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Pair last down at 1,168.1 vs 1,171.6 late yesterday in Seoul. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0031 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan 5-year government bonds may rise, boosted by gains in U.S. Treasurys, ample liquidity amid recent foreign fund inflows, but gains likely limited on profit-taking as yields near multi-month lows, says local bank trader. Notes most-active 5-year yield at 0.9349% late Tuesday after touching 5-month intraday low of 0.9125% Monday, tipped in 0.9150%-0.9350% band for day. But adds, 20-year bond may fall in when-issued trade as "insurers didn't seem interested in buying the 20-year bond at a yield below 1.8000% in its upcoming auction," he says. 20-year yield at 1.7625% yesterday, tipped in tight 1.7500%-1.7700% band for day. Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to auction NT$40 billion 20-year bond Aug. 9. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0030 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 1.4% at 9556.81, hit by strong yen. Exporters underperforming market, with \uc2 Nikon\uc2  (7731.TO) off 2.5% to Y1,497, \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  (8035.TO) off 2.4% at Y4,630, Nissan (7201.TO) off 1.8% at Y665; Nikkei may be supported at 9500 today, market observers say. "People may continue to worry about the rising yen as weak U.S. economic data is fueling concerns that the Fed may ease monetary policy," which could cause USD to fall further, says Naoki Fujiwara, manager at Shinkin Asset Management. Says even though Japanese exporters posted good 1Q earnings, selling may be unavoidable as long as USD/JPY trading below 86. Meanwhile, oil distributor \uc2 Idemitsu Kosan\uc2  (5019.TO) up 2.1% at Y6,690 on strong 1Q. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com) [ 08-03-10 2053ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100804e6840000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
