\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:USD/SGD Rebounds; Traders Unsure If MAS  \highlight22\uc2 Intervened \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2010\par 04:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD bounces back after hitting a new 2-year low overnight, traders say not sure if MAS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "Everyone's limits on the basket are set around those levels and the pair is self correcting," trader at local bank says. Adds "haven't heard of MAS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the pair may have bounced back on its own (via some SGD profit-taking), just the way MAS wants." Another trader adds "not sure" if MAS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Pair last 1.3507 vs 1.3477 overnight, 1.3540 late Asia yesterday. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP lower early on risk appetite after overnight gains on Wall Street; pair at 45.22 after settling at 45.33 late Monday, but off early low 45.13 in moderate volume; support 45.00, resistance 45.50; "We haven't seen the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far today, and we might not see them because some banks are starting take profit on the peso as the euro loses ground," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0226 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 1.2% at 9687.47 midday (well off intraday high of 9750.88); trading houses and other commodity-linked shares surge on sharp overnight rally in crude futures and continuing expectations China will refrain from new tightening steps after weak PMI data. "It's difficult for Japanese stocks to test higher, though, in the face of underlying strong yen concerns," says Hideyuki Ishiguro, strategist at \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2 . Notes worries about weaker USD/JPY (now 86.43) ahead of key U.S. economic figures due this week. Puts afternoon range for Nikkei at 9650-9750. All 33 Topix subindexes higher. Among trading companies, \uc2 Mitsubishi Corp\uc2  (8058.TO) up 4.2% at Y1,964, \uc2 Mitsui & Co\uc2  (8031.TO) up 4.0% Y1,153. \uc2 JX Holdings\uc2  (5020.TO) also up 2.1% at Y483. Banks and brokerages rise on brisk earnings from European banks; SMFG (8316.TO) up 1.9% at Y2,715. \uc2 Nomura Holdings\uc2  (8604.TO) up 3.1% at Y503. \uc2 Ricoh\uc2  (7752.TO) off 2.6% at Y1,201 after slashing FY 1H revenue forecast. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0219 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares last +0.5% at 7952.31, boosted by gains on Wall Street overnight, early rise in Asian stock markets, says Yuanta Securities' Kerry Chen. Taiex tipped in 7950-8030 range. "The local market is still on the uptrend as global economy remains resilient." Chip design company MediaTek (2454.TW) rebounds 3.4% at NT$445.00 on bargain hunting after it fell 11% in last 4 sessions on concerns over 2H demand. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0219 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD drops slightly on weaker-than-expected Aussie retail sales, building approvals data, though big drop is unlikely ahead of afternoon RBA decision on interest rates, says Greg Gibbs, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy for RBS Australia. "The data does lend itself to the RBA being on hold, but it's going to be a minor reaction because that's already consensus," says Gibbs. AUD/USD recently at 0.9109, down from 0.9125 just prior to data. Gibbs tips support at 0.9060, resistance at 0.9120-0.9130. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.7% at 21,562.90, adding to 1.8% rise Monday, supported by rally on Wall Street; but further upside likely capped as declines in China markets (Shanghai Composite down 0.1%) suggest profit-taking likely to set in. "The investment environment continues to be optimal, given the improving corporate earnings outlook, while a loose monetary backdrop will be underpinned by the fragile economic recovery in most of the leading economies," Taifook says. Expects rangebound trading to prevail near term, with trading range tipped at 21,000-22,500. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) +1.9% at HK$82.60, while Hang Seng Bank (0011.HK) flat at HK$109 after releasing results. China property stocks up on expectation China to ease off on tightening pace, with China Overseas (0688.HK) +2.0% at HK$17.56, \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) +1.8% at HK$17.46. Volume brisk at HK$8.19 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] Good-sized USD/JPY offers stand at 86.80-87.00 area, but large bids staggered from 86.20 down to 85.90, says Singapore-based dealer; adds Middle East name bought huge amounts of USD/JPY yesterday, but only managed to drive pair to 86.89 high as large offers there stalled further gains. USD/JPY last 86.43. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds steady after PBOC leaves 1-year bill yield unchanged at 2.0929% for 9th straight week in open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , move indicates \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  satisfied with current market interest rate levels, says analyst at Shanghai Securities. Adds, PBOC cuts 1-year bill sale size to CNY18 billion from last week's CNY23 billion following weak July PMI data, indicating its effort to ensure sufficient liquidity in the banking system to support economy. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.10 vs 126.09 last close, tipped in narrow 126.0-126.2 band rest of session. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares moving up to fresh year high, with overnight gains in Wall Street on good manufacturing data spurring buying interest in blue chips; benchmark PSEi up 0.8% to 3481.97 in moderate volume, with gainers swamping losers 66 to 15; support 3450, resistance 3500; leading advancers are Metropolitan Bank (MBT.PH), up 1.4% to PHP61, Philippine National Bank (PNB.PH), up 4.5% to PHP41.80, property group Alliance Global Group (AGI.PH), up 2.2% to PHP6.07, and Megaworld (MEG.PH), up 1.5% to PHP1.57. Ricardo Puig, research head at ATR Kim Eng Securities, says good earnings and improving business sentiment underpinning positive investor sentiment, although limited liquidity in market capping gains.(cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] Little to change near-term outlook for RBA policy in Australia's building approvals or retail sales numbers, but focus should be further out the horizon as further hikes likely if 3Q inflation surprises on upside, says St George Bank economist Amanda Tan. "Chances are (the RBA) could still raise rates." But for today, expects cash on hold at 4.50%; "there's nothing to affect the implication of last week's inflation figures." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0213 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY slightly lower on USD weakness overnight, but downside limited as central parity at 6.7722 vs 6.7742 yesterday set higher than market's expectation of around 6.7700. In OTC trade, USD/CNY at 6.7730 vs 6.7742 last close; tipped to stay in 6.7710-6.7750 range for session. "Considering the large fall of the dollar overnight, the fixing's decline is quite modest," says Shanghai-based trader at foreign bank. "It means the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still doesn't want the dollar-yuan level to easily fall below the recent low." Adds trading volume not heavy so far, with most price quotes close to fixing level. Offshore 1-year USD/CNY NDFs at 6.6710/6.6750 vs 6.6728/6.6758 late yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0212 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai PM Abhisit says domestic economy could expand as much as 7%-8% this year owing to surging exports, but cautions that such rapid growth may be restrained, pulled back closer to 6% because of financial risks in European economies; tells Dow Jones he'd be satisfied with annual economic growth rates averaging 4%-5%. "Too rapid and unbalanced growth is something that we don't want. I think that it's important that growth is in line with what society can adapt to. Too rapid growth often creates tension, socially and also politically." PM says China's decision to allow greater yuan flexibility has had no major effect on Thai exports, government more worried about EU, euro. Adds trade with China has great potential for expansion, while India also key market for trade, investment. Also says elections - which must be called by end 2011 - could be held as soon as early next year, though wants period of stability before calling poll. See INTERVIEW: Thai PM: 2010 Economic Growth Could Hit 7%-8%. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com) [ 08-02-10 2234ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100803e6830002a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
