\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Higher Late On Risk Taking, May Stabilize\b0\par\par\uc2 244 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2010\par 09:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                          Latest   Change \par    USD/SGD               1.3523   -0.0017 \par    Overnight rate        0.13%    -6 bps \par    Two-year bond yield:  0.45%    Unchanged \par    10-year bond yield:   2.03%    -1 bp \par    Two-year swap offer:  0.91%    -4 bps \par    10-year swap offer:   2.57%    -2 bps \par    2-10-yr swap curve:   166 bps  +2 bps \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar continued to rise against the U.S. dollar after a string of strong company results across the world attracted investors to riskier assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar fell to as low as S$1.3477 in overnight trade, before recovering a bit as traders booked profits after the pair reached close to what many believe to be the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's undisclosed tolerance limit.\par \par \uc2 "Most traders' limits are set around the perceived level where the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That helped the U.S. dollar claw back some lost territory," said a trader at a local bank.\par \par \uc2 The local unit may remain near the current levels through the week "unless some scary data spooks the markets," the trader said, adding the U.S. dollar is unlikely to fall below its historic low of S$1.3438.\par \par \uc2 Singapore government bonds were flat with investors chasing other assets such as shares and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -By Gaurav Raghuvanshi; Dow Jones Newswires; +65 64154 154; gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com [ 03-08-10 0736GMT ]\par \pa