\par\par \b \uc2 3rd UPDATE: Singapore 2Q GDP Soars; MAS To Let  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 898 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2010\par 06:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , \uc2 HSBC\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Gaurav Raghuvanshi, Sam Holmes and Costas Paris \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Singapore's economy continued to soar in the second quarter as manufacturing surged, prompting the government to sharply raise its growth forecasts and sending the local dollar to a three-week high.\par \par \uc2 Gross domestic product jumped 26% on a seasonally adjusted and annualized basis from the previous three months, the government said Wednesday, well above the 17.4% forecast in a Dow Jones Newswires survey.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore won't stand in the way of the local dollar's subsequent rise, a person familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 The city-state's rocketing growth is pacing Asia's strong recovery, which is leading the rest of the world out of the economic crisis. Asian central banks recently have become more aggressive in raising interest rates to preempt inflation, even as Europe's debt crisis and a slowing U.S. economy cast a shadow over major economies.\par \par \uc2 Given sluggish demand in those key export markets, however, Singapore's Ministry of Trade and Industry said the island economy's sizzling growth is likely to moderate going forward, even as it revised up its first-quarter growth reading to 45.9%--the fastest on record going back to 1975--from an initial estimate of 38.6%.\par \par \uc2 "The exceptionally strong growth experienced by the Singapore economy in the first half of 2010 is therefore not likely to be sustained into the second half of the year," the ministry said in a statement. "While year-on-year growth rates in the second half will be healthy, sequential growth from current levels of economic activity will be low."\par \par \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  also noted the expected shift ahead.\par \par \uc2 "Expect a slowdown (not a slump)" in the second half, two \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  economists wrote in a research report. "Given signs of easing momentum, and a slowdown in the U.S. and China," third-quarter GDP data should pull back 11% from the current torrid pace and be "broadly flat" in the fourth quarter--though they added that there were strong upside risks to this outlook.\par \par \uc2 On-year, GDP grew 19.3% in the second quarter, accelerating from the first quarter's 16.9% and beating the Dow Jones survey's forecast of a 16.5% rise. First-quarter growth was revised up from 15.5%.\par \par \uc2 The government raised its full-year growth forecast to 13%-15% from its May estimate of 7%-9%.\par \par \uc2 That's stronger than most economists' projections, but appears achievable, CIMB economist Song Seng Wun said.\par \par \uc2 "I suppose the only question now is how much more juice is left, but the forecasts are realistic," Song said, adding that 2010 is likely to be a tale of two halves.\par \par \uc2 The strong growth boosted the Singapore dollar, with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falling to S$1.3741, its lowest since June 21, from S$1.3858 before the announcement.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise unless the U.S. dollar falls to around 1.3600, said the person familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S dollar is still well within the MAS' range. The U.S. dollar is also broadly weak against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in recent days, the person said. "You won't see any movement by the MAS unless we hit the 1.36 level or below."\par \par \uc2 The MAS uses the trade-weighted exchange rate, rather than interest rates, as its main policy tool because trade flows dwarf the island's domestic economy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  now targets a "gradual and modest appreciation" for the Singapore dollar within an undisclosed trading band.\par \par \uc2 The person said that at this point "there are no thoughts about further tightening by the MAS when it meets in October" for its next scheduled policy review.\par \par \uc2 But others are entertaining such thoughts. If the economy's rapid growth continues, the MAS may tighten by "re-centering" its targeted band for the Singapore dollar, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Frederic Neumann wrote.\par \par \uc2 "There's no more slack left in the economy and the MAS may want to re-center again in October," Neumann said. "With numbers like these, it's certainly on the table."\par \par \uc2 Inflation remains relatively modest, with the government forecasting in May that consumer prices would rise 2.5%-3.5% for the full year. Wednesday's report doesn't give new inflation figures.\par \par \uc2 In the second quarter, manufacturing output rose 45.5% from a year earlier, accelerating from the first quarter's 38.2% growth.\par \par \uc2 Manufacturing expanded 58.6% on-year in May, the fastest since comparable records began in 1980, and breaking April's record of 49.7%. Services grew 11.4% on-year in the second quarter, while construction expanded 13.5%.\par \par \uc2 The main gauge of Singapore's exports rose 28.7% in June from a year earlier, topping the 26.1% rise for non-oil domestic exports forecast in the Dow Jones survey, and up from May's revised 24.3%.\par \par \uc2 All sectors expanded, with electronics shipments up 43.9% and pharmaceuticals rebounding 29.8%.\par \par \uc2 -By Gaurav Raghuvanshi; Sam Holmes and Costas Paris, Dow Jones Newswires; +65 64154 154; costas.paris@dowjones.com [ 14-07-10 0429GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100714e67e00092\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
