\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won jumps; Singapore dollar hits 3-week high on GDP\b0\par\par\uc2 947 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2010\par 08:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Sing dollar surges briefly on record Q2 economic growth\par \par \uc2 * Won jumps as much as 1.7 pct but faces resistance\par \par \uc2 * Dollar/yuan NDFs hover near 2-week low\par \par \uc2 * Baht inches up ahead of rate decision\par \par \uc2 By Kevin Yao\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, July 14 (Reuters) - The Singapore dollar briefly surged to a three-week high on Wednesday after data showed the economy grew at a record pace in the second quarter, while stronger stock markets boosted the South Korean won and most other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Asian stocks rose to a three-week high, led by gains in technology issues, while the euro held steady near a two-month high against the dollar, with high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Australian dollar supported by a seemingly significant improvement in risk appetite. [ID:nSGE66D02R]\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE DOLLAR, RINGGIT\par \par \uc2 Singapore dollar <SGD=> briefly hit a three-week high at 1.373 per U.S. dollar but later retreated to 1.3766, down slightly on the day, as investors increasingly worried that \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temper the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  jumped in early trade after data showed Singapore's economy grew by a record 19.3 percent in the second quarter from a year earlier, prompting the government to raise its full-year growth forecast. [ID:nSGE66C0GX]\par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> rose as much as 0.7 percent to 3.1800 per dollar, the highest in just over three weeks, tracking rising stock markets and the Singapore dollar.\par \par \uc2 But the ringgit later trimmed gains on short dollar covering.\par \par \uc2 "It looks like some players were still caught short (dollars) after NDF fixing. I think 3.20 should be toppish for now," said a trader in Kuala Lumpur.\par \par \uc2 WON\par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> jumped as much as 1.7 percent to 1,191.9 per dollar as foreign investors continued to buy local stocks and exporters bought the won for settlements.\par \par \uc2 But the won faces some resistance and investors appear to be reluctant to chase the won <KRW=> more aggressively on caution over possible dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities.\par \par \uc2 It later pulled back to the weaker side of 1,200.\par \par \uc2 "Dollar bids are quite strong around 1,197 and it looks difficult for the won to strengthen more," said a local bank dealer.\par \par \uc2 Seoul shares <.KS11> rose 1.6 percent as foreign investors buy a net 701.0 billion won worth of stocks on the main exchange.\par \par \uc2 YUAN\par \par \uc2 Dollar/yuan NDFs hovered near a two-week low in thin activity, with gains capped by the PBOC's slightly stronger daily mid-point <CNY=SAEC> for the yuan against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Moves in forwards are getting smaller. They are trying to break out of the range," said a trader at a European bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 One-year dollar/yuan non-deliverable forwards (NDFs) <CNY1YNDFOR=> were quoted little changed at 6.6548 bid at midday from Tuesday's close of 6.6535, with their implied 12-month yuan appreciation falling a tad to 1.81 percent from 1.90 percent.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  set the yuan's mid-point <CNY=SAEC> at 6.7755 against the dollar on Wednesday, up from Tuesday's 6.7802, while spot yuan was trading at 6.7732 to the dollar at midday, down slightly from Tuesday's close of 6.7720.\par \par \uc2 The yuan has gained 0.8 percent since its depegging from the dollar late last month.\par \par \uc2 BAHT\par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=> inched up to 32.20 per dollar, tracking other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buoyed by expectations of a 25 basis point interest rate increase by the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  later in the day.\par \par \uc2 Nine out of 16 analysts polled by Reuters expect the BoT to raise its policy rate by 25 bps from a record low of 1.25 percent. Seven expect no change. [ID:nSGE66D02F]\par \par \uc2 "Investors are turning to riskier assets like stocks and commodities, pushing the dollar back. Most analysts see a 25 basis point rate rise although the expected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision has mostly been factored in," a Bangkok-based trader said.\par \par \uc2 The baht has appreciated 3.16 percent this year, trailing the Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> and Indonesian rupiah <IDR=>. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on the day at   0537 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        88.98          88.70       -0.31\par  Sing dlr        1.3765         1.3758       -0.05\par  Taiwan dlr      32.135         32.300       +0.51\par  Korean won     1201.40        1212.50       +0.92\par  Baht             32.30          32.33       +0.09\par  Peso             46.27          46.44       +0.37\par  Rupiah         9047.00        9045.00       -0.02\par  Rupee            46.65          46.77       +0.25\par  Ringgit         3.1955         3.2010       +0.17\par  Yuan            6.7731         6.7720       -0.02\par  Change so far in 2010\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        88.98          92.90       +4.41\par  Sing d