\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Asian Reserves Drop In May On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , Euro\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Roman   \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \par \uc2 801 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2010\par 06:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--Asia's official \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell 1.2% in May from April, the biggest drop in well over a year, hit by a decline in the value of euros held by central banks and the sale of U.S. dollars to stop local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening too fast.\par \par \uc2 As global markets were hit by concerns that euro-zone economies like Spain and Portugal may need financial assistance, and a military crisis on the Korean peninsula, central banks including those in South Korea and Indonesia were suspected of heavy dollar sales late in May to curb declines in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders estimate Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold as much as $2 billion-$2.5 billion in a single day, likely its biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  collapse in 2008.\par \par \uc2 These moves highlight the complex issues faced by central banks across the region, as they were coupled with similar, albeit generally smaller, \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars to contain appreciation pressures on local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Analysts say many central banks may remain on both sides of the market to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in narrow ranges in coming months, balancing the need to keep their exports competitive and inflation at bay.\par \par \uc2 According to calculations by Dow Jones Newswires, reserves held by 11 key Asian central banks, excluding China's, stood at US$2.776 trillion at the end of May, down from US$2.810 trillion at the end of April. That was the largest drop since January 2009, and compared with a 1.5% increase posted in April from March.\par \par \uc2 China, which only releases its data on a quarterly basis, reported US$2.447 trillion in reserves at the end of March.\par \par \uc2 Much of the decrease in Asian reserves was due to the lower valuation of euros held by central banks, since the euro lost 7.5% against the dollar in May. Euros are believed to account for around 20%-25% of total reserves.\par \par \uc2 Overall, emerging market central banks, mostly Asian, have bought around US$1 trillion worth of euros since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was launched in 1999, \uc2 United Overseas Bank\uc2  estimates. Speculation that these central banks may lose confidence in the euro has added to uncertainty in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, despite recent statements by big reserve holders like China and Russia, saying they remain committed to the euro as an alternative reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 In fact, many observers say that while a slowdown or even a freeze in future euro purchases is possible, central banks are unlikely to dump the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  even as it drops to fresh multi-year lows against the dollar, given a lack of safe, liquid alternatives -- and the fact that the U.S. fiscal situation is not much better than the European Union's.\par \par \uc2 "The biggest point here is that emerging market countries have not been accumulating reserves just in order to have more reserves; that's never been a policy goal. Rather, higher \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are a side-effect, a residual of the real policy goal, which has been to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  relatively stable," analysts at \uc2 UBS\uc2  said in a recent report. "And this means continuing to accumulate dollars and euros."\par \par \uc2 Following are the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for Asia's 12 leading economies. All numbers are in billions of U.S. dollars.\par \par \uc2 The total number excludes China.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par             May 10       May 09       April 10     As Of \par TOTAL        2,776       2,519         2,810   end April Ex China \par  \par China        2,447       1,954         2,447   end March \par Japan        1,041       1,024         1,047   end May \par India          272       261.2         279.6   28-May \par Indonesia     75.6        57.9          78.6   end May \par Korea        270.2       226.8         278.9   end May \par Malaysia      95.5        88.3            96   end May \par Pakistan        16        11.5            15   28-May \par Philippines   47.7        39.3            47   end May \par Singapore    198.4       171.8         203.4   end May \par Taiwan       360.1       312.6         357.6   end May \par Thailand     143.4       120.6         147.6   28-May \par Hong Kong    256.2       205.1         259.2   end May \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (David Roman, a regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reporter, has been a Dow Jones correspondent since 2002, covering economic and corporate news. He can be reached at 65 6415 4045 or by e-mail: david.roman@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 10-06-10 0420GMT ]