\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:Asian Reserves Down On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , EUR -  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA\b0\par\par\uc2 1,816 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2010\par 06:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0449 GMT [Dow Jones] Asia's official \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell 1.2% in May from April, biggest drop in more than a year, hit by decline in value of EUR held by central banks, sale of USD to stop local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakening too fast. As global markets hit by concerns euro-zone economies like Spain and Portugal may need financial assistance, military crisis on Korean peninsula, central banks including South Korea's, Indonesia's suspected of heavy USD sales late May to curb declines in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders estimate Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold as much as $2 billion-$2.5 billion in single day, likely biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  collapse. Moves highlight complex issues faced by central banks across region, as they were also coupled with similar, but generally smaller, \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy USD to contain appreciation of local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Analysts say many central banks may remain on both sides of market to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in narrow ranges in coming months, balancing need to keep exports competitive and inflation at bay. See =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Asian Reserves Drop In May On \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Euro (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0448 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end lower, led by blue chips, as some investors took profit amid continuing concerns over Europe's fiscal woes. PSEi down 1.0% to 3224.49 with all sub-indices posting declines amid heavy trading. "There could be some profit-taking after we hit a new high last week," notes Jose Vistan, AB Capital Securities research head. Tips market to stay in 3150-3300 range Friday as investors eye overseas cues. Leading market slide are PLDT (TEL.PH), off 0.2% at PHP2,380; Metropolitan Bank (MBT.PH), off 2.7% at PHP53.50; Universal Robina (URC.PH) off 3.8% at PHP25.50. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0443 GMT [Dow Jones] India call rate, collateralized lending and borrowing obligation, or CBLO, remains high, breaches RBI's key lending rate of 5.25% as corporates step up borrowing for advance tax payments, banks withdraw for INR110 billion debt sale Friday, says trader with state-run bank. Overnight call rate on government's negotiated dealing system at 5.25% vs 5.20% last close, CBLO unchanged vs 5.26 at open. "Banks have been borrowing about INR670 billion-INR700 billion daily from the RBI's key lending window. This requirement is likely to go up to INR1 trillion early next week as the tax payment deadline nears," trader says. Tips call rate in 5.10%-5.50% during session, CBLO in 5.10-5.30%. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 June futures up 0.4% at 9520; while next-tier downside support seen at 9250 for options, many investors expect June Nikkei options and futures to settle Friday around 9500 line, according to market analysts, as there are few selling catalysts. Rollover to September futures now seen going smoothly; index may move in narrow 9450-9550 range for remainder of session. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0437 GMT [Dow Jones] CIMB Economic Research says real GDP growth of 6.0% a year under 10th Malaysia Plan (5-year blueprint that runs from 2011 to 2015) "reasonable yet challenging." Economist Lee Heng Guie says being a small economy, Malaysia has been susceptible to positive, negative forces of globalisation. "Faced with external headwinds and internal constraints, the economy continues to perform below potential and has underperformed compared to others. But looking back at previous plans, we find that the government has a tendency of setting a high growth target when drawing up the plan and revising it downwards during the mid-term review," says Lee; notes estimated real GDP growth of 4.2% a year during the 9th Malaysia Plan period significantly below target of 6.0% a year as private sector underperformed. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] Gains in Singapore bourse accompanied by light volume as investors inclined to just nibble. STI +0.7% at 2764.28 midday, may continue to drift in this week's 2726-2772 trading range for rest of session. Market breadth tad positive, with volume thin at 418.2 million shares, driven mostly by lower liners. \uc2 Petra Foods\uc2  (P34.SG) most active, off 1.7% at S$1.18 on 76.4 million shares, following share placement. "There's no catalyst to lead the market any where. Most of our retail clients are gone from the market. It's part of the World Cup syndrome," says dealer at local brokerage. Key gainers among blue chips include Singapore Exchange (S68.SG), +1.1% at S$7.36, \uc2 SingTel\uc2  (Z74.SG), +1.4% at S$2.92, CityDev (C09.SG), +1.3% at S$10.62, \uc2 Singapore Press Holdings\uc2  (T39.SG), +1.1% at S$3.72. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.5% at 19,717.62 midday, tracking modest gains in regional bourses as EUR reverses early losses vs USD as Aussie job data, China export data, both stronger-than-expected, spur risk appetite. Returning risk appetite boosts Cnooc (0883.HK), +4.0% a HK$12.86, outpacing blue chips' gains; Esprit (0330.HK) +2.6% at HK$43.15 on EUR rebound. Still, likely too early to declare HSI out of woods despite index rising for 3rd straight session, as still trapped in 19,211-19,792 range held so far this week. "The euro's rebound is the key for a sustainable Hong Kong stock rally," says strategist at Chinese-background brokerage; adds, China CPI data due tomorrow also keenly awaited. Market volume remains lackluster at HK$25.80 billion at interval. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0430 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +0.6% at 500.51 vs 496.50 intraday low, with foreigners buying in select property stocks on 2Q earnings hopes. "Foreigners have been active in recent days after key index has fallen 9% over the past month, and I expect more local players to soon come back to buy on hopes that prices will recover on 2Q earnings results," says trader with ACB Securities. Volume low with 33.5 million shares valued at VND1.05 billion traded. Among actives, Itaco (ITA.VH) ends +1.3% at VND23,700, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) +1% at VND20,700, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) flat at VND34,800. Traders expect more gains tomorrow with key index to test 505. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0428 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares ticking up after market reacted positively to RBNZ rate hike, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker Suzanne Kinnaird; "This has been taken positively by the business community. It is a good sign that the economy can sustain an interest rate hike." Also, notes 25bp hike to 2.75% "not really a big deal compared to where we started the easing process." RBNZ slashed OCR by 575 bps between July 2008, April 2009 to combat severe recession. NZX-50 last +0.3% at 3007.54. Index bolstered by Telecom (TEL.NZ) +3.9% at NZ$1.88 on improving sentiment, bargain hunting after stock hit fresh all-time low yesterday. \uc2 New Zealand Oil & Gas\uc2  +3.7% (NZO.NZ) at NZ$1.41; news company encountered hydrocarbons at its partly-owned Tui SW-2 Well offshore from Taranaki on west coast of North Island helping sentiment. Says investors will remain closely focused on offshore markets for direction; "Any big moves either way will be reflected in our market tomorrow." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0427 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia April industrial output +10.1% on year vs revised +14.2% in March; slower than +12.4% expected by 15 economists polled by Dow Jones. Statistics Department says on year gains driven by manufacturing sector (+14.3% on year vs +20.5% in March), electricity (+12.1% on year vs +24.9% in March); mining +0.8% on year vs down 0.5% in March. Manufacturing sales +19.6% on year in April vs +26.4% in March. Local economist says though IPI lower than expectations, "it's not anything to be worried about and pretty much in line with the weaker April exports" due mainly to month-to-month fluctuations. Adds, on year growth "still intact"; expects on year output growth rate to start "normalizing" towards 2H as 1Q affected by low base effect. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0426 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi +0.7% at 1658.40 in light volume, off day's low of 1643.71 on hefty program buying, local retail investors' buying stocks; but foreign selling caps further gain. "Sentiment suddenly improved after EUR/USD showed a sharp rebound in Asian trading hours, easing risk aversion a little," says Lee Sun-yup, analyst at Shinhan Investment Corporation; but notes, impact neutral from BOK governor's comments on sustained growth of local economy, price stability. China's strong growth in exports in May seems to have eased worries over possible slowdown in global economic growth, says Lee Jin-woo, market analyst at Mirae Asset Securities. But analysts warn of emergence of big market swings closer to end of session due to simultaneous expiration of options, futures today, with 1670 near 120-day moving average as cap. Exporters up as well as China plays with steelmakers, shipyards, higher; \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) +1.0% at KRW778,000, \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) +0.6% at KRW33,000, Posco (005490.SE) +0.9% at KRW451,000, \uc2 Hyundai Heavy\uc2  (009540.SE) +4.4% at KRW213,500. But banks still down, with \uc2 Woori Finance\uc2  (053000.SE) down 2.0% at KRW15,050. (soo-yung.seo@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan MOF sells Y2.2117 trillion of 0.4% 5-year bonds with lowest price of 100.02, almost in line with market expectations, yielding 0.395%; bid-to-cover ratio improves to 3.57 from 3.11 in May, while tail at 0.02 vs 0.01 at last tender. Tender goes smoothly, as "worse-than-expected U.S. non-farm payroll figures (released last week) encouraged demand from domestic investors who had been hesitant to buy due to economic improvements and the announcement of Greek rescue plans," says Nobuto Yamazaki, executive fund manager at \uc2 DLIBJ Asset Management\uc2 . Adds speculation that new Japan government administration with Kan as prime minister could increase pressure on BOJ to ease policy further may also have had psychological impact on strength of policy-sensitive medium sector. September JGB futures down 0.07 at 140.70.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com) [ 06-10-10 0049ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100610e66a0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
