\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: MAS Won't  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In USD/SGD Yet-Source\b0\par\par\uc2 1,569 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2010\par 07:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] MAS comfortable with SGD moves vs USD, EUR, won't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  until USD/SGD rises "convincingly" above 1.4200, person familiar with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's thinking tells Dow Jones Newswires; notes pair still "well within the MAS trading band." Also notes stronger USD not reflective of weakness in Singapore or Asia but because of debt issues in euro zone. Also, MAS not concerned about falling EUR yet given island-state's exports to euro zone haven't fallen yet. USD/SGD last at 1.4055. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0528 GMT [Dow Jones] Even though Aussie bond prices ripe for pull back after European uncertainty drove big rally last week into today, Stephen Halmarick, head of investment markets research for Colonial First State says drop may not be in cards near term. Notably, 10-year bond prices are up 4 ticks to 94.65 today, up from low of 94.475 last week. "Any stability in equity markets and the rally in fixed income would turn around given economic data out of Australia is still reasonably good. But the near-term outlook is too uncertain still given the questions in Europe," says Halmarick. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0520 GMT [Dow Jones] Rubber producers giving leg up to Asian economy, as prices of this key commodity have doubled since July last year, says DJ's Money Talks column. Strong sales of tires, automobiles in post-recession period benefiting rural economies of Southeast Asia as most natural rubber is produced by poor growers in holdings of 2.0 hectares each or less. Almost 84% of world's natural rubber is produced in Thailand, Indonesia, Malaysia, Vietnam, which also control around 90% of global trade. Europe's debt crisis isn't expected to have big impact on rubber producers, which are banking on Asian demand. See: "=MONEY TALKS: Rubber Producers Give Asian Econ A Leg Up" (sameer.mohindru@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0510 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds fall more on Prime Minister's comments that high prices are matter of deep concern, government's highest priority is containing inflation, says trader with private bank. Benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond currently at 102.57 after falling to 102.50 post-comments vs 102.60 before comments. "There has been a sell-off since morning on 3G money outflow concerns, which was accentuated by the Prime Minister's comments on curbing inflation which could imply more interest rate hikes," dealer says. Tips benchmark 2020 bond in 102.40-102.70 range vs 102.95 Friday close. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0509 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered pushes back house view on timing of CNY "de-pegging" from USD to 3Q from previous call of move in May, due to recent financial market volatility, deteriorating sentiment toward global recovery, recent sharp rise of CNY in real effective exchange rate. China still needs to allow CNY to strengthen vs USD to ease tensions with trade partners, while stabilizing EUR would help as well, Standard Chartered says. "If China does not move in the summer, it will become more difficult to do so later in the year, when domestic growth is likely to slow," firm says. Expects USD/CNY at 6.82 at end of 3Q vs CNY6.77 previous forecast, 6.80 year-end vs 6.70 previous call. Pair last at 6.8275 vs 6.8277 Friday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0509 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield last up 0.5 bp at 1.240% as investors taking cue from somewhat-stable Japanese share prices with Nikkei Stock Average falling only 0.3% as risk low that Greece's fiscal issues will worsen immediately, says Mizuho Securities chief market analyst Tetsuya Miura. Adds, investors piled up bond holdings rapidly last week, so they are likely to sell some of them to adjust their portfolio, he says. "If the Nikkei recovers to trade above 10,000, the benchmark 10-year JGB yield could rise above 1.250%," he says. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0500 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore April CPI +3.2% on year vs +1.6% in March; consumer prices rise faster than analyst estimates, on higher housing, transport costs. Dow Jones poll of 7 analysts predicted April CPI +2.6%. Index +0.5% on month, seasonally adjusted terms, vs +0.4% March, +0.3% poll. Transport costs +13.4% on year on higher car, fuel prices; housing +2.9% on electricity tariffs, lower rebates on services. Higher than expected inflation unlikely to move MAS, which tightened policy last month by raising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading band, allowing SGD to appreciate. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0500 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi +0.7% at 1610.78 in modest volume, with banks, techs, shipbuilders leading gains on bargain-buying after last week's big falls. "Stocks are on a technical rebound after the global and Korean markets fell sharply last week, reflecting much of worries over a possible double-dip in the world economy and heightened geopolitical risk (on the Korean peninsula)," says Bae Sung-young at \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2 . Adds, "investors will likely buy whenever the Kospi dips below 1600, although it looks hard for the Kospi to make a meaningful rebound in the near term." Kospi eyed in 1580-1620 band for rest today. KB Financial (105560.SE) +1.9% at KRW50,400, \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) +0.8% at KRW762,000, \uc2 Hyundai Heavy\uc2  (009540.SE) +5.2% at KRW211,000. But autos remain weak with \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) down 0.7% at KRW138,500. (soo-kyung.seo@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0450 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield last at +1 bp at 1.245%, may rise to 1.250% today as there hasn't been major development in Greece fiscal issues over weekend, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  Global Markets Japan chief strategist Kazuhiko Sano. "It's difficult to justify buying of bonds with the 10-year yield below 2.0% without further worsening in the Greece issue," he says. But issue likely to linger longer-term, thus risk rising that 10-year yield could fall toward 1.155%.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0446 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.6% at 19,657.88 midday, reversing earlier losses (intraday low at 19,470.28), as sharp gains in China markets lend support (Shanghai Composite +3.1%) on hopes Chinese authorities will take cautious stance toward tightening near-term. Taifook thinks most local stocks "are grossly oversold and are compelling for their strong business positioning." China property stocks sharply higher on bargain-buying, with China Overseas (0688.HK) +5.4% at HK$15.52, \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) +7.5% at HK$15.00. HK property stocks mixed ahead of land auction later today, with Cheung Kong (0001.HK) down 0.3% at HK$89.35, \uc2 Sino Land\uc2  (0083.HK) +0.9% at HK$12.96; investors not expecting strong response from today's auction after disappointing Tung Chung site sales results 2 weeks ago. Volume modest at HK$38.07 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0439 GMT [Dow Jones] Rebound in Singapore shares enables STI to close technical gap of 2718-2735 formed late last week. Benchmark +1.4% at 2740.14 midday, but expected to face resistance at 2788 (May 20 high). Gainers outnumber decliners 2 to 1 in broad market, but overall participation low with 586.3 million shares traded. "The issues in Europe, while bad, (are) overblown. It is not the end of the world and it wouldn't derail global growth. The current weakness, in my view, is a correction, and not the start of a bear market," says DMG research head Terence Wong. Best performers among STI components include Noble Group (N21.SG) +3.6% at S$1.75, \uc2 Hongkong Land\uc2  (H78.SG), +4.1% at US$4.87, Wilmar (F34.SG), +3.4% at S$5.73, \uc2 CapitaLand\uc2  (C31.SG) +2.9% at S$3.61. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0439 GMT [Dow Jones] Given upside surprise in India government's revenue intake from 3G auctions, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says house forecast of 4.5% central government fiscal deficit "back on track." Notes, government provisionally stands to gain over INR677.2 billion, 90% above INR350 billion target. Broadband wireless access auction also likely to generate more than INR150 billion, almost three times base price. "We also believe that the non-tax revenue intake of the government could potentially increase by INR500 billion, indicating an increased upside for total revenue. The incremental revenue provides a cushion for the government to reduce its projected fiscal deficit (INR3814 billion) by more than 8.0% and potentially lower its bond borrowing programme in 2H FY11." Medium term, house sanguine on bond supply concerns due to demand from banks, life insurance cos, potentially lower issuance post 3G. Adds, "media reports about increases in FII investment limits in government bonds are also supportive of bonds in the medium term." Tips 10-year yield in 7.50-8.15% range during quarter vs 7.42% now. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com) [ 05-24-10 0129ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100524e65o00044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
