maker Astra (ASII.JK), Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK), heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) may lead falls on foreign selling. Foreign funds yesterday net sellers of IDR860 billion worth of shares. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds up slightly amid rising appetite for less risky assets after steep falls on Wall Street overnight. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 124.80 vs 124.79 last close; upside likely limited given concerns over further credit-curbing measures after Shanghai Securities News report that banks extended around CNY700 billion worth of new loans last month, up from CNY510.7 billion in March, says Shanghai-based analyst with local brokerage house. "If the reported loan figure is true, new yuan loans totalled CNY3.3 trillion in the first 4 months, or 44% of Beijing's full-year target of CNY7.5 trillion. The credit expansion pace is still too fast." (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones]USD/KRW off highs as exporters sell while euro coming off overnight lows helps support sentiment for risk sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders say. But expect overall upward bias in pair to remain amid broad risk aversion, Kospi's 2.8% fall. Foreign bank trader tips pair in 1,160-1,170 range rest of session; "offshore players are continuously picking up the dollar at lower levels." But adds hard to expect pair to rise beyond 1,170 level. "I think the market is somewhat shrugging off the (initial) panic we had," on euro-zone debt woes, sharp Wall Street losses. Pair still trading sharply higher at 1,163.9 vs 1,141.3 late yesterday in Seoul, but off earlier 1,169.5 high. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0159 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds rise as Taiex down 1.7% and on buying in 5-year note by securities houses, says local bank trader. Adds volatility in global stock markets due to eurozone debt woes turning investors increasingly risk-averse; "I believe the eurozone problems will drag on for quite a long time...it isn't something that can be solved overnight." Notes if local shares fall more in coming weeks, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may push back timing for monetary policy tightening; "I would say the bond market has definitely turned more bullish recently." Most active 5-year yield at 0.9674% vs 0.9946% last close, and tipped in 0.9500%-0.9800% band; 10-year yield at 1.4300% vs 1.4390% and tipped at 1.4200%-1.4400%. (charmian.kok@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0159 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals lower across board after Wall Street's sharp losses but reaction could be overdone says Jonathan Barrett, managing director at Commodity Broking Services in Sydney. "People are factoring in a disaster scenario. If that contagion fear (of Greece fiscal crisis spreading to other European countries) is real why did European markets only fall 1% to 1.5%?" Barrett says error in Wall Street trading which saw intraday plunge and recovery of around 400 points in the space of minutes should be ignored. "It's an error, that's all." Doesn't expect any immediate recovery but notes that non-fram payrolls expectations for tonight are mostly positive. "The fear factor has taken over so a lot of the metals are at the wrong value," Says not ready to cover short on base metals, put new short on gold at $1,204 earlier. Spot gold at $1,201.30/oz, down $7.50 since NY close. LME 3-month copper at $6,825/ton, down $123, but off intraday low of $6,760 with very heavy volume of 2,460 lots traded. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares are likely to fall with investors selling across the board after weakness in global markets. Markets being hammered by lots of negatives and "nobody knows the direction it will take in the coming days," says Hochiminh City-based trader. Tips key index in 535-540 range today after ending +0.3% at 549.51 yesterday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [Dow Jones] GBP/USD should gain, possibly enjoy a relief rally as high as 1.5500 in near term if it becomes clear Tories can form a government in the U.K., but pair will sell off from those levels, says \uc2 Westpac\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Robert Rennie. Closely watched exit poll shows U.K. opposition Conservative Party looks set to win the most seats in Thursday's general election, but fall short of majority, but early results have thrown up surprises suggesting outcome remains uncertain. "I like a bit of upside in the GBP in the very short term." Says profit takers will jump on any test of 1.5500. Last 1.4817. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open down led by metal firms, banks on domestic tightening concerns, tracking falls in U.S., regional stocks. Shanghai Composite Index last down 1.8% at 2689.60, support pegged at psychological 2600 level. "There is little to be bullish about, with a monetary tightening environment and new share offerings...investors would have to look ahead to next week's economic data for possible bright spots," says Zhang Gang from Central China Securities. Among actives, Baoshan Iron & Steel (600048.SH) down 4.0% at CNY6.18, Yunnan Copper (000878.SZ) also down 4.0% at CNY21.20, \uc2 China Construction Bank Corp\uc2 . (601939.SH) off 2.4% at CNY4.97, \uc2 Ping An Insurance\uc2  (Group) Co. (601318.SH) down 1.6% at CNY45.50. Shenzhen Composite Index down 2.6% at 1060.55, China May stock index futures down 2.3% at 2905.0. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0153 GMT [Dow Jones] Ignore RBA's upgraded inflation, economic growth forecasts, given everything that's gone in last 24 hours those numbers are dated in terms of when \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may hike rates next, says Rory Robertson, IR Strategist with Macquarie. "If the Reserve bank board was sitting this morning, it would struggle to hike rates. There's always been a risk problems in Europe will drag markets down, but those worries seem more real now," says Robertson. "Whatever forecast you had a weak ago, you wouldn't view them with same confidence given events of past day." (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] Neutralize tactical Underweight in Australian resources position, says Southern Cross Equities Director Charlie Aitken. Also recommends buying BHP (BHP.AU) near A$36.50. "Outside of that, we maintain a broadly conservative strategy but will spend the next few weeks trawling through the top-down mess looking for bottom-up value," says Aitken. "I am slowly moving up the risk curve because I believe risk is now more appropriately priced, yet I am obviously aware any bounce will be subdued by the foreign investor view of Australian assets and the fact Canberra will more likely exacerbate the market's frustration as time goes on." BHP last down 0.5% at A$37.41. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metal futures on the Shanghai Futures Exchange fall sharply, tracking broad market retreat. "It's a little mystifying but the main trigger seems to be coming from \uc2 Procter and Gamble\uc2 ," says Wang Zhouyi of Shanghai Cifco Futures. \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2  (PG) declined 2.3%, to 60.75, but fell as low as 39.37 during NY trade overnight on potentially erroneous trade before quickly recovering. Analysts unsure if single specific cause behind financial chaos; factors include fears of slowing growth, debt problems in euro-zone, uncertainty in outcome of U.K. elections. Copper likely to test CNY52,000/ton support, Zhou says. Shanghai benchmark copper last down 3.3% at CNY54,980/ton; zinc down 4.5% at CNY15,710. (chuin-wei.yap@dowjones.com) [ 05-06-10 2204ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100507e65700027\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
