\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: STI +0.4%; Bulls Still In Control - CIMB\b0\par\par\uc2 1,620 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2010\par 03:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0135 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares in broad-based advance, mirroring gains in Japan and South Korea following Wall Street's Friday run-up. STI +0.4% at 3000.42, heading back above 3000 for first time in six sessions, would be higher if not for several stocks trading ex-dividend; resistance tipped at year-to-date high of 3038. "The bulls are still in control," says CIMB; expects STI to eventually close technical gap of 3085-3113 formed in June 2008. Market breadth at 5 gainers for every decliner, with most FTSE ST sub-indexes positive. \f28 \uc2 \f2 CapitaLand\f28 \uc2 \f2  (C31.SG) off 1.8% at S$3.87, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sembcorp Industries\f28 \uc2 \f2  (U96.SG) off 2.3% at S$4.34 as both stocks trade ex-dividend. Lower liners most active on SGX, led by Baker Technology (568.SG), off 7.1% at S$0.525, GMG Global (5IM.SG) flat at S$0.23, Ellipsiz (E13.SG) +9.4% at S$0.175. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0134 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB lower at 32.15 vs 32.21 late Friday in moderate trade, in line with general soft USD tone vs Asians. Market keeping eye on domestic politics, but no impact so far today given absence of violence, major changes over weekend in Bangkok. "We still put some weight on that but at this stage we haven't seen any new developments. Apart from that we base everything on external factors," says Bangkok-based dealer. Tips BOT to step in to stem THB rise if pair drifts down to test 32.05. Range likely 32.05-32.30. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] Fixing China's looming water crisis can be profitable, but government's focus on increasing water supply with megaprojects, such as multibillion-dollar network of canals being built to divert water to the north, offers a poor return on investment, consultancy \uc2 McKinsey & Co\uc2 . says in new report. Instead of spending as much as an estimated CNY500 billion on South-to-North Water Transfer Project, McKinsey says China should invest in things like patching up leaky municipal water pipes, managing irrigation better, recycling more industrial-use water. McKinsey argues in cost-curve analyses that water conservation and recycling projects could bring in CNY131 billion in annual profits. Says rates of return on some solutions "represent attractive investment opportunities for the private sector," said Martin Joerss, a partner at the company who led the study. See: =WSJ BLOG/China Real Time Report: The Water (Profit) Margin (shai.oster@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [Dow Jones] Some emerging market economies may need to build up their reserves further to counter surging liquidity and capital flows, Standard Chartered Bank's chief economist Gerard Lyons says in paper. Notes surging inflows threatening fragile economic recovery as they create bubbles in equity, real estate markets, since "many countries do not have the capacity to absorb such inflows." Recommendation on reserves going broadly against IMF argument that reserves are too high in many countries, but Lyons does point that in some cases like China, sterilization costs are a growing concern, in line with IMF's view. Also says minor, temporary capital controls may help with inflow management. Other measures suggested include greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility (but no reference to CNY and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  widely believed to be undervalued), broadening of local capital markets, macro-prudential steps aimed at curbing house and property prices, counter-cyclical fiscal policy and encouraging capital outflows to offset inflows. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] June Nikkei 225 futures up 1.8% at 11,150 on Wall Street's rally and easing worries over Greece's debt problems, says Investrust CEO Hiroyuki Fukunaga. Says contract may remain solid for rest of session today on improving sentiment, with key technical resistance for this week likely around 11,270; adds, traders will be watching Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) and Bank of Japan (BOJ) meetings this week, while U.S. housing data also possible catalyst. (juro.osawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] Closure of European airspace to have minimal impact on Thai exports, with exports to EU constituting only 11% of country's 2009 exports, says DBS; adds only small amount of these are likely by air. "Assuming this share is a fifth of total exports to EU (it is likely a lot less), the flight delays may have trimmed exports in April by 1% (on month, seasonally adjusted) - minor relative to the impact of moving Chinese New Year holidays on Asian output," house says in note. Seasonally adjusted exports may slip 3% on-month, seasonally adjusted - reflecting normalization in exports, also flight delay impact. But says this translates to non-seasonally adjusted exports of $13.5 billion. Commerce Minister last week said flight disruption, series of public holidays may trim export value to $12 billion in April vs $16.25 billion in March. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY, USD/JPY rise as short-term investors selling safe-haven JPY for EUR, USD, other units, says senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Japan bank. Says after Greek Finance Minister Papaconstantinou expressed confidence at G-20 officials' meeting that country likely to receive financial aid soon, players expect issue to move away from spotlight this week; says helping outlook was Papaconstantinou's noting that Germany had agreed to support in March, allaying some concern over comments from German Finance Minister Wolfgang Schauble that country hasn't decided yet on Greek aid, German Foreign Minister Westerwelle that country "not ready to write a blank check." Adds hedge funds, other short-term investors in Asia "looking more favorably on riskier \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this morning" also as equities strengthen, with Nikkei +1.9%. EUR/JPY at 126.16 vs earlier low at 125.30, USD/JPY at 94.22 vs earlier low 93.85. But says further rises may be capped as Japan exporters, speculators looking to take profits sell units, with USD/JPY possibly capped at 94.50, EUR/JPY at 126.50. Says EUR/USD also helped by improved risk appetite; pair last 1.3393 vs earlier low 1.3317. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may rise tracking gains on Wall Street, also on rebound after 3-session losing streak (down 1.8%) with today's rebound target at around 21,500, says Ernie Hon at ICBC International. Tips crude price strength likely to help support oil stocks, especially upstream plays such as Cnooc (0883.HK), with ADRs ended +1.8% at equivalent of HK$13.81 vs local close of HK$13.58; June crude futures settled +$1.42 at $85.12/barrel on Nymex Friday. But adds, overall trading sentiment remains cautious, on concerns of more tightening measures from China, including RRR hike or interest rate hike, while mainland property stocks likely to remain relatively weak. Suggests buying stocks when index falls below its 50-day moving average (21,066). HSI ended down 1.0% at 21,244.49 Friday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may rise on strength in U.S. stocks Friday, Shanghai Composite Index tipped to consolidate around 3000 after ending down 0.5% at 2983.54, says Jacky Zhang at Capital Securities. "But the Shanghai index's upside is capped as investors are concerned that follow-up property tightening measures will be launched." Economic Observer reports today China plans to launch annual property tax in 1H. Shenzhen index ends down 0.7% at 1204.44. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR near 2-year low of 3.1750 vs 3.1900 late last Friday on strong short-term flows, says dealer with local bank. "There's quite a bit of speculative buying on the ringgit as well as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar," says dealer; adds Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  not spotted. Thinks pair may test 3.1650 in next few days given prevailing strong interest in MYR. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0104 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  may hike policy rate by another 25 bps to 2.50% on May 13, says CIMB Economics Research; thinks policy will likely stay at this level until year-end. "We believe \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  will continue to normalise interest rates at a measured pace so as to ensure that the recovery becomes firmly entrenched," says CIMB; adds further rate hikes would be data dependent as uncertainty still clouds global outlook in 2H10. This, after Malaysia's CPI posted moderate gain of 1.3% on-year in March (+1.2% in February), signalling tame inflationary pressures. "The headline inflation will gradually trend up, albeit moderately, on the back of a firmer domestic demand, the still-firm commodity prices as well as the disappearing of high base effect," says CIMB; keeps this year's CPI estimate at +2.0% on average. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely down, tracking Friday's fall in U.S. Treasurys on better U.S. housing data, economic outlook. "We expect selling across maturities after what happened in the U.S. on Friday," says trader at international bank who tips yields to rise 3-5 bps. Yield on 2-year Singapore bond unchanged at 0.52% Friday, 10-year yield down 1 bp at 2.75%. U.S. Treasurys down Friday on news March new home sales +26.9%. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com) [ 04-25-10 2135ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100426e64q00013\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
