\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/MYR Near 2-Year Low; 3.1650 Support Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2010\par 03:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR near 2-year low of 3.1750 vs 3.1900 late last Friday on strong short-term flows, says dealer with local bank. "There's quite a bit of speculative buying on the ringgit as well as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar," says dealer; adds Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  not spotted. Thinks pair may test 3.1650 in next few days given prevailing strong interest in MYR. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0104 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  may hike policy rate by another 25 bps to 2.50% on May 13, says CIMB Economics Research; thinks policy will likely stay at this level until year-end. "We believe \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  will continue to normalise interest rates at a measured pace so as to ensure that the recovery becomes firmly entrenched," says CIMB; adds further rate hikes would be data dependent as uncertainty still clouds global outlook in 2H10. This, after Malaysia's CPI posted moderate gain of 1.3% on-year in March (+1.2% in February), signalling tame inflationary pressures. "The headline inflation will gradually trend up, albeit moderately, on the back of a firmer domestic demand, the still-firm commodity prices as well as the disappearing of high base effect," says CIMB; keeps this year's CPI estimate at +2.0% on average. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely down, tracking Friday's fall in U.S. Treasurys on better U.S. housing data, economic outlook. "We expect selling across maturities after what happened in the U.S. on Friday," says trader at international bank who tips yields to rise 3-5 bps. Yield on 2-year Singapore bond unchanged at 0.52% Friday, 10-year yield down 1 bp at 2.75%. U.S. Treasurys down Friday on news March new home sales +26.9%. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] G20 officials seeking CNY revaluation took effective approach over weekend in framing issue as beneficial to China, avoiding overt pressure; may get revaluation in next few months, says Shinkin Asset Management senior manager of trading Jun Kato. Instead of ratcheting up pressure on China to let \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise, officials opted for subtler message, with U.S. Treasury Secretary Geithner saying CNY appreciation would be in China's interests. "I think that the G20's pushing of their agenda in this more nuanced way increases the chances of the Chinese authorities moving on the issue in the near-term, probably by around July," Kato says. Says as China "very reluctant to be pressured into action," can now revalue CNY without it appearing to be direct result of outside calls for such move. Says threat of inflation, bubble formation makes "this a good point for China to adjust policy through a widening of the yuan trading band," as any CNY rises would help cool surging economy. Adds, when CNY revaluation comes, may briefly boost JPY, other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but no long-term benefit to JPY expected as "historically yuan rises have not led to any sustained yen strengthening."(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi +1.0% at 1753.67 on broad gains across the board, with local market sentiment buoyed by strong close from U.S. market Friday, eased worries about Greek fiscal problems, positive start from Nikkei. "Overall, the U.S. stocks rose sharply (last Friday) and we're seeing risk premium for Western Europe head lower after Greece requested for the bailout funds," says Taurus Investment & Securities analyst Oh Tae-dong; adds "So long as U.S. stock markets remain stable, Asian markets will likely remain on the uptrend." Korea Life (088350.SE) up 1.8% at KRW9,590 on news of strong Samsung Life IPO pricing Friday; Oh says Korea Life likely benefiting as investors that missed out on Samsung Life likely betting Korea Life will rise along with Samsung. Oh adds, Kospi likely to trade in 1745-1760 range going ahead but says export-related firms like \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE), \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE) and \uc2 Hyundai Motor Co\uc2 . (005380.SE) could suffer should USD/KRW extend declines; pair hit lowest levels in more than 19 months at 1,104.5 earlier Monday vs 1.108.7 late Friday. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0043 GMT [Dow Jones] 10-year cash JGB yield likely to move in 1.295%-1.355% band this week, with futures expected to lead movements in yield curve, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  Global Markets Japan chief strategist Kazuhiko Sano; demand from investors who missed chance to buy 10-year bonds when yield hit around 1.4% will likely drag down 10-year yield to near 1.3%, while need to secure income from JGB holdings ahead of Japan holidays also expected to support JGBs. Lead June JGB futures now down 0.17 at 139.19; 10-year cash bonds yet to change hands (yield at 1.315% at Friday's close). (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0035 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds expected to open tad lower, tracking negative cues from U.S. Treasurys, likely firmer local stocks, says local brokerage trader; adds bond yields will likely remain in range until re-opened 10-year bond starts when-issued trading May 24. Focus this week is auction of re-opened 20-year bond Wednesday, "but this won't impact much (more active) five and ten-year bonds," she says; tips auction result likely around 2.0% vs last close at 1.9775% in when-issued trade. Yield on most-active 5-year bond ends at 1.0023%, tipped to test resistance at 20-day moving average of 1.01%, support at 1.0%; adds yields have limited downside as short-term market rates like repo rate, overnight rate have all been gradually rising as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  slowly tightens liquidity. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0035 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to open higher after U.S. stocks rose to fresh highs Friday, says Randy Chang, trader at KGI Securities. Tipped to trade 8000-8100 band today vs Friday's close +0.3% at 8004.89. Adds electronic plays likely to support Taiex ahead of 1Q results expected from tech heavyweights TSMC (2330.TW), UMC (2303.TW), Hon Hai Precision (2317.TW) this week. "We're pretty bullish on Taiwan shares this week, and expect largely positive earnings results in the next week to provide a catalyst for the Taiex," he says. Tips China plays like car sector, textiles, petrochemicals, to be in focus this week ahead of Shanghai Expo May 1; televised debate between President Ma Ying-jeou, chairman of opposition DPP Ing-wen Tsai about cross-straits economic trade pact yesterday "also helped clarify some doubts about the trade pact, so we may see a fresh round of buying in sectors set to benefit," he says. (charmian.kok@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0033 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD hits 20-month low of 1.3672 tracking Friday's gains in EUR, building up risk appetite after news Greece formally asked for financial aid package, Germany said prepared to help, says trader at local bank; adds, MAS (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) may step in if pair falls very sharply. "I won't be surprised if they (MAS) come in a little bit to soothe the market and if the SGD rises more than the market fundamentals," trader says. Tips immediate support at 1.3640, resistance 1.3740. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0034 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 1.9% at 11,121.22 (intraday high), helped by weaker yen and positive sentiment on receding Greek-debt worries after euro-zone countries and IMF promised to support country over weekend; Nikkei may find resistance at 11,150 today, say market analysts. "Technically, investors generally are ready to push the market higher (after last week's 1.7% fall), while taking cues from Japanese corporate earnings (with several bellwether firms due to report this week)," says Fujio Ando, senior managing director at Chibagin Asset Management. All 33 Topix subindexes up; exporters higher with Canon (7751.TO) up 3.3% at Y4,385, \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) +2.9% at Y3,675. IHI (7013.TO) up 5.0% at Y169 after Nikkei report \uc2 Toshiba\uc2  (6502.TO) and IHI (7013.TO) to form JV this fall. \uc2 Toshiba\uc2  +1.5% at Y540. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0027 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI likely to open higher, taking cue from firmer U.S. stocks, says dealer with local brokerage. "The rebound on Wall Street last Friday, with the benchmark Dow Jones average rising for the longest weekly winning streak in six years, sparked by the biggest gain in new home sales in almost five decades, should spill-over to regional markets and hence boost local sentiment," says TA Research; tips immediate resistance at 1344, previous 2 week's high, then 1347 ( April 7's pivot high), support at 1329, followed by 1320 (50% Fibonacci retracement of 12-day rally from 1292 low of March 22 to April 7's peak of 1347). Thinks glove makers may continue to attract interest given robust earnings outlook while select lower-liners may see rotational interest. KLCI ended down 0.2 point at 1336.78 Friday. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0025 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW hits lowest levels in more than 19 months at 1,104.5 vs 1,108.7 late Friday in Seoul as KRW demand robust due to Kospi's early 1.0% gain, analysts say. "A short dollar bias still dominates the market with a general understanding that the pair is headed for a test of the key 1,100 support some time this week," says Woori Futures analyst Byeon Ji-young. Notes, various factors likely to keep KRW solid including anticipated exporter USD sales for month end settlements, news late last week that Samsung Life to raise $4.4 billion via IPO. Pegs support at 1,102, with downside cushioned by possibility of USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by local \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com) [ 04-25-10 2106ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100426e64q0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
