\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/SGD Lower At 20-Month Low; MAS May  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2010\par 02:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0033 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD hits 20-month low of 1.3672 tracking Friday's gains in EUR, building up risk appetite after news Greece formally asked for financial aid package, Germany said prepared to help, says trader at local bank; adds, MAS (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) may step in if pair falls very sharply. "I won't be surprised if they (MAS) come in a little bit to soothe the market and if the SGD rises more than the market fundamentals," trader says. Tips immediate support at 1.3640, resistance 1.3740. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0034 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 1.9% at 11,121.22 (intraday high), helped by weaker yen and positive sentiment on receding Greek-debt worries after euro-zone countries and IMF promised to support country over weekend; Nikkei may find resistance at 11,150 today, say market analysts. "Technically, investors generally are ready to push the market higher (after last week's 1.7% fall), while taking cues from Japanese corporate earnings (with several bellwether firms due to report this week)," says Fujio Ando, senior managing director at Chibagin Asset Management. All 33 Topix subindexes up; exporters higher with Canon (7751.TO) up 3.3% at Y4,385, \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) +2.9% at Y3,675. IHI (7013.TO) up 5.0% at Y169 after Nikkei report \uc2 Toshiba\uc2  (6502.TO) and IHI (7013.TO) to form JV this fall. \uc2 Toshiba\uc2  +1.5% at Y540. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0027 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI likely to open higher, taking cue from firmer U.S. stocks, says dealer with local brokerage. "The rebound on Wall Street last Friday, with the benchmark Dow Jones average rising for the longest weekly winning streak in six years, sparked by the biggest gain in new home sales in almost five decades, should spill-over to regional markets and hence boost local sentiment," says TA Research; tips immediate resistance at 1344, previous 2 week's high, then 1347 ( April 7's pivot high), support at 1329, followed by 1320 (50% Fibonacci retracement of 12-day rally from 1292 low of March 22 to April 7's peak of 1347). Thinks glove makers may continue to attract interest given robust earnings outlook while select lower-liners may see rotational interest. KLCI ended down 0.2 point at 1336.78 Friday. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0025 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW hits lowest levels in more than 19 months at 1,104.5 vs 1,108.7 late Friday in Seoul as KRW demand robust due to Kospi's early 1.0% gain, analysts say. "A short dollar bias still dominates the market with a general understanding that the pair is headed for a test of the key 1,100 support some time this week," says Woori Futures analyst Byeon Ji-young. Notes, various factors likely to keep KRW solid including anticipated exporter USD sales for month end settlements, news late last week that Samsung Life to raise $4.4 billion via IPO. Pegs support at 1,102, with downside cushioned by possibility of USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by local \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0022 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR lower at 3.1850 vs 3.1900 late Friday, tracking USD/Asians, says dealer with local brokerage. "People are re-looking higher risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Greek government's request for financial aid helped allay concerns over a Greek sovereign debt default." Thinks USD/MYR will be well-supported at 3.1800, if breaks, then pair may test 3.1650. Believes developments in Europe will continue to dictate flows on local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  front; tips resistance at 3.1950. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0019 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to stay stable around 6.8260, vs 6.8263 last close on OTC, after PBOC says Friday conditions for China's external trade worsening due to rising protectionism, other factors; comments indicate Beijing unlikely to allow CNY to appreciate in near term, as that could further hurt exporters, says Shenzhen-based trader at local bank. Expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will set central parity in 6.8260-6.8265 range vs Friday's 6.8264. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0019 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead June JGB futures open lower, following Friday's weakness in U.S. Treasurys, Nikkei rebound (last +1.5%); contract currently down 0.11 at 139.25. If stock rises push USD/JPY to near Y95 (vs last at Y94.26), futures and 10-year zone could weaken, says Deutsche Securities strategist Makoto Yamashita. Adds long-term JGB yield likely to be biased upward this week, with focus on market movements after FOMC outcome due coming Wednesday. 10-year cash bonds yet to change hands (1.315% at Friday's close). (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0017 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to trend lower on continued expectations for CNY appreciation, likely firmer local stocks, weaker USD/Asians, says foreign bank trader. "But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hopes the Taiwan dollar won't rise too quickly," which will keep pair in range, he adds. Pair at session-high 31.460 late Friday after suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support pulls pair off intraday low of 31.325 late in session; notes "heavy" USD selling interest at 31.500 by local banks, exporters, which will likely cap USD there, tips support at 31.300. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0011 GMT [Dow Jones]NZX-50 +0.3% at 3311.899, likely to drift sideways as volumes remain light due to lack of corporate news, Australia on holiday, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  institutional broker David Price; "the market has reached a point where it's rushed up pretty much one way and it now really needs an earnings push to justify going any higher." \uc2 Auckland International Airport\uc2  (AIA.NZ) +0.5% at NZ$2.02 after it said international, domestic passenger volumes rose in March on-year; "we've got a relatively positive outlook for tourism and this has been borne out in the passenger numbers," Price noted. NZX (NZX.NZ) +1.1% at NZ$1.82 after it said 56% of shareholders elected to receive their dividend as bonus shares.(lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0006 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks may get boost from easing concerns over Greece's debt problems, signs U.S. housing market stabilizing based on stronger-than-expected March new home sales. STI may head above 3000 for first time in six sessions, with resistance at year-to-date high of 3038; last week's low of 2943 likely to offer support. Benchmark closed +0.3% at 2988.49 Friday. "With Greece approaching the IMF and the EU for help with its debts, concerns of a default should ease. The positive U.S. home sales may also help sentiment. But whether all this is enough to encourage buying in a big way remains to be seen," says trader at local brokerage, noting low participation in recent sessions with daily volume below 2 billion shares. \uc2 Sembcorp Industries\uc2  (U96.SG) may buck expected gains by other blue chips as stock trades ex-dividend today; closed +3.3% at S$4.44. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2356 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds likely steady, with March consumer price index or CPI seen to have muted impact, says dealer with local bank. This, after Malaysia March CPI +1.3% on-year vs February's +1.2%, lower than median +1.4% forecast by 14 economists polled by Dow Jones. "The March CPI, albeit slightly higher than February, isn't unexpected as it is the result of the normalisation of the base effect," says dealer; doesn't see March CPI having much impact on monetary policy; adds investors likely want more cues before deciding on next course of action. Last Friday, May 2013 MGS flat at 2.96%, August 2015 MGS down 7 bps at 3.50%, September 2017 MGS down 1 bp at 3.80% and November 2019 MGS down 1 bp at 4.04%.(elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2355 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi may open higher after ending down 0.1% at 1737.03 Friday on positive cues from Wall Street, but market likely to see relatively weak bounce with majority of big earnings results over and \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2 ' (005930.SE) earnings due April 30. MiraeAsset Securities analyst Lee Jin-woo says market likely to continue testing 1,750 resistance but doesn't expect sharp gains, adding market may take a breather; "right now the market sentiments are positive, but for now we're more likely to see the market test the previous highs as opposed to strong gains." Life insurance firms Korea Life (088350.SE), Tong Yang Life (082640.SE) could be boosted after Samsung Life's impending IPO priced at KRW110,000 - near top end of indicative range. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com) [ 04-25-10 2033ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100426e64q0000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
