\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,142 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2010\par 10:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE CENTRAL BANK INTERVENES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  BRIEFLY TO BOOST US DOLLAR -SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wednesday \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to boost the U.S. dollar, a person familiar with the situation said. (published 0851 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN BANK SHARES MUTED ON IMF TAXES PROPOSAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares of banks across Europe gave a muted reaction Wednesday to an International Monetary Fund proposal to double-tax financial institutions to prevent and pay for future crises. (published 0733 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN FLIGHTS RESUME, BUT NORMALITY WEEKS AWAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Flights were resuming across Europe Wednesday as air space mostly reopened following a reappraisal of the risks of the volcanic ash cloud that had severely interrupted flights for the past six days, but airlines said it would take weeks for all the stranded passengers to get home and for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to return to normal. (published 0733 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GREECE TO BEGIN AID TALKS WITH EUROPE, IMF OFFICIALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greece is set to begin talks Wednesday with a delegation of international financial officials amid apparent divisions within the Greek government on whether--and when--to ask for financial aid. (published 0746 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ SEES LIGHT AT END OF DEFLATION TUNNEL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SENDAI, Japan (Dow Jones)--A top Bank of Japan official Wednesday declared that "beams of light" were visible at the end of the deflation tunnel, the most optimistic comment so far from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0856 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN MULLING DEFICIT-CUTTING TARGETS FOR FISCAL REFORM BILL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government is considering a variety of new numerical targets to reduce its massive debt, including achieving a primary budget balance and decreasing the deficit-to-output ratio, government officials familiar with the matter said. (published 0746 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN MOF UPS VIEW ON REGIONAL ECONOMIES IN QUARTERLY REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  raised its view of the nation's regional economies for the first time in two quarters, as industrial output picked up due to strong overseas demand and the effect of the government's stimulus measures. (published 0230 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ NISHIMURA: BOJ FRAMEWORK FOR PRICE VIEW BEST FOR NOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Deputy Gov. Kiyohiko Nishimura said Wednesday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's current framework for its outlook on prices is the appropriate one for now, ruling out the government's call to seek an inflation rate above 1%. (published 0624 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA MARCH METAL IMPORTS UP BUT POLICY CURBS CLOUD OUTLOOK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Robust commodity imports in March continued to bolster bullish projections for the strength of Chinese underlying demand, but market attention is shifting to the government's crackdown on bubble-territory property prices, which may suppress trade appetites in coming months. (published 0828 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RBI GOKARN: WILL RAISE CASH RESERVE RATIO IF EXCESS LIQUIDITY IN SYSTEM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  will further increase banks' cash reserve requirements if there is excess liquidity in the banking system, a deputy governor said Wednesday. (published 0558 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ SURVEY SHOWS INDIVIDUAL DEMAND FOR LOANS RECOVERING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese individuals' demand for bank loans recovered in the first quarter of the year as lending rates came down, according to Bank of Japan survey results released Wednesday, a sign that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's super-easy policy is helping support private demand. (published 0331 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIAN ECONOMY SET FOR GROWTH SURGE IN 2010 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A leading index of Australian economic activity grew at its fastest pace in 13 years in February, signaling that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  may need to keep tapping on the monetary policy brakes, taking policy to a restrictive stance by year end. (published 0401 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA LAST FY DIRECT TAX REVENUE LIKELY INR70 BLN LOWER THAN AIM - OFFICIAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India is likely to fall short of its direct tax revenue aim by INR70 billion for the just-ended fiscal year, the chairman of the Central Board of Direct Taxes said Wednesday. (published 0540 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA MINISTRY: CHINA FINANCING TO VENEZUELA INCLUDES YUAN FUNDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Half of the US$20 billion in financing to Venezuela pledged by China Development Bank Corp. is denominated in yuan, Foreign Ministry spokeswoman Jiang Yu said Tuesday. (published 0322 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA APRIL TRADE SURPLUS LIKELY BIGGER THAN MARCH'S $1.82 BLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's trade surplus in April will likely be bigger than the preceding month's $1.82 billion, Korea Customs Service said Wednesday. (published 0150 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IRISH REGULATOR: BANKS ON PATH TO BEING STRONGLY CAPITALIZED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ireland's head of financial regulation, Matthew Elderfield, said Wednesday the country has developed a credible path to a "strongly capitalized" banking sector. (published 0830 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INVESTORS BUY UP NAKHEEL'S $980M SUKUK, EXPECTING REPAYMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are betting \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dubai World\f28 \uc2 \f2  will pay off $980 million sukuk issued by its property arm Nakheel to avoid a default as it negotiates a debt restructuring proposal with creditors worth about $24 billion. (published 0523 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS: POLITICAL HOPE KEEPS STERLING RESILIENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling continues to resist a strong political downdraft, possibly on hopes that all will come right in the end. (published 0815 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: EUR/USD TO EXTEND WEAKNESS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: The euro continues to weaken following Wednesday's push into fresh 8-day lows below 1.3416, and Tuesday's bull failure at 1.3523 brings the 1.3366 higher low into the frame. However, scope has been created for downside to test the Apr. 8 higher low at 1.3282. (published 0624 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: BONDS TO EDGE LOWER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Key support at 123.31/36 is set for a retest as the setback off Monday's contract high at 123.88 looks likely to extend. Below 123.31 would leave a near-term bull failure at 123.88 and prompt further weakness towards the Apr. 15 higher low at 122.87. (published 0631 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: HK CONSUMER PRICE GROWTH LIKELY SLOWED IN MARCH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's consumer prices likely rose at a slower pace in March than in February, when the Lunar New Year holiday boosted prices, but upward pressure remains because of higher food and commodity prices globally, economists said. (published 0342 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: MALAYSIA CONSUMER PRICES LIKELY ROSE 1.4% IN MARCH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's consumer price index likely rose in March due to higher food prices and a slight increase in transportation costs, economists said. (published 0554 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OPTIONS: DOLLAR/YEN OPTIONS UP ON SPOT'S EARLIER FALL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar/yen options rose slightly in Asia Wednesday as a modest fall in the underlying exchange rate encouraged some players to pick up hedges against any further declines. (published 0328 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MONEY TALKS: THAI MARKETS CALM DOWN BUT FUNDS WILL LOOK LONG TERM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand is not the new Indonesia but the speed with which the country's financial markets have calmed down over the recent political upheaval is encouraging. (published 0536 GMT) [ 21-04-10 0801GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100421e64l000ox\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
