\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Singapore Dollar Will Head Higher, But Slowly\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Roman   \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \par \uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2010\par 06:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar has soared since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  switched to an appreciation policy for the first time in almost two years, but precedent suggests the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't let it run too far, too fast.\par \par \uc2 That means anyone expecting the Singapore dollar to lead the charge higher in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be disappointed.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar has climbed over two cents against the greenback since the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore tightened policy Wednesday, a huge leap for a normally mild-mannered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which the MAS manages within an undisclosed band against a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket.\par \par \uc2 The rally spooked policymakers. A person familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking told Dow Jones Newswires Thursday that MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible if the U.S. dollar "falls well below" S$1.3700 in coming days. It was last at S$1.3776.\par \par \uc2 The MAS dropped its neutral policy stance because the city-state's recovery has turned red hot. The economy expanded at its fastest pace on record in the first quarter and the government raised its growth and inflation forecasts for this year. Wednesday's tightening was unprecedented: never before has the MAS both revalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and shifted the slope of its target band toward appreciation.\par \par \uc2 The aggressive move let Singapore dollar bulls loose: strategists tripped over themselves issuing reports predicting a sharper rally in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the MAS doesn't appear to be looking for a much stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at least for now, since inflation--at 1% in March--remains under control.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to allow the U.S. dollar to drop below S$1.35 in coming even months, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  as needed to ensure the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar gains are "modest and gradual" in line with its statement Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "The basket peg, by definition, is a constraint for the Singapore dollar," said Tim Condon, a strategist with ING Bank.\par \par \uc2 Condon expects the U.S. dollar may drop to S$1.35, but only in about three months. That would still keep the Singapore dollar--which is up 2.3% against the greenback this year--as an underperformer compared to high-fliers like the Malaysian ringgit, which has gained 7%, and the Korean won, up 4.7%.\par \par \uc2 Standard Chartered analysts reckon the MAS may allow a 2% annual gain of the Singapore dollar against the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket, which would provide additional upside only if \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the ringgit and Chinese yuan, believed to be key basket components, gain substantial ground.\par \par \uc2 StanChart Wednesday upgraded its rating for the Singapore dollar to overweight from neutral, but anticipates the greenback will only slide to S$1.35 by the end of June, and then to S$1.33 by the end of September.\par \par \uc2 ING and StanChart assume in their forecasts that China, one of Singapore's major trading partners, will let the yuan rise against the dollar by the end of June. That means the Singapore dollar, unlike other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would get no additional boost if Beijing makes such a move, Condon said.\par \par \uc2 "This was a pre-emptive move, we believe, ahead of China moving on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Condon said. "I think a stronger yuan is already included in (the Singapore dollar) price."\par \par \uc2 (David Roman, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reporter, has been a Dow Jones correspondent since 2002, covering economic and corporate news. He can be reached at 65 6415 4045 or by e-mail: david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 16-04-10 0427GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4258 5095   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100416e64g0009n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
