\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,126 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2010\par 09:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB'S SMAGHI: VAGUE STATEMENTS ON GREECE NOT SUFFICIENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European officials underestimated the need for concrete action to help Greece, and their "vague statements" that the country would solve its own problems weren't helpful, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board member Lorenzo Bini Smaghi said Thursday. (published 0758 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UK CONSUMER CONFIDENCE WEAKENED IN MARCH ON ECON, JOBS OUTLOOK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. consumer confidence weakened significantly in March as people became less optimistic about the outlook for the economy over the next six months, and more pessimistic about the outlook for the jobs market. (published 0001 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN MARCH TAX REVENUE FELL 3.7% ON YEAR - HANDELSBLATT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German tax revenue fell an annual 3.7% in March, Handelsblatt newspaper reported Thursday, citing the finance ministry. (published 0638 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA 1ST-QUARTER GDP GREW 11.9%; TIGHTENING URGED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's economy grew a brisk 11.9% from a year earlier in the first quarter of 2010, the government said Thursday, indicating a continued strong recovery and prompting economists to urge further tightening measures. (published 0325 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA WPI UP 9.90% ON YEAR IN MARCH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's wholesale inflation rate rose a tad to 9.90% in March from February's 9.89%, the government said Monday. (published 0631 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA PRIMARY ARTICLES INDEX UP 1.0% TO 288.0 IN WEEK TO APRIL 3 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The wholesale price index for primary articles in India rose 1.0% to 288.0 in the week to April 3 from 285.1 in the previous week, showed data issued Thursday by the Ministry of Commerce and Industry. (published 0640 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA FINANCE MINISTER: EXPECTED MARCH WPI TO TOUCH DOUBLE DIGITS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's Finance Minister, Pranab Mukherjee, had expected the wholesale price index-based inflation rate to touch double digits in March, the minister said Thursday. (published 0641 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S&P: IMPACT OF CORPORATE TAX CUT ON TAIWAN FISCAL BALANCE UNCERTAIN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --A significant increase in Taiwan's debt burden could pressure its credit rating, but it is too early to say how a proposed cut in the island's corporate tax rate would affect the government's financial health, an analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ratings Services said. (published 0640 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MAS MAY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IF US DOLLAR FALLS 'WELL BELOW' S$1.37 - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore was surprised at how much the local dollar rose after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tightened policy Wednesday, and the MAS may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  keeps rising, a person familiar with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's thinking said Thursday. (published 0535 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA OFFICIAL: ROOM TO SOON HIKE REPO, REVERSE REPO RATES BY UP TO 50 BPS EACH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has room to soon raise its overnight lending, or repo, rate and overnight borrowing, or reverse repo, rate by up to 50 basis points each, a senior government official said Thursday. (published 0506 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA: INVESTMENT CONTRIBUTION STILL BIGGER SHARE OF GDP GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investment continued to be the largest contributor to China's economic growth in the first quarter, and the contribution from consumption remained stable, Li Xiaochao, spokesman for the National Bureau of Statistics, said Thursday. (published 0339 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA: MARCH TRADE DEFICIT SHOWS STRONG DOMESTIC DEMAND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's trade deficit in March indicates strong domestic demand and good recovery momentum for the country's economy, National Bureau of Statistics spokesman Li Xiaochao said Thursday. (published 0317 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA COMMERCE MINISTRY: EXPECT 2010 TRADE SURPLUS TO SHRINK $100 BLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China expects its trade surplus to shrink by another $100 billion this year, with monthly trade deficits likely for the rest of the first half of 2010, Ministry of Commerce spokesman Yao Jian said Thursday. (published 0336 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par KOREA VICE FINANCE MINISTER: RISK FROM HASTY RATE HIKE, WON RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A hasty interest-rate hike by the Bank of Korea or an excessive rise in the won would be two key risks to South Korea's economic recovery, Vice Finance Minister Hur Kyung-wook said Thursday. (published 0155 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par KOREA 1Q GDP GROWTH LIKELY MUCH FASTER THAN EXPECTED -OFFICIAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's economy is likely to have grown "much faster" in the first quarter than the government earlier expected, probably matching the Bank of Korea's recently upgraded forecast, an official at the Ministry of Strategy and Finance said Thursday. (published 0227 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SINGAPORE PM: STRONG GDP GROWTH WON'T CONTINUE INDEFINITELY - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong said the island-state's robust economic growth is unlikely to continue indefinitely and that the government will introduce more measures to curb speculation in the housing market if needed, The Business Times reported Thursday. (published 0014 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MALAYSIA THINK TANK MIER UPS 2010 GDP GROWTH FORECAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Malaysian Institute of Economic Research said Thursday it could be difficult for Malaysia to attain developed country status by 2020, even as it raised the country's 2010 economic growth forecast to 5.2% from 3.7%. (published 0502 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par =EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: STERLING TO PROBE HIGHER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: Tuesday's rally off 1.3545 remains beneath the week's high at 1.3692, and a lateral consolidation pattern is taking place which suggests more drifting between those two levels. Should a break above 1.3679 be achieved, the skew would favour more upside pressure on 1.3692, and create room for congested resistance at 1.3819. Loss of the 1.3595 higher low would prompt corrective weakness towards 1.3545. (published 0630 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MONEY TALKS: CHINA INFLATION DATA MAY CREATE (A BIT OF) POLICY SPACE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has posted reassuringly-solid gross domestic product figures for the first quarter, but the more interesting read comes from inflation. (published 0553 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : DOLLAR, EURO TICK UP VS YEN ON HIGHER ASIA STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar and euro ticked up against the yen in Asia Thursday as higher regional stocks encouraged non-Japanese hedge funds to buy the two \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the safe-haven yen, while Japanese importers also picked up the dollar and euro on a regular settlement day. (published 0556 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OPTIONS: DOLLAR/YEN OPTIONS DOWN AS SPOT STAYS IN TIGHT RANGE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar/yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  option prices fell in Tokyo Thursday as investors trimmed bets against swings in the U.S. unit, which held to a thin trading band against its Japanese counterpart. (published 0312 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S BUDGET MAY PROJECT ROBUST FISCAL OUTLOOK - ECONOMISTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's budget will likely return to surplus much sooner than the government has previously forecast and an expected quick pace of economic growth means the government should seek to rein in spending, analysts said in a preview of next month's annual budget. (published 0317 GMT) [ 15-04-10 0703GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100415e64f000k1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
