\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,195 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2010\par 08:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: CHINA 1ST-QUARTER GDP GREW 11.9%; TIGHTENING URGED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's economy grew a brisk 11.9% from a year earlier in the first quarter of 2010, the government said Thursday, indicating a continued strong recovery and prompting economists to urge further tightening measures. (published 0325 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MAS MAY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IF US DOLLAR FALLS 'WELL BELOW' S$1.37 - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore was surprised at how much the local dollar rose after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tightened policy Wednesday, and the MAS may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  keeps rising, a person familiar with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's thinking said Thursday. (published 0535 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDIA OFFICIAL: ROOM TO SOON HIKE REPO, REVERSE REPO RATES BY UP TO 50 BPS EACH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has room to soon raise its overnight lending, or repo, rate and overnight borrowing, or reverse repo, rate by up to 50 basis points each, a senior government official said Thursday. (published 0506 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA: INVESTMENT CONTRIBUTION STILL BIGGER SHARE OF GDP GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investment continued to be the largest contributor to China's economic growth in the first quarter, and the contribution from consumption remained stable, Li Xiaochao, spokesman for the National Bureau of Statistics, said Thursday. (published 0339 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA: MARCH TRADE DEFICIT SHOWS STRONG DOMESTIC DEMAND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's trade deficit in March indicates strong domestic demand and good recovery momentum for the country's economy, National Bureau of Statistics spokesman Li Xiaochao said Thursday. (published 0317 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA COMMERCE MINISTRY: EXPECT 2010 TRADE SURPLUS TO SHRINK $100 BLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China expects its trade surplus to shrink by another $100 billion this year, with monthly trade deficits likely for the rest of the first half of 2010, Ministry of Commerce spokesman Yao Jian said Thursday. (published 0336 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: AUSTRALIAN CONSUMER INFLATIONARY EXPECTATIONS UP IN APRIL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian consumer inflationary expectations rose in April, while unemployment expectations declined, with consumers forecasting recent growth in the local economy will continue, according to surveys by the Melbourne Institute and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac Banking Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . released Thursday. (published 0150 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par KOREA VICE FINANCE MINISTER: RISK FROM HASTY RATE HIKE, WON RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A hasty interest-rate hike by the Bank of Korea or an excessive rise in the won would be two key risks to South Korea's economic recovery, Vice Finance Minister Hur Kyung-wook said Thursday. (published 0155 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par KOREA 1Q GDP GROWTH LIKELY MUCH FASTER THAN EXPECTED -OFFICIAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's economy is likely to have grown "much faster" in the first quarter than the government earlier expected, probably matching the Bank of Korea's recently upgraded forecast, an official at the Ministry of Strategy and Finance said Thursday. (published 0227 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: 5-YEAR JGB SALE STRONGER-THAN-TIPPED AS ZONE CHEAPENS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New five-year Japanese government bonds received stronger-than-expected demand at an auction Thursday, supported by banks' holdings of ample surplus cash and the zone's cheapness due to recent falls in Tokyo interbank offered rates . (published 0523 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDONESIA FINANCE MINISTER: 1Q ON-YEAR ECONOMIC GROWTH AT 5.7%-5.8% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's economy is expected to have expanded by between 5.7% and 5.8% in the first quarter of 2010 from a year earlier, Finance Minister Sri Mulyani Indrawati told reporters Thursday. (published 0221 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SINGAPORE PM: STRONG GDP GROWTH WON'T CONTINUE INDEFINITELY - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong said the island-state's robust economic growth is unlikely to continue indefinitely and that the government will introduce more measures to curb speculation in the housing market if needed, The Business Times reported Thursday. (published 0014 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: MALAYSIA THINK TANK MIER UPS 2010 GDP GROWTH FORECAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Malaysian Institute of Economic Research said Thursday it could be difficult for Malaysia to attain developed country status by 2020, even as it raised the country's 2010 economic growth forecast to 5.2% from 3.7%. (published 0502 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =CITI POLL: 62% OF INVESTORS BULLISH ON CLIMATE IN NEXT 6 MOS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nearly two-thirds of investors expect the investment climate to improve in the next six months, according to a national survey released by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . on Thursday. (published 0401 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN HOME-LOAN ARREARS REMAIN AT LOW LEVELS-S&P \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian home-loan arrears underlying prime residential mortgage-backed securities remained steady at their lowest level in two years in the fourth quarter, according to a survey Thursday by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ratings Service. (published 0430 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: AUSTRALIA'S NSW TCORP PRICES A$1B BENCHMARK BOND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's New South Wales state government Thursday received nearly twice as much in bids as it needed in pricing a new A$1 billion May 2020 government bond, showing the state government is having little problem in establishing a new curve of debt not guaranteed by the federal government. (published 0548 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDONESIA GOVT TO AUCTION IDR5 TLN OF T-BILLS ON APRIL 20 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indonesian government plans to sell IDR5 trillion in treasury bills on April 20, a statement from the finance ministry said Thursday. (published 0500 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA FINANCE MINISTRY TO SELL CNY28 BLN 7-YEAR BONDS WEDNESDAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Thursday it plans to auction CNY28 billion worth of seven-year bonds Wednesday. (published 0317 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDIA EXIM BANK'S $200 MLN BOND OVER 3 TIMES SUBSCRIBED -SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Export-Import Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2 's sale of a $200 million five-year bond late Wednesday was subscribed over three times, according to a person familiar with the deal, reflecting continued investor interest for papers offering higher yields than those in developed markets. (published 0338 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par COUNTRY GARDEN TO PRICE DOLLAR BOND AS EARLY AS THURSDAY -SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bond, which has a maturity of seven years with a call option after four years, will be priced as early as later Thursday, the person said. (published 0400 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: CHINA INFLATION DATA MAY CREATE (A BIT OF) POLICY SPACE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has posted reassuringly-solid gross domestic product figures for the first quarter, but the more interesting read comes from inflation. (published 0553 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : DOLLAR, EURO TICK UP VS YEN ON HIGHER ASIA STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar and euro ticked up against the yen in Asia Thursday as higher regional stocks encouraged non-Japanese hedge funds to buy the two \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the safe-haven yen, while Japanese importers also picked up the dollar and euro on a regular settlement day. (published 0556 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OPTIONS: DOLLAR/YEN OPTIONS DOWN AS SPOT STAYS IN TIGHT RANGE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar/yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  option prices fell in Tokyo Thursday as investors trimmed bets against swings in the U.S. unit, which held to a thin trading band against its Japanese counterpart. (published 0312 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =AUSTRALIA'S BUDGET MAY PROJECT ROBUST FISCAL OUTLOOK - ECONOMISTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's budget will likely return to surplus much sooner than the government has previously forecast and an expected quick pace of economic growth means the government should seek to rein in spending, analysts said in a preview of next month's annual budget. (published 0317 GMT) [ 15-04-10 0601GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100415e64f000eb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict