\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/KRW Off Lows On Suspected BOK  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,693 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2010\par 03:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0110 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW off earlier lows on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early trade, say 2 Seoul-based traders, noting BOK may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  via a local bank, possibly buying USD from around KRW1,121. But despite this, traders don't expect any strong rebound as upside likely capped by foreign flows into local stocks. "It's not that I want to fight the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities or anything but I'm going to stick to my dollar/won short position. Considering various external factors, and with foreign investors buying local stocks for a 19th straight session, a weaker dollar/won is still the right answer," says local bank trader. Adds aiming for breach of 1,120 support may not be easy for today "with (BOK) like this since morning. But if not today, it'll fall tomorrow." Another trader tips 1,118-1,125 range. Pair last tad down at 1,122.4 vs 1,123.2 late yesterday in Seoul, but off earlier 1,120.7 low. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0103 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi flat at 1725.67 in light volume; techs, autos weak on profit-taking but most banks, energy stocks offering support. "As \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  announced its earnings guidance for the first quarter yesterday, impact of earnings expectations that had led the broad market rally seems to have weakened," says Park Seok-hyun at KTB Securities. Still, foreigners extend buying streak to 19 sessions, preventing any sharp falls across board. Size of foreign buying likely key to determine Kospi's direction today, says Park. Expects Kospi to drift around last close of 1726.09. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 0.5% at KRW865,000, Hynix (000660.SE) down 2.1% at KRW27,750, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) down 1.9% at KRW126,500. Shinhan Financial (055550.SE) +0.5% at KRW46,450. SK Energy (096770.SE) +4.2% at KRW125,000 on higher oil prices, earnings hopes. \uc2 Asiana Airlines\uc2  (020560.SE) +7.3% at KRW5,150 as carrier's cargo, passenger traffic sharply increased last month while fears of capital reduction recede thanks to signs \uc2 Kumho Asiana Group\uc2 's restructuring process running smoothly. (soo-kyung.seo@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei +0.1% at 11,297.64% after opening higher, then dipping into negative territory. 17/33 Topix subindexes in positive territory, with buying focused on recent laggards such as banks stocks. USD slight weakening against JPY spurs profit-taking from recent gains in high-tech, auto sector. Players cautious to chase upside with 25-day moving average ratio of gainers/losers at 144.81%, Japanese brokerage trader says (120% level suggests market overheated). Nikkei may gain to around 11,350 level, he adds. MUFG (8306.TO) up 1.8% at Y499. SMFG (8316.TO) +3.7% at Y3,245; Nikkei banking sector +10.5% Feb. 10 to Apr. 6, vs +13.2% for Nikkei over same period. Insurers also higher, led by Tokio Marine's (8766.TO) 2.3% rise. Among exporters, \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) down 2.0% at Y3,445. Panasonic (6752.TO) off 0.3% at Y1,439. Nissan (7201.TO) down 0.6% at Y823. (yoshio.takahashi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0055 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan retail investors likely to sell USD/JPY on any further falls, possibly accelerating any drops, says senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at major Japanese bank. Unlike Japan, overseas institutional investors, Japan retail investors were slow to get behind USD/JPY-buying trend in late March; says "that means that since they wound up buying at higher prices, they will be quicker to sell to lock in profits on any falls." Many retail investors bought USD/JPY only after it had risen to 92.50, dealer says; for this reason, they have been selling after minutes from FOMC March 16 meeting overnight suggested Fed was reluctant to raise rates too soon. USD/JPY 93.75, off earlier lows but still down from intraday high of 93.84.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD lower at 1.3946 vs 1.3959 late Asia Tuesday in line with broad USD weakness vs most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says trader with local bank; but adds, pair unlikely to see big moves given renewed worries on debt in Greece, which has pulled the EUR down. Despite Greek denial of report yesterday that country might seek revision to deal involving IMF aid, investors remain uneasy over unresolved fiscal woes. "SGD usually tracks the euro, so we will not see very big gains," trader says. Adds, participants also unlikely to take big positions ahead of MAS policy meeting due this month. Tips support at 1.3860, resistance at 1.4080.(venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0049 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stock market poised for re-rating after consolidating in 1Q10, driven by improving economic fundamentals, positive employment outlook, reasonable valuations, says Nomura. Notes Singapore economy has rebounded "remarkably quickly" in wake of global crisis, adds impact of macro global risks on Singapore is less pronounced due to country's sound fiscal position. Says Singapore market reasonably priced at 1.7X P/B, 14X P/E, both at long-term averages. Adds dividend yield for market attractive at 3.4% vs 0.5% for 12-month term deposits. Based on current market EPS growth of 12%, tips STI target of 3250 by end-2010. Remains bullish on banks, commodities, conglomerates, but suggests avoiding property developers given risk of more government measures to cool residential market. Also bearish on gaming sector on view analysts too optimistic about size of Singapore's gaming market. STI closed +0.2% at 2975.51 yesterday. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0046 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds may rise on overnight gains in Treasurys, expectations of weak Asian equities market due to lack of fresh leads, weak performance on Wall Street overnight, says trader with foreign bank. "Also, bonds were on a sell-off mode in recent sessions, so some participants might want to get in now as yields look attractive." Expects yields to fall between 2-3 bps. Yesterday, 2-year bond yield +4 bps to close at 0.64%, while 10-year unchanged at 2.89%. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to rise a tad on USD's strength vs EUR overnight, but upside very limited as near-term CNY appreciation expectations remain; central parity eyed 6.8261 vs 6.8260 yesterday; USD/CNY tipped in 6.8250-6.8270 vs 6.8258 last close; "if the central parity is set at below 6.8260, the market may show some reaction (by selling dollars)," says Shanghai-based trader at Japanese bank. "But most people are waiting for economic data out later this week and more clear signals regarding the direction of the yuan." (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to fall after breaking below psychologically important 31.700 level to hit 31.695 late yesterday, weighed by possible widening NDF discount, expected foreign fund inflows to buy local shares, potential selling pressure from exporters to avoid further losses, says foreign trader. "Expectations of strong Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will push the pair further down in the near term." Still, expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in session to stem TWD gains to support island's exports. Tips pair in 31.600-31.700 band for day. (crystal.hsu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 11.1 points, or 0.2%, at 4964.8 in early trade, with modest gains across all sectors bar property trusts, broadly in line with expectations after flat lead from Wall Street. "It's a good, steady start; the rising tide is lifting all boats," says Peter Morgan, senior private client adviser at Patersons in Sydney. Says, "Interest is all over the coal stocks and everyone's kicking themselves for not buying into them earlier," after Macarthur Coal (MCC.AU) rejected improved A$14/share bid from Peabody. MCC down 54 cents at A$14.56 but still well above A$12.00 levels traded before first A$13/share approach. Mirvac's (MGR.AU) planned A$350 million placement at A$1.40/security is driving some weakness across property trusts, says Morgan, as institutions free up some capital for discounted placement. Westfield (WDC.AU) down 7 cents, \uc2 Stockland (SGP.AU) \uc2 down 4 cents, as Morgan says "raising money at this late point in the cycle is disappointing." (bill.lindsay@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0043 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may fall amid absence of fresh leads, bringing an end to a winning streak that lasted 11 days, longest in 16 years. KLCI ended +0.2% at 1344.37 yesterday. "We need some bullish developments to sustain the market's run-up, but newsflow has been rather thin this week. Investors may decide to take some profit at this point," dealer says; pegs immediate support at 1338, with resistance at 1350. Oil and gas-related stocks may continue to be in focus amid strong crude oil prices. "Bullish breakouts on oil and gas stocks such as Dialog (7277.KU) and \uc2 Scomi Group\uc2  (7158.KU) should accelerate further gains," TA Research says. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0041 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares likely to consolidate in narrow band as uncertainties over domestic economic growth remain amid weak exports, growing expectations Beijing will continue to tighten credit control. Shanghai Composite Index is likely to trade around 3150 after ending +0.02% at 3158.68 yesterday, tips resistance at 3200, says Shenyin & Wanguo Securities analyst Li Xiaoxuan; "the increasing supply of new shares from IPO and refunding will continue to curb the performance of large-caps," li says. Real estate developers may remain weak on growing worries of further measures targeting rising housing prices. Shenzhen Index +0.2% at 1237.43.(JIN) [ 04-06-10 2110ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100407e6470000v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
