\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/KRW Lower Late;BOK  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Suspected\b0\par\par\uc2 1,415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2010\par 08:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0711 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW moderately lower at 1,132.6 late Tuesday vs 1,134.7 late yesterday in Seoul amid anticipation of fund inflows related to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hynix Semiconductor\f28 \uc2 \f2  (000660.SE) stake block sale, traders say. But falls limited late in session with four Seoul-based traders suspecting mild USD buying by BOK near KRW1,132. One of those traders estimates suspected buying of around $100 million. Foreign bank trader tips 1,128-1,138 trading range for tomorrow. Adds, fund inflows related to Hynix deal unlikely to have significant impact in market tomorrow as, "I'd expect the selling related to the deal to be around $200 million at the most," which isn't that large. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0702 GMT [Dow Jones] "We see little interest in the SEK at the moment," says Handelsbanken. Notes that, "a bit surprisingly," the AUD, the NZD and the CAD have traded stronger despite lower risk appetite and commodity prices. "The JPY is stronger getting support from lower risk appetite and lower US Treasury yields. Nevertheless, the possibility of further monetary easing in Japan tomorrow should limit further JPY strengthening." EUR/SEK currently trades at 9.7223. (karl.bruze@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0702 GMT [Dow Jones] The recent fall in EUR/CHF so far hasn't prompted an SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 UBS\uc2  notes. Markets are now looking for an indication of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy, economist Reto Huenerwadel says, adding that a speech scheduled by directorate member Danthine at SNB's money market event on March 18 may provide clues. EUR/CHF is around 1.4513. (martin.gelnar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0701 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  sticks to paying 5-year THB IRS, expects BOT to hike rate next month, supported by strong economic data; house entered trade at 3.34%, targets +25 bps, swap now at 3.41%. But says swaps on 2-year, 3-year tenors have risen more than 5-year's, following BOT hawkish statement last week; "we should have positioned at a shorter date...that is where in retrospect the largest up-move in yields happened after the BOT meeting last week." (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0700 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.2% at 21,048.05, with trading interest staying lackluster (volume modest at HK$33.06 billion), as investors tend to remain cautious ahead of FOMC meeting later tonight, says First Shanghai's Linus Yip. Tips index to stay in 20,800-21,300 range near-term. "It's highly expected that the U.S. Fed will keep interest rates at low levels, but the focus is on its policy direction." Adds, HK government's plan to sell 374 subsidized apartments likely neutral to shares of property stocks, as sector expected to continue to benefit from low interest rate environment. Hang Seng Property Subindex down 1.0% at 27,652.54. HKEx (0388.HK) down 0.9% at HK$130.90, as recent thin trading volume of local bourse not supportive. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0657 GMT [Dow Jones] Technical factors point to a continued decline in EUR/CHF, \uc2 Landesbank Baden-Wuerttemberg\uc2  says. A close below EUR/CHF 1.4550 would signal a further fall towards 1.4410, then 1.4320. Only a close above 1.4610 would stabilize the market, according to the bank. The pair stands at 1.4513. (martin.gelnar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0656 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore STI supported by gains in index heavyweights property, banks and \uc2 SingTel\uc2  (Z74.SG) in an otherwise lackluster session; STI +0.4% at 2,884.95 with resistance tipped at 2,900. U.S. futures providing little lead ahead of Fed meeting tonight (DJIA +1 point). "The market is very quiet, but I'm hoping we'll see a consolidation and then head higher," says AmFraser senior VP equity sales Gabriel Gan. Adds while Fed widely expected to keep rates on hold, wording of statement will be closely watched to see if there is any change to Fed's commitment to keep rates low for an "extended" period. \uc2 SingTel\uc2  (Z74.SG) +1.6% at S$3.16 as stock reverses recent underperformance vs STI; City Developments (C09.SG) +1.9% at S$10.86 supported by robust property market data; OCBC (O39.SG) biggest bank riser +1.0% at S$8.88. Market volume remains thin with losers outpacing gainers 1.2 to 1. (kirsty.green@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0653 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD tad higher on outright buying by investment banks, says trader at U.K. bank. Tips pair in 7.7570-7.7610 band near term. "Still, the pair is biased down, but likely to remain in tight range as the upcoming IPOs aren't sizable and can't boost the demand for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  significantly," trader says. IPOs in pipeline include Chinese sofa maker Man Wah (M42.SG) plans to kick off up to US$440 million IPO next week. Pair last at 7.7595 vs 7.7588 this morning, 7.7589 yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0652 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx Japan Series 12 tightens slightly to 123 bps from yesterday's 126.5 bps; "while individual-name CDS trading has been very limited before the end of the fiscal year (in March), a tightening trend is continuing on a indication basis" after sharp widening last month, says Japan bank trader. Single-name CDS deals done today include \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2 's senior USD-denominated protection at 92 bps vs 130 bps on Feb. 3, \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ's USD-subordinated protection at 95 bps vs 105 bps on March 3. CDS players will also keep eye on which firms' protection iTrax index provider decides to add and remove from new iTraxx Japan Series 13 before its rollover next week. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD still lower at 31.806 vs 31.820 yesterday, weighed by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's report seen by Dow Jones yesterday saying stronger TWD will help ease imported inflationary pressure, but losses limited by expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says local bank trader. "It seems that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not allow the pair to fall sharply in the short term," to deter speculators, he says. Notes many players still on sidelines awaiting cues from FOMC's meeting results due later in global day, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Governor briefing to legislature tomorrow. Tips pair to hover around 31.800 rest of session. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0646 GMT [Dow Jones] Enter short EUR/JPY calendar spreads to exploit elevated EUR/JPY realized volatility and steep volatility curve, positioning for realized vol outperformance via spread's long gamma and capturing falling implied vol via its net short vega, says Dow Jones CommentaryPlus senior analyst Russell Floyd. Suggests buying EUR100 million Apr. 15 expiry 123.70 EUR/JPY straddle and selling EUR100 million Sep. 16 expiry 114.15/131.75 EUR/JPY strangle for 0.292% EUR, or EUR292,000 upfront premium. See =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  OPTION VIEWS:Short EUR/JPY Calendar;Realized-Implied Divergence. (SMG)\par \par \uc2 0639 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures end higher in afternoon trade ahead of FOMC outcome; June futures end session +18 ticks at 110.69, though closing yield levels for cash bonds not available 