\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Korea Govt Bonds Steady; May Remain Rangebound\b0\par\par\uc2 1,579 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Februar 2010\par 02:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0135 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures little moved in early trade, taking a breather after some sharp losses late last week, analysts say; 3-year yield +1 bp 4.19%, 5-year yield down 1 bp at 4.76%, March futures +3 ticks at 110.19. Bonds fall late last week on BOK governor's hawkish remarks, Fed's surprise discount rate hike and "bonds - which had become somewhat immune to bond-negative factors after favorable BOK monetary meeting results in February - are likely to make more cautious moves now after those events," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daewoo Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Yoon Yeo-sam; adds "3-year yields are likely to remain within the 4.00% to 4.25% range for a while", before rushing to test any fresh lows.(MJL)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may rebound after Friday's sharp falls; lower-than-expected U.S. CPI data likely to help ease concerns of potential near-term rate hike in U.S., says Conita Hung at Delta Asia. Thinks index could rise above 20,000; tips 20,100-20,200 range today. Adds, HK property stocks may outperform ahead of land sales later today, with surveyors cited by SCMP expecting Tseung Kwan O residential site could sell for between HK$2.6 billion and HK$4.4 billion, vs guaranteed bid of HK$2.0 billion. HSI ended down 2.6% at 19,894.02 Friday. (SUT)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares +1.9% at 7585.70 to catch up to DJIA's about 3% gain for the week, rallies in other global markets during week-long Lunar New Year holiday. But off 7619.87 high at opening as "investors are still cautious about the strength of the rebound and are afraid selling may emerge as the main index approaches three-month moving average at 7700," says Concord Securities trader Allen Lin, adding weakness in Hong Kong market last week also weighs; tips main index in 7550-7650 range rest of session. Steel sector +2.7%, best performing sector with China Steel (2002.TW) +2.1% at NT$33.45 on expected price hikes amid rising raw material costs; TSMC (2330.TW) +2.2% at NT$59.70, \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) +1.2% at NT$36.75 after Taiwan government says Feb. 10 to ease restrictions for both sectors' investments in China. (JEH)\par \par \uc2 0128 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.7% at 1266.23 on broad based gains, with blue chips leading advances, says dealer with local brokerage; thinks index may test 50-day moving average of 1272 later if buying interest sustained. "The local market is rising in tandem with regional markets, which are all relieved that Wall Street did not react as adversely as initially feared to the U.S. Federal Reserve's discount rate hike," said dealer. Volume moderate with 36 million shares worth MYR42 million traded while market breadth positive gainers outnumbering losers 142 to 27. Among gainers, Public Bank (1295.KU) +1.3% at MYR11.24, Tenaga (5347.KU) +1.1% at MYR8.08, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2  (1961.KU) +1.2% at MYR5.38. \uc2 Sime Darby\uc2  (4197.KU) +0.9% at MYR8.60 and Genting Malaysia (4715.KU) +1.9% at MYR2.72. (ECH)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  government's latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the property market could dampen demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  banks' mortgage loans, says \uc2 UBS\uc2 . Notes that housing loans, which make up 32% of total loans, were up 14.8% on year in 2009; "we think in the near term this will continue on the back of property transactions that were done in 2009, but in the later half of the year the momentum could slow as property transactions are likely to cool off." Says worries over impact of property cooling measures, "likely to dampen the share prices of the bank stocks in the coming days." But maintains bullish view on Singapore banks as says proportion of mortgages affected by new 80% loan-to-value cap is relatively small and banks should see positive impact from higher interest rates later in year. Reiterates Buy rating on all 3 Singapore banks. DBS (D05.SG) +0.6% at S$14.18, OCBC (O39.SG) +0.5% at S$8.58, UOB (U11.SG) +1.0% at S$18.78.(KIG)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] Major JGB market focus this week is how strongly investors eager to buy 20-year notes with yield above 2.15% at tomorrow's tender, says \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  strategist Kenro Kawano; Japan MOF slated to auction Y1.1 trillion in 20-year JGBs. Says though superlong sector unlikely to outperform in yield curve, absolute yields look relatively attractive; superlong JGBs usually meet strong demand from domestic life insurers, who aim at lengthening duration of yen-denominated assets to match liability. Recommends keeping long positions in JGBs for now, especially buying longer zone bonds when prices dip. 20-year cash JGB yield last flat at 2.170%, 10-year yield +1.0 bp at 1.340%. Lead March JGB futures down 0.08 at 139.39. (MEF)\par \par \uc2 0120 GMT [Dow Jones] LME copper down $90 vs PM kerb at $7,340/ton in early LME trading, zinc down $45 at $2,318. LME falls as expected after SHFE comes back online after week-long closure for Lunar New Year holiday. LME copper +9.2% last week, zinc +8.8%. Change to arbitrage means SHFE gets bought, LME sold. However, Chinese market participants expected to emerge from holiday in bullish mood, as indicated by ongoing buying interest just before the holiday, a period that usually sees activity quieten down. Better than expected euro-zone, U.S. economic data releases Friday also helping to underpin sentiment. Rusal today releasing full year output report, CEO Oleg Deripaska says demand picking up with orders from Europe and U.S. encouraging optimism. (EFB)\par \par \uc2 0120 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may open higher after week-long Lunar New Year break as Wall Street's strong performance last week likely to offset Beijing's surprise decision to hike bank reserve requirement ratio by 50bps from Feb. 25. Still, U.S. decision to raise rate it charges banks for emergency loans may weigh on sentiment. Shanghai Composite Index may face resistance at 3,100, after ending +1.1% at 3018.13 on Feb 12. "Investors who retreated to the sidelines before the holidays would return to the market. But there could be some weakness after the Federal Reserve's (discount rate hike) announcement. The Asian markets fell last Friday because of that," says Zhang Qi from Haitong Securities. Blue chips like banks may rise following news they are among 90 underlying securities for upcoming margin trading, short selling derivatives. Shenzhen Index +1.1% at 1130.52. (FSE)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  cuts GBP's outlook as house now expects BOE's dovish monetary policy stance to last for time being; Greece fiscal concerns also weigh on unit as investors now paying attention to U.K. debt conditions, says house's chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Japan Masafumi Yamamoto. Tips GBP/USD may hit 1.53 in 3 months, 1.55 in 6 months, lower than previous view of 1.63, 1.63. Tips GBP/JPY may hit 141 in 3 months, 147 in 6 months, cut from 150, 155. GBP/USD last at 1.5481, GBP/JPY last at 141.91.(TMO)\par \par \uc2 0104 GMT [Dow Jones] Speculation is growing that China will let CNY gain at some point in months ahead, to curb inflation, says \uc2 UBS\uc2 ; notes growth in new lending has already prompted PBOC to hike RRR this year, suggesting strong commitment to take decisive action when called for. "With exports having recovered from the lows seen at the depths of the Crisis, it is believed the peg will be loosened either independently of, or in combination with, a hike in the policy rate. Our economists believe any loosening of the yuan peg will be gradual and modest in size," probably taking USD/CNY no lower than 6.40-6.50. Says a CNY revalue would likely lead to brief period of AUD and JPY strength, but it wouldn't last.(RXM)\par \par \uc2 0103 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY may fall after week-long Lunar New Year holiday; banks, local companies may have enough USD reserves due to strong demand pre-holiday, says Shanghai-based local bank trader. Says pair may fall below 6.8300 vs 6.8330 last close. "Onshore players may shrug off Federal Reserve's discount rate hike (last week), as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely keep the central parity stable regardless of what happens with the Fed's monetary policy," she says. Central parity was at 6.8269 Feb. 12, last trading session before holiday. (WYL)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP may slip as EUR gains some ground, a sign of improved appetite for riskier assets; pair tipped to find immediate support at 46.15 after settling at 46.28 late Friday, with wider trading range pegged at 46.00-46.40; "We're looking at weaker dollar because of risk appetite," says a local bank trader. (SFL)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 21, 2010 20:35 ET (01:35 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100222e62m00025\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
