\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Asian Reserves Up In Jan As  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Continues\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Roman   \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \par \uc2 778 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2010\par 05:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose 0.8% in January from a month earlier, boosted by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and large inflows into several key economies, indicating that the fast-growing region remains a strong draw for foreign capital.\par \par \uc2 According to calculations by Dow Jones Newswires, reserves held by 11 key Asian central banks, excluding China's, stood at US$2.774 trillion at the end of January, up from US$2.753 trillion at the end of December. That compares with a 1.1% decrease posted in December from November, the first decrease in 10 months.\par \par \uc2 China, which releases its data on a quarterly basis, reported US$2.4 trillion in reserves at the end of December.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar's surge against alternative reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the euro and British pound lowered the value of non-dollar reserves in January, as it did in December. However, dollar purchases in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by Asian central banks - most of which want to stop their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising too much - as well as strong investment flows offset the valuation effect in January.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose 3% in January against the euro and 0.9% against the British pound. It fell 2.8% against the Japanese yen.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also lost some ground against other key Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the Indonesian rupiah, Thai baht and Korean won, prompting suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in these countries and elsewhere. As a result, reserves rose over 2.5% in Korea, Thailand and Indonesia, hitting an all-time high in January in all three countries.\par \par \uc2 In Taiwan, where the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also is prone to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, reserves increased by 0.7% to set an all-time high for the fourteenth consecutive month, the longest streak in the region.\par \par \uc2 That trend seems likely to continue. China's economic growth, which Xinhua reports the Academy of Sciences think-tank expects to be 10% this year, is driving other economies in the region to growth rates well above those in the developed world.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts expect that inflows will keep pushing Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher, driving central banks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coming months and building up their reserves.\par \par \uc2 Central banks are likely to remain focused on protecting the competitiveness of local export industries amid concern about the strength of final demand in the developed world, leaving inflation worries as a secondary concern despite an uptick in energy and commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 UBS\uc2  economist Jonathan Anderson wrote recently that policymakers in Asia and other emerging markets historically have maintained a preference for weak or stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a trend that should continue.\par \par \uc2 Anderson noted that many emerging economies face steeper inflation rates - four percentage points higher on average - than do developed markets. That increases the cost of their products and makes them lose competitiveness over time, even if their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain stable against those of their trading partners in the developed world. As a result, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen as a way to protect their export industries.\par \par \uc2 Following are the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for Asia's 12 leading economies. All numbers are in billions of U.S. dollars.\par \par \uc2 The total number excludes China.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par              Jan 10       Jan 09      Dec 09    As Of \par TOTAL         2,774        2,406       2,753   end Jan Ex China \par  \par China         2,400        1,946       2,400   end Dec \par Japan         1,053        1,011       1,049   end Jan \par India           281        248.6       283.5    29-Jan \par Indonesia      69.6         50.9        66.1   end Jan \par Korea         273.7        201.7         267   end Jan \par Malaysia         97         91.3        96.7    29-Jan \par Pakistan       14.5         10.2          15    30-Jan \par Philippines    45.4         39.6          45   end Jan \par Singapore     189.6        167.1       187.8   end Jan \par Taiwan        350.7        292.7       348.2   end Jan \par Thailand      142.4        110.7       138.4    29-Jan \par Hong Kong     257.1        181.7       255.8   end Jan \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (David Roman, a regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reporter, has been a Dow Jones correspondent since 2002, covering economic and corporate news. He can be reached at 65 6415 4045 or by e-mail: david.roman@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along wit