\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Weaker Late On U.S. Jobs Data Jitters\b0\par\par\uc2 289 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 2009\par 08:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                          Latest   Change \par    USD/SGD               1.3813   +0.0019 \par    Overnight rate        0.31%    unchanged \par    Two-year bond yield:  0.61%    +2 bps \par    10-year bond yield:   2.55%    +1 bp \par    Two-year swap offer:  1.40%    -1 bp \par    10-year swap offer:   2.94%    +1 bp \par    2-10-yr swap curve:   154 bps  +6 bps \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was weaker late Friday, weighed down by a selloff in most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and equities as investors remain cautious ahead of the release of key U.S. jobs numbers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The U.S. Department of Labor Friday releases its non-farm payrolls data for November, with economists expecting to see 125,000 jobs lost in the month and the unemployment rate steady at 10.2%.\par \par \uc2 A Singapore-based trader for a foreign bank said the Singapore dollar will take its cues from the euro's reaction to the jobs data.\par \par \uc2 However, he expects there will be some limit to the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation even if a strong employment number supports a burst of risk appetite, given the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts.\par \par \uc2 "Even if the U.S. dollar does weaken a bit and goes down to S$1.3750, I think through fear and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  it will come back," the trader said.\par \par \uc2 "All the central banks in Asia are trying to stop their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising too much--the MAS is not the only one."\par \par \uc2 Singapore bonds also weakened with yields rising slightly on positioning ahead of the employment data.\par \par \uc2 -By Sam Holmes, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4157; samuel.holmes@dowjones.com [ 04-12-09 0751GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1163 1859   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091204e5c4000ic\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
