\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Malaysia Govt Bonds Steady; 3-Yr Auction Awaited\b0\par\par\uc2 1,633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2009\par 08:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0724 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds steady late; dealer says volumes thin ahead of long weekend; markets will be closed Friday for Muslim holiday. "There are no drivers today, while some investors are staying on the sidelines ahead of Monday's auction (of new 3-year benchmark)," says dealer. Yields for February 2015 MGS down 1 bp at 3.72%, November 2019 MGS flat at 4.21%. (KPL)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0721 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.1% at 1272.40 in modest volume, continues to consolidate within tight 1270-1275 range ahead of long weekend; some local heavyweight stocks bucking regional trend but "with the lack of catalysts to fuel buying power ahead of the impending weekend, expect some profit-taking later today," CIMB says. "Local funds may continue to lend support to big-cap government-linked companies but gains are likely to be small. Expect profit taking into strength to cap gains," says dealer. Among gainers, Maxis (6012.KU) +1.1% at MYR5.36, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2  (1961.KU) +0.9% at MYR5.46, Proton (5304.KU) +2.2% at MYR4.10. On downside, Gamuda (5398.KU) down 5.2% at MYR2.75, WCT (9679.KU) down 4.5% at MYR2.55. (VGB)\par \par \uc2 0716 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS lower, tracking sharp losses in stocks (Shanghai Composite Index down 3.6% at 3,170.98). 3-year down 1 bp at 2.93%-2.99%, 5-year down 1 bp at 3.47%-3.55%. Trader at Shanghai-based foreign bank says swaps could extend losses as expectations of further tightening measures fading, "We don't think there will be any rate hike or other measures soon, so the swaps will be unable to hold on at current levels." (WYL)\par \par \uc2 0714 GMT [Dow Jones] USD drop has driven it either through or towards some critical levels vs other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with USD/CHF pushing through parity for 1st time since April 2008, and CAD, AUD also approaching parity vs USD, says \uc2 UBS\uc2 , with potential for further inroads vs USD next few days when holiday conditions are likely to constrain liquidity. "The question now being asked is how sustainable these levels might be. Of central importance to answering this question is to assess whether further upside in these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' dollar axes will push their real effective exchange rates to deviate from long-trends, as this is often a good measure of overvaluation or undervaluation. By this measure all three \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are overvalued, though admittedly not by much." USD likely to stay choppy vs CHF, CAD, AUD and "we cannot rule out that in the short term all three will break through parity, at the same time. However, these gains will probably be short-lived, as ultimately they (though the Australian dollar to a lesser extent) are not the vehicles where the U.S.' imbalance corrections, via a weak dollar, should be expressed."(RXM)\par \par \uc2 0709 GMT [Dow Jones]--USD/CHF is expected to continue its recent fall, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. A small rebound back towards 1.0040/1.0100 isn't ruled out, but that would be likely followed by a further weakness. Sees the pair aiming at 0.9740. Trades at 0.9999. (MGE)\par \par \uc2 0701 GMT [Dow Jones] Capital Economics says China's growing domestic production of ores, refined metals is undermining country's import demand; "given the current state of the market in China, it seems likely either that China's exports of metals such as copper will pick up, or its imports will tail off further, or both. Whichever happens, developments in China are unlikely to support world prices much longer," says Mark Williams, international economist at the consultancy. Adds, in contrast to base metals like copper, zinc, the steel market "looks relatively balanced." LME 3-month copper at $6,966/ton, down $14 with 1,354 lots done so far. (JAC)\par \par \uc2 0656 GMT [Dow Jones] Risk for USD/SGD still "definitely on the downside" given USD/DXY has broken important levels, trader says; adds pair has plenty of scope to run down to 1.3700, where he expects is lower bound of MAS NEER band. According to people familiar with situation, MAS earlier today \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in USD/SGD, buying at 1.3797. USD/SGD now 1.3787. Trader notes while Asian equities markets slightly weaker today, risk appetite still largely in neutral phase. "I think the trend is for more Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation near-term."(SRH)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD at 32.189, down on yesterday's 32.236 but almost unchanged from 32.190 midday, tracking weaker global USD, slight selling by exporters. Trading interest low given slow global markets for Thanksgiving holiday, says foreign bank trader. Investors also awaiting Taiwan's 3Q GDP data to be released around 0800 GMT. But even if data better than expected, "I don't think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will allow the pair to fall under NT$32," says trader; tips pair in narrow 32.180-32.220 range in afternoon. (JEH)\par \par \uc2 0645 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 1.8% at 22,2216.15 vs 1.1% decline midday, as China markets fall more with Shanghai Composite down 3.4%, led by banks as fundraising concerns continue to weigh. Peter Lai at DBS tips index may test 21,200 medium-term if declines persist. "It's likely to see more downside in the near-term, as sentiment in China banks remains weak," says Lai. ICBC (1398.HK) down 2.8% at HK$6.61, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) down 3.5% at HK$6.90, Bank of China (3988.HK) down 1.8% at HK$4.40. \uc2 Bank of East Asia\uc2  (0023.HK) +3.4% at HK$34.80, after \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  upgrades stock to Buy from Sell. Volume strong at HK$46.40 billion, of which HK$4.85 billion coming from debutante Minsheng (1988.HK). (SUT)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stock market remains quiet with participants mostly sidelined after cashing out early gains. Overall volume anemic at over 733 million shares, with about 1.5 decliners for every gainer. STI down 0.9% at 2768.08, expected to end lower, with support at 2750. "There's not much business," says retail dealer at bank-backed brokerage; "business has already dwindled in recent weeks. It's even worse today." Despite sluggish broad market, some lower liners still attracting interest. \uc2 PEC Ltd\uc2 . (IX2.SG) +7.2% at S$0.67 after DBS Vickers starts coverage at Buy with S$0.88 target price; Swiber (AK3.SG) +3.5% at S$0.90 as company bags US$77 million contract to install gas pipelines in Myanmar; Cerebos Pacific (C20.SG) +4.8% at S$3.72 as company reports 26% on-year rise in net profit to S$25.1 million for fiscal 4Q ended September. (FKH)\par \par \uc2 0643 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds give up some gains on steady uptick in primary goods prices, 6.90% 2019 bond at 98.16 vs 98.21 before data, 98.00 last close. "Everything said and done, rising food prices are a fact of life, and the market was well aware of it before too," says trader with primary dealership. Adds, short-term sentiment still positive as market focuses on slowing federal bond supply. Tips bonds to find buying support at current levels. India's primary articles index rose 1.2% to 279.7 in week to Nov. 14 from 276.4 previous week. (BIJ)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets roiled after sharp USD losses overnight; USD/JPY briefly hits 14-year low at 86.29 as stop-loss sell orders around 87.00 triggered; decline comes despite ratcheted-up warning from Japanese Finance Minister Fuji that he will monitor USD/JPY movement "very closely." USD/JPY at 86.66 from 87.33 late NY overnight; EUR/JPY at 130.92 vs 132.25, dragged by USD/JPY. EUR/USD at 1.5104 vs 1.5143. USD's broad weakness drives regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher, several central banks -- Philippines, Korea, \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Thailand -- suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support USD. Regional stock markets mostly lower; Nikkei down 0.6% as exporters hit by strong JPY; S&P/ASX 200 down 0.3%, Kospi down 0.8%, Sensex down 0.3%, Taipei down 0.2%, STI down 0.8%, Shanghai Composite down 2.6%, HSI down 1.6%; Philippines +0.8%, NZX-50 +0.4%. Vietnamese shares down 4.1%, after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  yesterday announces VND devaluation, rate hikes effective December. Australian 3Q business investment down 3.9% Q/Q vs 0.2% fall expected. Singapore October manufacturing output growth weaker-than-expected due to continued weakness in pharmaceuticals; total manufacturing +3.6% Y/Y vs +6.3% expected. NZ business confidence tad down in November; NBNZ survey shows net 43.4% of businesses expect better conditions over next 12 months vs 48.2% in October. BOK widens its annual inflation target band to 2%-4% for three years starting 2010 vs 2.5%-3.5% range in 2007-2009. Philippine 3Q GDP +0.8% Y/Y vs +1.9% expected. Spot gold at $1,193.60/oz, up $1.50 from NY; hit record high of $1,195.10/oz early Asian hours, supported by EUR gains, news Sri Lanka's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys 10 tons of IMF gold. Gold helps LME 3-month copper hit 14-month high of $7,060/ton; last at $6,982/ton, up $2 vs London PM kerb. Nymex January crude down 60 cents at $77.36/bbl. (CNG) [ 11-26-09 0224ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091126e5bq0005d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\