\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Higher Late As Risk Appetite Builds On US Data\b0\par\par\uc2 358 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2009\par 09:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                       Latest   Change \par    USD/SGD               1.3962   -0.0062 \par    Overnight rate        0.50%    +19 bps \par    Two-year bond yield:  0.63%    -2 bps \par    10-year bond yield:   2.60%    -1 bps \par    Two-year swap offer:  1.56%    Unchanged \par    10-year swap offer:   3.02%    +1 bps \par    2-10-yr swap curve:  146 bps   +1 bps \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was higher in late Asian trade Friday as risk appetite improved on better-than-expected U.S. third-quarter gross domestic product data, and analysts say further gains will depend on cues from the meeting next week of the U.S. Federal Reserve.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The sharp rise in the local unit also prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the U.S. dollar from falling sharply.\par \par \uc2 Still, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't major, a person familiar with the situation said. The person said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as needed to slow gains in the local unit.\par \par \uc2 "We are already trading in the stronger end of the band," said a trader with a local bank. "Going forward, cues will come from the FOMC meeting next week, and until then the Singapore dollar will trade in a range against the U.S. dollar."\par \par \uc2 The MAS uses the exchange rate to maintain price stability and pegs the Singapore dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  under an undisclosed band.\par \par \uc2 A strong rally in Asian equity markets also saw some fund inflows, a trader with a foreign bank said.\par \par \uc2 "Risk appetite seems to be building up again," the trader said.\par \par \uc2 In the government bond market, prices rose a bit on some buying by local banks, even as volumes remained thin.\par \par \uc2 "I would say the market was very quite," another trader with a foreign bank said. The volume was about S$200 million compared with S$500 million to S$1 billion traded on a good day, the trader said.\par \par \uc2 -By P.R. Venkat, Dow Jones Newswires; +65 64154 152; venkat.pr@dowjones.com [ 30-10-09 0823GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1163 1859   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091030e5au001kc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
