\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Asia to loosen grip on  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  as inflation returns\b0\par\par\uc2 834 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2009\par 06:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Asia c.banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains as dollar drops\par \par \uc2 * But \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will ease as inflation revives\par \par \uc2 * Inflation muted now, but will be a concern next year\par \par \uc2 * Less pressure for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if China unshackles yuan\par \par \uc2 By Kevin Yao\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Oct 23 (Reuters) - The dollar's plunge has prompted Asian central banks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb strength in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and support exports, but they may change tack in 2010 to rely on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation to contain inflation.\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are rallying strongly as investors pile cash into emerging markets, believing they will not only recover from the global downturn before developed economies but will start to raise interest rates first as well.\par \par \uc2 But the risk for Asian central banks is that raising interest rates as growth and inflation revives could spur yet more capital inflows, especially if it means a growing rate premium over G7 economies.\par \par \uc2 Another option would be to reduce the level of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and allow \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation to take up the policy slack.\par \par \uc2 "At this juncture, we think central banks are seeking to prevent excessive speculative pressure while trying to accommodate endemic dollar weakness," said Emmanuel Ng, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 OCBC Bank\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "Probably in the first to second quarter, the global outlook would have better clarity and the central bankers would be able to sleep better when they loosen up slightly to allow their respective exchange rates to reflect better economic conditions."\par \par \uc2 For now, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is serving the purpose of helping Asian exporters in their time of need as they try to recover from the global downturn and compete with China, which has kept the yuan flat against the dollar since mid 2008.\par \par \uc2 But China is likely to unshackle the yuan early next year when export markets are expected to pick up, which will help calm fears among its smaller neighbours about losing out to Chinese exporters, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 Investors are already pricing in the prospect of stronger economic growth and higher interest rates. The South Korean won and Indian rupee have each gained 6 percent since September. The Philippine peso is up 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah is soaring. It's up 16 percent this year as the dollar sits at 14-month lows against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Obviously we see \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -strengthening despite the fact that they are \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and I would expect that to continue to be the case as long as there are appreciating pressures," said Magnus Prim, chief Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at SEB in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll shows analysts expect the won and rupee to gain about 6 percent from now to the end of 2010, leading Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher. [MKTPOLL1]\par \par \uc2 Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves held by Asian central banks surged by nearly $112 billion in September to a record $4.96 trillion, reflecting dollars accumulated in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nSP496890]\par \par \uc2 "PRE-CONDITION"\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, Australia became the first G20 country to raise interest rates and markets are pricing in the risk of tighter policy elsewhere in Asia, including in South Korea, India and China.\par \par \uc2 Analysts believe some Asian central banks, particularly those in Indonesia, India and the Philippines, may see inflation rising closer or beyond their comfort levels in the first half of 2010.\par \par \uc2 But raising interest rates aggressively when borrowing costs stay low in the United States would only fuel capital inflows and eventually negate the tightening effect of rate rises, analysts at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "In other words, monetary policy decoupling in Asia virtually requires as a pre-condition that exchange rates appreciate. This is especially so the longer low interest rates are expected to persist in the West," they said.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll suggests Philippines inflation could hit 4.1 percent in the second quarter of 2010, inside the government's annual target of 3.5-5.5 percent. It's just 0.7 percent now.\par \par \uc2 The poll shows Indonesia's inflation will more than double to hit 5.9 percent in the second quarter of next year, against a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecast of 4-6 percent.\par \par \uc2 In India, analysts expect inflation to surpass the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's comfort level of 5 percent before March 31 from 1.2 percent now.\par \par \uc2 Another incentive to cut back on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is that it can fuel inflation because of the domestic liquidity that is generated.\par \par \uc2 "That's when one of the side effects of fighting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation becomes a pressing domestic issue," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2  in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 While reduced \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may suit a policy purpose, China may hold the key to easing \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  nerves about rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I think in general terms, this kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to step down rather than step up when China starts to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen again, which I expect to happen during the first quarter of next year," SEB's Prim said. (Editing by Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091026e5an0006s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
