\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: HKMA  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  To Stay Large, Ongoing - \uc2 HSBC\b0\par\par\uc2 1,309 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2009\par 05:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0320 GMT [Dow Jones] HKMA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely remain "large and ongoing" as rate spreads of HKD, USD to stay "constrained", says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; adds USD/HKD peg likely to stay put anyway. HKMA sold total HK$41.85 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market over past week, as USD/HKD kept hitting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  level at 7.7500; pair last 7.7503. HKMA base rate (HK policy rate) has to follow Fed funds rate due to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  peg, local market rates such as Hibors very low (benchmark 3-month Hibor at 0.20%) due to flushing liquidity.(ARP)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0311 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD climbs strongly after RBA assistant governor Phil Lowe says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects a higher average exchange rate in future. \uc2 Deutsche Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist John Horner says that remark on top of upbeat comments about outlook for Australia's economy and trade with Asia helped to lift pair. Lowe also endorses RBA's policy direction saying it's proper to start returning rates to normal. AUD/USD rises from around 0.9120 to 0.9162 now.(JEG)\par \par \uc2 0311 GMT [Dow Jones] 1-month ATM USD/JPY implied volatilities up at 13.50%/14.20% from 13.30%/14.00% Friday in NY amid slightly higher demand for USD upside hedges after unit marked fresh 3-week high Friday after Tokyo hours, dealers say. Options will likely hover near current levels for rest of week, dealers say; while USD gains have led to upside hedging demand, have not prompted players to offload USD downside hedges, as many players remain hesitant to sell protection against any USD falls amid U.S. earnings releases. (ATM)\par \par \uc2 0303 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares down slightly on weaker overseas markets; benchmark PSEi off 0.2% to 2916.07 on light volume; losers led by PLDT (TEL.PH), down 0.8% at PHP2,570, geothermal power producer Energy Development Corp. (EDC.PH), down 1.0% at PHP4.80; funds also shifting to small-cap stocks such as NiHao Mineral Resources (NI.PH), MRC Allied (MRC.PH). PCCI Securities Brokers says market "undergoing a minor correction following its recent breakout" into the 2900 level; "Most trending and volume oscillators are bearish for the near-term," it adds. (SFL)\par \par \uc2 0300 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  adds Korean financials (\uc2 Shinhan Bank\uc2  2016 bond, National Agricultural Cooperative 2017 bond), lower-end of investment-grade names (Noble Group 2013 bond, \uc2 Petroliam Nasional\uc2  2014 bond) to Asian bond portfolio for October. Tips further de-risking of Asian banking sector on higher capital requirement, "stickier" funding, safer balance sheet; says news flow over coming year likely be "creditor friendly." Also sees investment value in IG names that trading in lower-rating yield. (ARP)\par \par \uc2 0259 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore market likely to trade sideways in 4Q09 as further re-rating factors are absent, says Nomura. Broker notes recent Singapore economic data mixed with September PMI reading suggesting weaker output growth ahead; phasing out of government's Jobs Credit Scheme likely to cause earnings drag in FY10. "We see 2010 being a transition year, with subdued economic and earnings growth." Advises investors to position themselves in stocks that can offer superior earnings growth; "stock picking will be important and the market will reward stocks that can deliver above-market earnings growth." Says banks UOB (U11.SG) and OCBC (O39.SG) and commodity plays Wilmar (F34.SG) and Noble (N21.SG) look good bets. While companies like ST Engineering (S63.SG) and \uc2 SingTel\uc2  (Z74.SG), which provide certainty of strong cashflows, high dividends, also likely to be in favor. STI last off 0.5% at 2,695.40.(KIG)\par \par \uc2 0258 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD likely to suffer shallow, weak correction, says Westpac Bank markets strategist Imre Speizer. Notes NZD, along with AUD, underwent reversal signal Friday, but so far correction hasn't materialized. "At the moment, any correction looks like it will be shallow and weak. But if it breaks below 0.7300, there is likely to be a much bigger correction." Says trading quiet today, data release calendar light for week, with release of RBA minutes tomorrow looming as potentially most influential news. Adds Kiwi unlikely to break above 0.7500. Pair last 0.7419.(SML)\par \par \uc2 0256 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last down 0.3% at 1635.93 in light volume, paring losses after hitting 1614.85 earlier on gains in car makers, some techs. Higher USD/KRW (last at 1,178.75 vs 1,164.5 Friday close) eased jitters about competitiveness of exporters, says Kim Seong-bong at \uc2 Samsung Securities\uc2 ; but expects volatile session as moves tend to be exaggerated by low trading volumes. Kospi support pegged at 60-day moving average around 1610. \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE) +2.7% at KRW115,000, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) +1.1% at KRW101,000. \uc2 Ssangyong Motor\uc2  (003620.SE) still +15% at KRW2,885 on its new restructuring plan, news it's in "initial stage of talks" with some strategic, financial investors interested in company. KB Financial (105560.SE) +1.3% at KRW62,000 on news Posco will buy KRW250 billion worth of shares in KB Financial. But \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) still down 1.7% at KRW733,000.(SKS)\par \par \uc2 0247 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares down 0.4% at 2504.696 in light volume, weighed by weak IDR, selling in automotive, financial-related stocks on concerns that higher oil prices to stoke inflation; immediate support at 2500. "It's a healthy correction...selling is on risk aversion due to rising oil prices," says trader at Reliance Securities. Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) down 1.3% at IDR7,850, Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) off 1.2% at IDR2,025, car maker Astra (ASII.JK) off 1.8% at IDR32,200. (EDH)\par \par \uc2 0245 GMT [Dow Jones] Key economic appointments to reinforce positive expectations on Indonesia's new government, Eurasia Group says. Sri Mulyani Indrawati, Finance Minister and acting Coordinating Minister for Economy, says over weekend was asked to retain fiscal, economic-related portfolio, indicating she will stay on in at least one of her current roles; Trade Minister Mari Elka Pangestu, another key reformer, also says asked to retain similar role. President Yudhoyono likely to announce final cabinet line-up Wednesday after inauguration tomorrow; "the final line-up will likely prove positive for Indonesia's medium-term economic development plans," Eurasia says.(RLC)\par \par \uc2 0238 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares last +0.2% at 611.18 on moderate 35.7 million shares valued at VND1.49 trillion, with local institutions buying on expectations of positive outcome from meeting of lawmakers tomorrow to work out new ways to continue stimulating economy through year end. "Though the pressure for taking profit remains, buying appetite is also strong and I expect the key index to end in positive territory today," says trader with Bao Viet Securities. Among advancers, \uc2 PetroVietnam Finance\uc2  (PPC.VH) +0.9% at VND43,900, Minh Phu Seafood (MPC.VH) +4.7% at VND49,500, Hoa Phat Group (HPG.VH) +0.7% at VND74,000.(HAA)\par \par \uc2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures extend gains, as foreigners reduce net-selling volume in futures. Foreigners now sellers of net 2,413 contracts vs selling net 4,016 contracts earlier. 3-year yield down 3 bps at 4.53%, 5-year yield down 2 bps at 4.94%, December futures +12 ticks at 108.44. "The market seems to be finding some stability today," after unexpected steep falls Friday on foreign futures selling, says local securities firm trader. Adds, barring any surprise dumping by foreign investors in futures again, bonds may be well supported for day. Puts 3-year yield support at 4.51%. (MJL) [ 10-18-09 2320ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091019e5aj0002m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
