\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Singapore seeks balancing act on  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2 , rates\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao \par \uc2 873 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2009\par 10:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 6 (Reuters) - Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen playing a delicate balancing act in the months ahead by keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable and borrowing costs low to cement an economic recovery while heading off possible asset bubbles.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) is tipped to keep its zero appreciation policy next Monday, while concerns about asset bubbles, particularly in the housing market, may have persuaded it to prevent further declines in already ultra-low money rates, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 "The MAS's money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have helped put a floor under money market rates recently," said Mirza Baig, strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Singapore's short-term interest rates, which track U.S. funding costs as the country imposes few capital controls, have fallen sharply since the collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  in September 2008, but they have stabilised in recent months.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had acted to keep market liquidity flush at the height of the credit crunch, analysts said, and they suspected it may have achieved that by sterilising less local money from the banking system after \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The MAS can engage in money market activities to push down rates and logically they could continue to be doing so in one form or another, though the degree of activity could vary," said Vishnu Varathan, economist at Forecast in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 He predicted Singapore's rates would be kept low for the next 6-12 months, but may drift higher later as the U.S. Federal Reserve and other central banks begin to tighten policy.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore 6-month swap offered rate (SOR), a benchmark for mortgage and commercial lending in the economy <SGDDFIX=ABSG>, has fallen steadily to 0.8 percent on Friday from 2.2 percent in September 2008.\par \par \uc2 The three-month interbank market rate (SIBOR) <SISGDD=ABSG> has been hovering near 0.7 percent since January, but its spread over three-month dollar LIBOR rates <LIBOR> has widened to 40 basis points since turning positive in May.\par \par \uc2 Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  calibrates policy by guiding the exchange rate within a undisclosed trade-weighted band rather than interest rates. It eased policy in April through a mild devaluation to stimulate the flagging economy.\par \par \uc2 The economy leapt out of its worst-ever recession in the second quarter, but the government has maintained its 2009 forecast for the economy to contract 4-6 percent.\par \par \uc2 CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 Low borrowing costs would help boost Singapore's domestic demand but exports, the key growth engine for the economy, remain weak despite improvements in the global economy, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 "The preference is probably for more accommodative interest rate stance given the credit contraction concerns for corporates, especially small- and medium-size enterprises," said Selena Ling, head of treasury research and strategy at \uc2 OCBC Bank\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Official data this week showed total bank lending in Singapore rose 1.03 percent in August to S$274.6 billion ($193.8 billion) from S$271.8 billion in July.\par \par \uc2 A wave of fund inflows into the country's stock and property markets, meanwhile, have raised concerns about asset bubbles.\par \par \uc2 Singapore's private home prices surged nearly 16 percent in the third quarter from the previous quarter, the biggest jump this decade despite the government's recent move to release more land and make it harder for home buyers to defer payments.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> has rallied 11 percent against the dollar since March, but \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not been seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  very aggressively even though analysts believe the unit has risen close to the upper end of the band.\par \par \uc2 Singapore's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves climbed by $2 billion in the first eight months of 2009 to $176.3 billion, far trailing increases in South Korea, Taiwan and Thailand.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's forward book -- a tally of its holdings of U.S. dollar swaps -- rose to $49 billion in August from $37 billion in May, indicating that the MAS has drained more liquidity from the market in recent months.\par \par \uc2 TIGHTENING RISKS\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to tighten policy by allowing trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation early next year, when the economy shows sustained recovery and inflation rises.\par \par \uc2 "The policy will be kept neutral for now," said Tim Condon, head of Asia research at ING, predicting a change in April 2010, when the bank next reviews policy.\par \par \uc2 Wei Zheng Kit, economist at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , also expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would tighten policy only in April 2010.\par \par \uc2 "Nevertheless, incipient inflation risks, and MAS's somewhat hawkish bias recently, suggests that risk of early tightening should not be ignored completely," he said in a note.\par \par \uc2 Three-month SIBOR rates may rise in line with expected U.S. interest rate rises early next year, but the spread between the SIBOR and LIBOR may turn negative again due to market expectations of a firmer Singapore dollar, Kit said.\par \par \uc2 "Continued demand for loans amidst a gradual easing of liquidity/deposit growth may also impute slight upward pressure on SIBOR rates into 2010," he added. (Reporting by Kevin Yao; Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-SINGAPORE/POLICY  (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI|ABX \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091009e5a6000kv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
