\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ UPDATE: \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  May  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  If US Dlr Below S$1.42-Source\b0\par\par\uc2 547 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2009\par 06:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail from source, analysis.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Costas Paris and Se Young Lee \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the U.S. dollar if it falls convincingly below S$1.4200, a person familiar with the situation said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar has seen a "steep rise" as it has climbed to its highest since December, the person told Dow Jones Newswires. "It will hurt the country's exports at a time when they are expected to turn around after being very weak this year."\par \par \uc2 This person said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the U.S. dollar stays around current levels, and if it does step in, it "will initially try to smooth the greenback's fall and then see what happens." The MAS doesn't want to fire its "big ammunition right from the start."\par \par \uc2 But in a recognition of the limits of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb rises in a single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the U.S. dollar is falling broadly, the person said, "All the calls are for the U.S. dollar to keep falling, so there is only so much a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can do."\par \par \uc2 The MAS uses the exchange rate as its chief policy tool because trade dwarfs Singapore's tiny domestic economy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  targets an undisclosed trading band for the local dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The person familiar with the situation said the strong end of the band for the Singapore dollar would now equate to the U.S. dollar trading around S$1.4150. The greenback was quoted at S$1.4238 at 0340 GMT, off the day's 2009 low of S$1.4232.\par \par \uc2 A stronger Singapore dollar could hurt the island state's competitiveness at a time when the economy remains weak due to the global downturn that has severely undercut demand for its exports.\par \par \uc2 The concern comes as the greenback falls broadly on expectations other countries will emerge from recession faster than the U.S., as gold has risen and as firmer equities markets have given a bid to riskier assets, such as those denominated in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Foreign reserves held by 11 key Asian countries, excluding China rose 2.2% in August from July, partly as central banks bought dollars to curb rises in their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to Dow Jones Newswires calculations.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's broad decline over the past 24 hours has given the MAS "some breathing room" as other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the basket have strengthened along with the Singapore dollar, said Sean Callow, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2  in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 Callow estimates the trade-weighed Singapore dollar is as high as 1.7% above the midpoint of the MAS band, but said this is "not an unusual level over the past few months and not implying an immediate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threat."\par \par \uc2 "It shouldn't hit the band just yet, but not too far away obviously," he said. "We're getting close, for sure."\par \par \uc2 -By Costas Paris, Dow Jones Newswires; +65 6415 4151; vincent.lee@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 09, 2009 00:21 ET (04:21 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020090909e599000lt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
