\par\par \b \uc2 INDIAN MORNING BRIEFING: Sensex Could Rally To 16,230\b0\par\par\uc2 1,568 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2009\par 05:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA           9497.34   +56.07  +0.59% \par Nasdaq         2037.77   +18.99  +0.94% \par S&P 500        1025.39    +8.99  +0.88% \par FTSE 100       4947.34   +14.16  +0.29% \par Nikkei Stock   10358.48  -34.75  -0.33% \par Hang Seng      20966.60 -103.21  -0.49% \par Kospi          1609.95    -9.74  -0.60% \par SGX Nifty      4,807.5   +5.5   +0.11% \par Sep contract \par  \par USD/JPY     92.32-36     -0.77% \par Range       93.08 -92.04 \par EUR/USD     1.4482-84    +1.05% \par Range       1.4535-1.4329 \par  \par CBOT Wheat Sep      $4.314 per bushel \par Comex Gold          $999.80   +$3.10 \par Comex Gold Range    $992.60-$1009.70 \par Nymex Crude (NY)    $71.10    +$3.08 \par  \par  \par CHARTING THE SENSEX \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bombay Stock Exchange's 30-share Sensitive Index could rally to 16,230, indicate charts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Tuesday's price action suggests weariness and profit-taking at higher levels. The occurrence of a "shooting star" candlestick pattern indicates a waning upside momentum.\par \par \uc2 The index must progress beyond the resistance at 16,430 to negate the chances of a short-term downward correction. A close below 15,850 will mark the start of the corrective phase and the index could then test support at 15,360.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks began the unofficial start to autumn just as they did this summer, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alcoa\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chevron\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 General Electric\f28 \uc2 \f2  at the forefront of a rally in energy, commodities and industrial companies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overall, the Dow Jones Industrial Average closed up 56.07 points, or 0.59%, at 9497.34, marking a gain for the third straight session. The Dow is up 45% from its 2009 low on March 9.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Helping push the index higher was another round of buying in several types of commodities including gold, silver and oil. From an economic standpoint, investors touted an expectation of growth globally that will, in turn, drive demand for each of these products into the end of the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The move in these commodities lifted several Dow components, such as aluminum giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alcoa\f28 \uc2 \f2 , up 3.5% at $12.60, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chevron\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which rose 2.2% to $70.48.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among other indexes, the Nasdaq Composite tacked on 18.99, or 0.94%, to 2037.77, hitting its highest close since Oct. 1. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  500 rose 8.99, or 0.88%, to 1025.39, including more than 1% gains for energy, materials, industrial and consumer discretionary companies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets were mixed Wednesday with commodity- and energy-related stocks higher on stronger oil and metals prices. In Japan, the market was dragged lower by concerns about further yen strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Resource stocks were the darlings for the day, after Nymex crude oil futures Tuesday posted their biggest single-day rise since Aug. 19. Metals prices were also broadly higher with spot gold above the $1,000 per troy ounce level.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Now that equities have rebounded and credit is flowing a little easier, the dollar is hitting the dumps, putting immense pressure on the price of oil and other commodities to go up," said Mike Sander of Sander Capital Advisors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was up, Japan's Nikkei 225 was down, South Korea's Kospi Composite was off and New Zealand's NZX-50 was higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index was lower with the Shanghai Composite and Taiwan's main index higher. Singapore's Straits Times Index was down 0.2%.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  majors were trading in very tight ranges as traders looked for clear direction after the U.S. dollar's losses against the yen and the euro Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was last at Y92.31 from Y92.27 in late New York trade, while the euro was at $1.4495 from $1.4489 and Y133.80 from Y133.71.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold was last up $3.60 from the New York close at $1,000.30 per troy ounce.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Metals were still untraded on LME Select. The three-month London Metals Exchange copper contract settled up $142 from the previous kerb at $6,465 per ton, near 2008 highs while three-month LME lead touched a new 16-month high and settled up $105 at $2,455 per ton Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil futures prices ended above $71 a barrel after posting the biggest single-day rise since Aug. 19 on a buying spree spurred by weakness in the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The October Nymex crude oil futures contract was last up 6 cents at $71.16 per barrel, after surging $3.08 or 4.5% in New York Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CALENDAR OF EVENTS Sept. 9 \par 0930 IST Mumbai:           RBI deputy governor, HSBC India chief executive, \par                            Unique ID Authority Of India chairman , \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan \par                            Chase\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief executive, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canara Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  chairman, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \par                            of Baroda\f28 \uc2 \f2  chairman and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Suzlon Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  COO at second \par                            day of FICCI global banking conference \par 1045 IST Bangalore:        \f28 \uc2 \f2 EMC Corp\f28 \uc2 